/* generated by build.py — edit cards in _build/cards_c*.py */
window.DECK = [{"id": "f0-function", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does <b>f(3)</b> mean — and what does it definitely <em>not</em> mean?", "back": "<p>“Put 3 into the machine called f.” It is <b>not</b> f multiplied by 3.</p><p>A function is a rule where the same input always gives the same output.</p><div class=\"chint\">A model <em>is</em> a function. Training does not change the function — it changes numbers inside it, which is what the subscript in f<sub>w,b</sub>(x) is telling you.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-01-what-is-a-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A vending machine for numbers. Put a coin in, get a chocolate bar out — same coin, same bar, every time.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">f</td><td class=\"sa\">“eff”</td><td>the machine's name. Could be g, h, J, σ — all just names</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">f(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“f of x”</td><td>what comes out. The brackets mean “applied to”, never “times”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">f(3)</td><td class=\"sa\">“f of three”</td><td>put 3 in. If f(x) = 2x+1 the answer is 7</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>def f(x): return 2*x + 1</code></p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-superscripts", "kind": "trap", "front": "Three superscripts: <b>x<sup>(2)</sup></b> · <b>x<sup>2</sup></b> · <b>a<sup>[2]</sup></b>. What does each mean?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>x<sup>(2)</sup></b> — round brackets: training <b>example</b> 2</li><li><b>x<sup>2</sup></b> — nothing: x <b>squared</b></li><li><b>a<sup>[2]</sup></b> — square brackets: <b>layer</b> 2</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The bracket style carries the meaning, and the courses are consistent about it. Reading a<sup>[2]</sup> as “a squared” is the most common notation error in Course 2.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-01-what-is-a-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Little raised numbers mean three different things, and the brackets around them are the only clue.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">x⁽²⁾</td><td class=\"sa\">“x, example two”</td><td>the SECOND house in your data</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x²</td><td class=\"sa\">“x squared”</td><td>x times itself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a[²]</td><td class=\"sa\">“a, layer two”</td><td>belonging to layer 2 of a network</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a shirt label: “size 10” and “10 in the pack” are both 10 and mean nothing alike.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-slope", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Slope</b> — the formula, and the two letters it goes by.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">slope = <span class='fr'><span>rise</span><span>run</span></span> = <span class='fr'><span>change in y</span><span>change in x</span></span></div><p>Written <b>m</b> in school maths, <b>w</b> in machine learning. Same thing.</p><div class=\"chint\">“If I step 1 to the right, how far up do I go?” Positive = uphill, negative = downhill, zero = flat.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-04-slope.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>How steep a line is, as one number: step one to the right, how far up do you go?</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">rise</td><td class=\"sa\">“the rise”</td><td>how much it went UP. Negative if it went down</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">run</td><td class=\"sa\">“the run”</td><td>how far you went ACROSS</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m</td><td class=\"sa\">“em”</td><td>the school-maths letter for slope</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">w</td><td class=\"sa\">“double-you”</td><td>the SAME thing, in machine learning</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">y = mx + c at school is f(x) = wx + b here. Identical; only the letters changed.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-derivative", "kind": "concept", "front": "What <b>is</b> a derivative, and what are the only three things you need to know about it?", "back": "<p>The <b>slope of a curve at one exact point</b> — found by taking two points very close together and shrinking the gap to nothing.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>it is a <b>slope</b></li><li>its <b>sign</b> says which way is uphill</li><li>its <b>size</b> says how steep</li></ul><div class=\"ceq\"><var>f</var>′(<var>x</var>) = lim<sub><var>h</var>→0</sub> <span class='fr'><span><var>f</var>(<var>x</var>+<var>h</var>) − <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>)</span><span><var>h</var></span></span></div><div class=\"chint\">You never need to differentiate by hand in this specialization. TensorFlow does it.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-05-derivatives.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Slope needs two points and a curve gives you one. So take a second point very close by, then slide it closer and closer. The number it settles on is the derivative.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">f′(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“f prime of x”</td><td>the derivative. The little dash is all it is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">df/dx</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee f by dee x”</td><td>the same thing, written differently</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">h</td><td class=\"sa\">“aitch”</td><td>the tiny gap to the second point</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">lim h→0</td><td class=\"sa\">“as h goes to zero”</td><td>“keep shrinking the gap and see what it approaches”</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>(f(x+h) - f(x)) / h</code> with h about 1e-5. Any smaller and rounding ruins it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-partial", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is a <b>partial derivative</b>, and what does the curly ∂ tell you?", "back": "<p><b>Freeze every variable except one</b>, and a hill becomes an ordinary curve whose slope you already know how to find.</p><p>The curly ∂ (rather than a straight d) is purely a notice that other variables exist and are being held still.</p><div class=\"chint\">Collect all the partials into one list and you have the <b>gradient</b>, ∇J — which points straight uphill. That is why gradient descent subtracts it.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-06-partial-derivatives.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You are on a hillside. “How steep?” is incomplete — steep in which direction? Pick one direction, walk only along that line, and you are back to an ordinary slope.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂</td><td class=\"sa\">“partial” or “curly dee”</td><td>a curly d. It only means “other variables are being held still”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂J/∂w</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee J dee w”</td><td>how much J changes if you nudge w alone</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∇J</td><td class=\"sa\">“grad J”</td><td>all the partials collected into one list — the gradient</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code, “held still” literally means you do not add h to it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-sigma", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Σ</b> — what is it, and how do you read it out loud?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>i</var>=1</sub><sup><var>m</var></sup> <var>x<sub>i</sub></var></div><p>A <b>for loop</b>. Counter underneath, stop on top, thing-to-add beside it.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>read as: “now do this for every training example, and add up the results”</li><li>in code: <code>np.sum(x)</code></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Σ(x²) squares each then adds. (Σx)² adds then squares. For [1,2] those are 5 and 9.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-07-sigma-notation.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Writing x₁ + x₂ + … + x₁₀₀₀ is silly, so maths has a shorthand. It is a for loop wearing a hat.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum of” (capital sigma)</td><td>add up what follows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">i = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">“i starts at one”</td><td>the counter and where it begins — underneath</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m</td><td class=\"sa\">“up to m”</td><td>where it stops — on top. Usually the number of examples</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">xᵢ</td><td class=\"sa\">“x sub i”</td><td>the thing being added; i changes each time round</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.sum(x)</code>, or <code>np.sum(x ** 2)</code> for Σx².</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-pi", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Π</b> — what does it mean, and why does code usually avoid it?", "back": "<p>Capital pi: <b>multiply them all together</b>. (Nothing to do with 3.14159 — that is lowercase π.)</p><div class=\"ceq\">log(<var>a</var> × <var>b</var>) = log(<var>a</var>) + log(<var>b</var>)</div><p>Multiplying many numbers below 1 underflows to exactly zero, so code computes <code>np.sum(np.log(p))</code> instead.</p><div class=\"chint\">The shrinking is the <em>point</em> in anomaly detection: five mild oddities multiply into one genuinely rare event.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-08-pi-notation.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Sigma's sibling. Same layout, one different instruction: multiply instead of add.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Π</td><td class=\"sa\">“product of” (capital pi)</td><td>multiply them all together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">π</td><td class=\"sa\">“pi” (lowercase)</td><td>the COMPLETELY different thing: 3.14159</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">underflow</td><td class=\"sa\">“UNDER-flow”</td><td>so many small numbers multiplied that the answer rounds to zero</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.prod(p)</code> — but real code writes <code>np.sum(np.log(p))</code> to stay safe.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-vector-length", "kind": "formula", "front": "What is a <b>vector</b>, and how do you find its length?", "back": "<p>A list of numbers in order. In ML, almost always <b>one row of your spreadsheet</b>.</p><div class=\"ceq\">‖<var class=\"ov vec\">x</var>‖ = √<span class='sqrt'><var>x</var><sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + <var>x</var><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> + …</span></div><p><code>np.linalg.norm(x)</code>. For [3, 4] it is √(9+16) = <b>5</b>.</p><div class=\"chint\">Double bars ‖x‖ for a vector's length; single bars |x| for one number's absolute value.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-09-vectors.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A list of numbers kept in order. Two numbers you can draw as an arrow; four hundred you cannot, and every formula works identically.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\"><span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></td><td class=\"sa\">“x vector”</td><td>the little arrow means “this is a list, not one number”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖x‖</td><td class=\"sa\">“the norm of x”</td><td>its LENGTH. Double bars</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">|x|</td><td class=\"sa\">“absolute value”</td><td>single bars — a different thing, for single numbers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">scalar</td><td class=\"sa\">“SCAY-lar”</td><td>the word for an ordinary single number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√</td><td class=\"sa\">square root</td><td>the number that, times itself, gives this one. √9 = 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.linalg.norm(x)</code>. It is just Pythagoras with more terms.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-dot", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>dot product</b> — both formulas, and what the answer means.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var class=\"ov vec\">a</var> · <var class=\"ov vec\">b</var> = <span class='sum'>Σ</span> <var>a<sub>i</sub>b<sub>i</sub></var> = ‖<var class=\"ov vec\">a</var>‖‖<var class=\"ov vec\">b</var>‖ cos <var>θ</var></div><ul class='cbul'><li>multiply the pairs, add them up → <b>one number</b></li><li>positive = pointing the same way · zero = at right angles · negative = opposed</li></ul><div class=\"chint\"><code>a @ b</code>, not <code>a * b</code>. The star keeps them separate; the @ collapses them. A neuron needs the collapse.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-10-dot-product.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A shopping list and a price list. Pair each item with its price, multiply, add it all up. One number comes out: the total bill.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">·</td><td class=\"sa\">“dot”</td><td>the dot product — includes the adding, unlike ordinary ×</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">aᵀb</td><td class=\"sa\">“a transpose b”</td><td>the same thing, written the matrix way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cos θ</td><td class=\"sa\">“cos theta”</td><td>how much two arrows point the same way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖</td><td class=\"sa\">the norm of x</td><td>the <b>length</b> of a vector. Double bars</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>a @ b</code> gives 32. <code>a * b</code> gives [4, 10, 18] — a list, not a total.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-shape-rule", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>matrix multiplication shape rule</b>, and the trick for checking it in your head.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">(<var>m</var> × <b><var>n</var></b>) × (<b><var>n</var></b> × <var>p</var>) = (<var>m</var> × <var>p</var>)</div><p>Write the shapes side by side. <b>Middles match → legal. The answer is the outer two.</b></p><div class=\"chint\">The inner number gets summed away and never appears in the result. And A@B ≠ B@A — usually one of them is not even defined.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-12-matrix-multiplication.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A test you can do in your head before writing any code: can these two tables be multiplied, and what size will the answer be?</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">(m × n)</td><td class=\"sa\">“m by n”</td><td>m rows, n columns. Rows always first</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">inner numbers</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the two in the middle. They MUST match</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">outer numbers</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the two on the ends. They become the answer's shape</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≠</td><td class=\"sa\">not equal to</td><td>these two are different — the shape rule uses it a lot</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>A @ B</code>. Write (3×2)(2×4) — middles match, answer is (3×4).</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-transpose", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does <b>transpose</b> do, and why do you keep needing it?", "back": "<p>Swaps rows and columns. (2,3) becomes (3,2). The numbers do not change — only where they sit.</p><p>You need it for one boring reason: <b>making two shapes line up</b> so a multiplication is defined.</p><div class=\"chint\"><code>M.T</code>. And it is <b>not</b> reshape — both can give (3,2) from a (2,3), and the numbers land in different places.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-13-transpose.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Tip the spreadsheet on its side. Rows become columns. No number changes value; they just move.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Mᵀ</td><td class=\"sa\">“M transpose”</td><td>the maths notation — a raised capital T</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">M.T</td><td class=\"sa\">“M dot tee”</td><td>the NumPy version. Same thing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">view</td><td class=\"sa\">“a view”</td><td>NumPy does not copy anything — it just changes how it walks the numbers</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">You reach for it when shapes refuse to match: <code>X @ W.T</code>.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-exp", "kind": "concept", "front": "Two facts about <b>e<sup>z</sup></b> that explain why it is everywhere in this specialization.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>it is <b>always positive</b>, whatever z is → which is why softmax uses it</li><li>it <b>grows very fast</b>, turning differences in scores into ratios in probability</li></ul><p>e ≈ 2.718. <code>np.exp(z)</code>. e<sup>0</sup> = 1, always.</p><div class=\"chint\">Why e and not 10? Because the derivative of e<sup>x</sup> is e<sup>x</sup> itself — which makes every derivative in the course come out clean.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-14-exponentials.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>e is a fixed number, about 2.718, like π. “e to the power z” is always positive and grows very fast — and those two facts are the whole reason it appears everywhere.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">e</td><td class=\"sa\">“ee”</td><td>≈ 2.71828. Nothing to solve</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e^z</td><td class=\"sa\">“e to the z”</td><td>also written exp(z), because superscripts are hard to type</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e^0</td><td class=\"sa\">“e to the zero”</td><td>= 1. Always</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">overflow</td><td class=\"sa\">“OH-ver-flow”</td><td>e^1000 is too big to store → the computer returns infinity</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.exp(z)</code>. And the sigmoid is just <code>1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))</code>.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-log", "kind": "concept", "front": "The two jobs <b>logarithms</b> do in machine learning.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>turn tiny into huge</b> — −log(0.001) = 6.9, which makes cross-entropy a usable loss</li><li><b>turn multiplying into adding</b> — log(a×b) = log(a) + log(b), which stops products of many probabilities underflowing to zero</li></ol><p><code>np.log</code> is base e (the ML default). <code>np.log2</code> for entropy.</p><div class=\"chint\">−log(1) = 0, so a confident correct answer costs nothing. −log(0) = ∞, so a confident wrong one costs everything. That asymmetry is the whole design.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-15-logarithms.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The undo button for exponentials. “What power did I raise e to, to get this number?”</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">log</td><td class=\"sa\">“log”</td><td>in ML always the natural log, base e, unless it says otherwise</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ln</td><td class=\"sa\">“ell-en”</td><td>the same thing. Older books and calculators use this</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log₂</td><td class=\"sa\">“log base two”</td><td>used for entropy, so answers come out in “bits”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−log(p)</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus log p”</td><td>small p → big penalty. This IS the loss function</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∞</td><td class=\"sa\">infinity</td><td>endlessly large</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.log(p)</code>. −log(1) = 0 and −log(0.01) = 4.6.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-prob-rules", "kind": "formula", "front": "The four <b>probability</b> rules, and the one that powers anomaly detection.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>always between <b>0</b> and <b>1</b></li><li>P(not A) = <b>1 − P(A)</b></li><li><b>AND</b> of independent things → <b>multiply</b></li><li><b>OR</b> of exclusive things → <b>add</b></li></ul><p>P(y=1 <b>|</b> x) — the bar means “<b>given</b>”.</p><div class=\"chint\">The multiplying rule is why five mild oddities become one rare event — the engine behind anomaly detection.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-16-probability.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A probability is between 0 (never) and 1 (certain). Three balls out of ten are red, so P(red) = 0.3.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">P(A)</td><td class=\"sa\">“P of A”</td><td>the probability that A happens</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">|</td><td class=\"sa\">“given”</td><td>the vertical bar. P(y=1 | x) = “given that we saw x”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">independent</td><td class=\"sa\">“in-de-PEN-dent”</td><td>one does not affect the other. Two coin flips are</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code, a probability estimated from data is just a mean: <code>y.mean()</code>.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-variance", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "How do you compute a <b>standard deviation</b> — and why is there a squaring step?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>find the <b>mean</b> μ</li><li>find each value's <b>deviation</b> from μ</li><li><b>square</b> them</li><li>average those squares → the <b>variance</b> σ²</li><li>square root → the <b>standard deviation</b> σ</li></ol><p>Squaring is necessary because the raw deviations <b>always add to exactly zero</b> — the ones above the mean cancel the ones below.</p><div class=\"chint\"><code>x.mean()</code>, <code>x.var()</code>, <code>x.std()</code>. The square root at the end puts the answer back into the original units.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-17-mean-variance.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two questions about any pile of numbers: where is the middle, and how spread out are they? [9,10,11] and [1,10,19] have the same middle and are nothing alike.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mu”, rhymes with few</td><td>the mean — the plain average</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>the standard deviation — the typical distance from the middle</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ²</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma squared”</td><td>the variance. Sigma is its square root</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">deviation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how far one value sits from the mean. Can be negative</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>x.mean()</code>, <code>x.var()</code>, <code>x.std()</code>.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-normal", "kind": "number", "front": "The <b>bell curve</b>: what fraction lies within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>μ ± 1σ → <b>68%</b></li><li>μ ± 2σ → <b>95%</b></li><li>μ ± 3σ → <b>99.7%</b></li><li>beyond ± 4σ → about 1 in 15,000</li></ul><p>Two numbers describe the whole curve: <b>μ</b> (where the top is) and <b>σ</b> (how wide).</p><div class=\"chint\">p(x) is a <b>density</b>, not a probability — it can exceed 1 when σ is small. Only the <em>area</em> is a probability, and it totals 1.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-18-normal-distribution.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Measure a thousand people's heights and you get a hill: lots in the middle, few at the edges. Two numbers describe it completely.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mu”</td><td>where the top of the hill sits</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>how wide the hill is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">density</td><td class=\"sa\">“DEN-sity”</td><td>the height of the curve. NOT a probability — it can exceed 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">3-sigma event</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>something in the outer 0.3%. That is where the phrase comes from</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.random.normal(mu, sigma, size)</code> draws samples from it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-argmax", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>max</b> vs <b>argmax</b> — and where you meet the difference.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>max</b>the biggest <b>value</b><br>x = [12, 31, 7] → <b>31</b></div><div><b>argmax</b>the <b>position</b> of the biggest<br>x = [12, 31, 7] → <b>1</b></div></div><p>“arg” means “the input that does it”. So argmax<sub>a</sub> Q(s,a) is <b>the action</b>, not the score.</p><div class=\"chint\">Classification uses <code>np.argmax(probs, axis=1)</code>; K-means uses argmin over distances; RL policies are argmax over actions.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-19-min-max-argmax.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Scores: 12, 31, 7. “max” asks what the biggest score is — 31. “argmax” asks where it is — position 1. One gives the value, the other the place.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">max</td><td class=\"sa\">“max”</td><td>the biggest VALUE</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">argmax</td><td class=\"sa\">“arg max”</td><td>the POSITION of the biggest</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">arg</td><td class=\"sa\">“argument”</td><td>in maths, “the input”. So argmax = “the input that maximises it”</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In code: <code>np.argmax(probs, axis=1)</code> — which class won, for each row.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-drill-dotprod", "kind": "drill", "front": "Compute the dot product: <b>a</b> = [1, 2, 3], <b>b</b> = [4, 5, 6].", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>a</var>·<var>b</var> = 1(4) + 2(5) + 3(6) = 4 + 10 + 18 = <b>32</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Pair up, multiply, add. Same recipe for a vector of 3 numbers or 3 million.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w1-10-dot-product.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "The Maths You Actually Need", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two lists of the same length. Pair up the numbers in the same position, multiply each pair, then add all the answers together. One number comes out the other end.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">a·b</td><td class=\"sa\">“a dot b”</td><td>the dot product — pair, multiply, add</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">4, 10, 18</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the three pairwise products, before adding</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a receipt: 4 apples at £1 each, plus 5 pears at £2 each — you add the LINE TOTALS, not the quantities or the prices alone.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-list-vs-array", "kind": "trap", "front": "<code>x * 2</code> — what happens if x is a <b>list</b>, and if x is a <b>NumPy array</b>?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>list</b><code>[1,2,3,1,2,3]</code><br>repeats the list</div><div><b>array</b><code>[2,4,6]</code><br>doubles each number</div></div><p>No error either way. <b>Always <code>np.array(my_list)</code> before doing maths.</b></p><div class=\"chint\">The difference is memory: a list holds pointers to objects of any type; an array holds raw numbers of one type in a solid block. That layout is where the speed comes from.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-03-lists-vs-arrays.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Both use square brackets. A list is a general container (so + means “join”); an array is a maths object (so + means “add the numbers”). Python warns you about neither.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">list</td><td class=\"sa\">“a Python list”</td><td>[1,2,3]. Can hold anything. * repeats it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">array</td><td class=\"sa\">“a NumPy array”</td><td>np.array([1,2,3]). All one type. * doubles each</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.array(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the conversion. The fix for the whole category of confusion</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The difference is memory: a list holds pointers to scattered objects; an array holds raw numbers in one solid block. That layout is where the speed comes from.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-slicing", "kind": "formula", "front": "<code>x[1:4]</code> — which elements, and what is the rule everyone gets wrong?", "back": "<p>Positions <b>1, 2, 3</b>. It <b>stops before 4</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li><code>x[0]</code> first · <code>x[-1]</code> last</li><li><code>x[:3]</code> from the start · <code>x[3:]</code> to the end</li><li><code>M[:, 2]</code> — all rows, column 2</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Handy consequence: the number you get is just (end − start). And a slice is a <b>view</b>, not a copy — changing it changes the original.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-04-indexing-slicing.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Square brackets ask for an address. The first thing lives at 0, not 1 — that feels wrong for about a week and then becomes invisible.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">x[0]</td><td class=\"sa\">“x sub zero”</td><td>the FIRST one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x[-1]</td><td class=\"sa\">“x minus one”</td><td>the LAST one. Minus counts back from the end</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x[1:4]</td><td class=\"sa\">“one to four”</td><td>positions 1, 2, 3. STOPS BEFORE 4</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">:</td><td class=\"sa\">“colon”</td><td>alone it means “everything in this direction”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">M[:, 2]</td><td class=\"sa\">“all rows, column two”</td><td>the 2-D version. Used constantly in Course 2</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">In Course 2 you will write <code>W[:, j]</code> to grab neuron j's weights — a dense layer stores one neuron per column.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-axis", "kind": "concept", "front": "<code>axis=0</code> vs <code>axis=1</code> — and the rule that makes it permanent.", "back": "<p><b>The axis you name is the one that disappears.</b></p><ul class='cbul'><li>(3,4) with <code>axis=0</code> → <b>(4,)</b> — one answer per <b>column</b></li><li>(3,4) with <code>axis=1</code> → <b>(3,)</b> — one answer per <b>row</b></li></ul><p>One statistic per <b>feature</b> → axis=0. One prediction per <b>example</b> → axis=1.</p><div class=\"chint\">Do not guess it — work out which number in the shape you want gone. That is your axis.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-05-shape-and-axis.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Which direction to work in. Axis 0 goes down the rows; axis 1 goes across the columns.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">axis=0</td><td class=\"sa\">“axis zero”</td><td>collapse DOWN → one answer per COLUMN</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">axis=1</td><td class=\"sa\">“axis one”</td><td>collapse ACROSS → one answer per ROW</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">no axis</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>collapse everything to a single number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">shape</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>(rows, columns). The named axis vanishes from it</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Say the sentence out loud: “one number per feature” → axis=0. “One per example” → axis=1.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-broadcast", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>broadcasting</b> rule, and the silent bug it causes.", "back": "<p>Line the shapes up <b>from the right</b>. Compatible if <b>equal</b>, or if <b>one is 1</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>(1000,4) + (4,) → (1000,4) ✓ — how a bias row is added to every example</li><li>(3,1) + (1,3) → <b>(3,3)</b> ⚠ — both stretch. Nine numbers where you wanted three, and <b>no error</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">When a result has a surprising shape, broadcasting is almost always what happened. Print the shapes of both operands.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-08-broadcasting.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You have a table with 1000 rows and one row of numbers to add to all of them. The shapes do not match and NumPy does it anyway, by stretching the smaller one.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">broadcasting</td><td class=\"sa\">“BROAD-casting”</td><td>quietly stretching a smaller array to fit a bigger one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">from the right</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how the shapes are lined up. Not from the left</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">one of them is 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the condition for stretching to be allowed</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Nothing is really copied in memory, which is why it is free. In code this is why <code>np.matmul(A, W) + b</code> works with b as a single row.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-star-vs-at", "kind": "trap", "front": "<code>a * b</code> vs <code>a @ b</code> — what is the difference, and why is it dangerous?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>*</b>elementwise<br>[1,2,3]*[4,5,6] = <b>[4,10,18]</b><br>same length out</div><div><b>@</b>dot / matrix multiply<br>[1,2,3]@[4,5,6] = <b>32</b><br>collapses to one number</div></div><p>On two square matrices <b>both run</b> and give the same shape. Only one is right, and nothing warns you.</p><div class=\"chint\">Prefer <code>@</code> over <code>np.dot</code> — np.dot behaves differently once you go past 2 dimensions.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-09-dot-in-code.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two things both called “multiply”. One keeps the numbers separate; the other adds them all up at the end.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">*</td><td class=\"sa\">“star”</td><td>elementwise. Pairs them up, keeps them separate → a LIST</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">@</td><td class=\"sa\">“at”</td><td>dot / matrix multiply. Pairs, multiplies AND adds → collapses</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.dot</td><td class=\"sa\">“numpy dot”</td><td>same as @ for vectors; behaves oddly past 2-D. Prefer @</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Dangerous because on two square matrices both run, both give the same shape, and only one is correct.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-mask", "kind": "code", "front": "What does <code>(preds == y).mean()</code> compute, and why does it work?", "back": "<p><b>Accuracy.</b></p><ol class='csteps'><li>the comparison gives an array of True/False — a <b>mask</b></li><li>True counts as 1 and False as 0</li><li>so the mean of that array is the <b>fraction that are True</b></li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Use <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code> (not <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>) to combine masks, and give each condition its own brackets.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-11-boolean-masks.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Ask a question of a whole array — “which are bigger than 25?” — and get back a whole array of yes/no answers. Put that in the brackets and you keep only the yesses.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">mask</td><td class=\"sa\">“a mask”</td><td>an array of True/False, same length as your data</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">True = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>which is why .sum() counts and .mean() gives a fraction</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">&</td><td class=\"sa\">“and”</td><td>combine masks. NOT the word 'and' — that fails on arrays</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">|</td><td class=\"sa\">“or”</td><td>the other combiner. Each condition needs its own brackets</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\"><code>(preds == y).mean()</code> is accuracy in one line.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-reshape", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<code>reshape(3,2)</code> vs <code>.T</code> on a (2,3) array — same shape, same result?", "back": "<p><b>Same shape, different numbers.</b> From [[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]:</p><div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>reshape(3,2)</b>[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]]<br>re-cuts the same sequence</div><div><b>.T</b>[[1,4],[2,5],[3,6]]<br>mirrors the positions</div></div><div class=\"chint\"><code>reshape(1,-1)</code> is the fix when a library insists on 2-D. And <code>.T</code> does nothing at all to a 1-D array.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-12-reshape.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Twelve numbers can be read as 3 rows of 4, or 4 rows of 3, or one long row. The numbers never move; only how you read them changes.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">reshape(3,4)</td><td class=\"sa\">“reshape three by four”</td><td>re-cut into that grid. Must total the same</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">-1</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus one”</td><td>“work this dimension out for me”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">flatten()</td><td class=\"sa\">“flatten”</td><td>squash everything back to 1-D</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">.T</td><td class=\"sa\">“transpose”</td><td>a DIFFERENT thing — mirrors positions rather than re-cutting</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\"><code>reshape(1, -1)</code> is the fix when Keras complains that it wants 2-D.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-pandas-five", "kind": "code", "front": "The <b>five pandas calls</b> to run on every new dataset, before modelling anything.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><code>df.head()</code> — look at it</li><li><code>df.shape</code> — how much is there</li><li><code>df.info()</code> — types, and <b>missing values</b></li><li><code>df.describe()</code> — the scale of each feature</li><li><code>df.columns</code> — the exact spelling of the names</li></ul><div class=\"chint\"><code>info()</code> catches the classic bug: a numeric column that arrived as text because of one stray “N/A”.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-13-pandas-dataframes.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>pandas knows about column NAMES; NumPy knows about positions. pandas is for loading and tidying; NumPy is for the maths.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">DataFrame</td><td class=\"sa\">“data frame”</td><td>a spreadsheet: named columns, an indexed set of rows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Series</td><td class=\"sa\">“series”</td><td>one column on its own, with its index attached</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">df.info()</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>types and missing values. The one that catches the classic bug</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">dtype object</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>means TEXT. A numeric column that arrived as strings</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Run head(), info() and describe() on every dataset before modelling anything.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-to-numpy", "kind": "code", "front": "How do you get <b>X</b> and <b>y</b> out of a DataFrame — and what is the shape trap?", "back": "<pre><code>X = df[['size','beds']].to_numpy()   # (m, 2)\ny = df['price'].to_numpy()          # (m,)</code></pre><p>Select the columns <b>before</b> converting — afterwards the names are gone.</p><div class=\"chint\"><code>df['price']</code> gives (m,) but <code>df[['price']]</code> gives (m,1). Libraries want y as (m,). If you end up with (m,1), use <code>y.ravel()</code>.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-14-pandas-to-numpy.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The one line that crosses from “loading data” to “doing maths”. You lose the column names, so pick your columns before crossing.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">.to_numpy()</td><td class=\"sa\">“to numpy”</td><td>the modern spelling</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">.values</td><td class=\"sa\">“values”</td><td>the older spelling you will see in old notebooks. Same thing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">X</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital X”</td><td>conventionally your features — 2-D, shape (m, n)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">y</td><td class=\"sa\">“lowercase y”</td><td>conventionally your target — 1-D, shape (m,)</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Single brackets give (m,); double brackets give (m,1). Libraries want y as (m,).</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-traceback", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "How do you read a Python <b>traceback</b>?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>read the <b>last line</b> first — it names the error and describes it in plain English</li><li>then the frame just above it — that is where it broke</li><li>work upwards only if you still need to know how you got there</li></ol><p>Two habits fix most of them: <b>print the shapes</b>, and <b>print the types</b>.</p><div class=\"chint\">The middle lines are just the chain of calls. Almost none of a traceback matters.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-15-reading-errors.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A wall of red text where almost nothing matters. The last line is a plain English sentence naming the actual problem.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">traceback</td><td class=\"sa\">“TRACE-back”</td><td>the chain of function calls that led to the failure</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the last line</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the error type and what went wrong. Read this FIRST</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the middle lines</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>just how the code got there. Ignorable at first</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bisecting</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>splitting a long line in half and printing, to find where it breaks</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Two habits fix most errors: print the shapes, and print the types.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-five-errors", "kind": "trap", "front": "The five errors you will actually hit — and the fix for each.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>ValueError: shapes not aligned</b> → print both shapes; transpose one</li><li><b>IndexError: out of bounds</b> → counting starts at 0; last is <code>x[-1]</code></li><li><b>TypeError: can only concatenate list</b> → <code>np.array(my_list)</code></li><li><b>NameError: not defined</b> → typo, or you never ran the import cell</li><li><b>KeyError: 'Price'</b> → <code>print(df.columns)</code>; it is probably lowercase</li></ul>", "lesson": "f0/w2-15-reading-errors.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The same handful of errors will account for nearly everything that goes wrong in the labs. Recognising them on sight saves hours.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">ValueError</td><td class=\"sa\">“value error”</td><td>usually shapes that do not line up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">IndexError</td><td class=\"sa\">“index error”</td><td>you asked for a position that does not exist</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">TypeError</td><td class=\"sa\">“type error”</td><td>an operation that does not make sense for that kind of thing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">NameError</td><td class=\"sa\">“name error”</td><td>a typo, or an import cell you never ran</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">KeyError</td><td class=\"sa\">“key error”</td><td>a column name that is not in the DataFrame</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Each one names the problem precisely. Read it before searching the internet.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-function-read", "kind": "concept", "front": "You meet a function you have never seen. What <b>three things</b> tell you almost everything?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>its <b>name</b> — good code names things honestly</li><li>its <b>parameters</b> — what it needs, matched <b>in order</b> unless you name them</li><li>what it <b>returns</b> — one value, or a tuple of several</li></ol><p>That is enough to <em>use</em> it without reading the body — which is exactly what every graded exercise asks of you.</p><div class=\"chint\">No <code>return</code> means it hands back <code>None</code>, which fails confusingly somewhere downstream rather than where the problem is.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w2-16-functions.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Python, NumPy and pandas", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A recipe with a name. You give it ingredients, it does some steps, and it hands you back a result.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">def</td><td class=\"sa\">“def”</td><td>“I am defining a function”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">parameters</td><td class=\"sa\">“pa-RAM-eters”</td><td>what it needs, in the brackets. Matched IN ORDER</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">:</td><td class=\"sa\">“colon”</td><td>starts the body. The indent below is what belongs to it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">return</td><td class=\"sa\">“return”</td><td>hands one value back. Without it you get None</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">indentation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>not decoration — it is what defines the body in Python</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Every graded exercise is “fill in the body of this function”, so reading a signature is the core skill.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-eigen", "kind": "formula", "front": "What does <b>Av = λv</b> say, and what makes those directions special?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>A</var><b>v</b> = <var>λ</var><b>v</b></div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>v</b> is an eigenvector — a direction the matrix does not <b>rotate</b></li><li><var>λ</var> is the eigenvalue — how much that direction is stretched</li><li>for these directions only, a whole matrix collapses to one number</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">A = [[2,1],[1,2]]: A[1,1] = [3,3] so λ = 3; A[1,−1] = [1,−1] so λ = 1.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-01-eigenvectors.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Multiplying by a matrix usually turns a vector AND stretches it. A few special directions do not get turned at all — they only get longer or shorter. Those are the eigenvectors.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">A</td><td class=\"sa\">“the matrix”</td><td>the transformation being applied</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">v</td><td class=\"sa\">“an eigenvector”</td><td>a direction the matrix leaves pointing the same way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ</td><td class=\"sa\">“lambda”</td><td>the eigenvalue — how much that direction gets stretched</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like stretching a rubber sheet: most arrows drawn on it swing round, but the ones along the pull do not.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-pca-why", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why are the principal components the <b>eigenvectors of the covariance matrix</b>?", "back": "<p>Because each eigenvalue <b>is</b> the variance along its own eigenvector.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>“find the direction of greatest variance” and “find the largest eigenvector of the covariance matrix” are therefore the same instruction</li><li>covariance matrices are symmetric, so their eigenvalues are real and their eigenvectors perpendicular — which is why PCA is well behaved</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Eigenvalues 4.976 and 0.064 → the first component keeps 98.7% of the variance.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-01-eigenvectors.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>PCA wants the direction the data spreads out most along. That is exactly what the biggest eigenvector of the covariance matrix is — the eigenvalue IS the spread along it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">covariance matrix</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a small grid describing how the data is spread out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">principal component</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one of those special directions, biggest first</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">98.7%</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>4.976 ÷ (4.976 + 0.064) — how much spread the first one keeps</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">This is the sentence Course 3 asked you to accept without explanation.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-svd", "kind": "formula", "front": "State the SVD, and say why it is more general than eigendecomposition.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>A</var> = <var>U</var> <var>Σ</var> <var>V</var><sup>T</sup></div><ul class='cbul'><li>rotate (V<sup>T</sup>), stretch (Σ), rotate (U)</li><li>works for <b>any</b> matrix — eigendecomposition needs a square one</li><li>Σ is sorted largest first, so truncating to the top k gives the <b>provably best</b> rank-k approximation</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Singular values squared and divided by n give exactly the covariance eigenvalues. Real PCA uses SVD because forming the covariance matrix loses precision.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-02-svd.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Any grid of numbers at all can be pulled apart into three simple steps: turn, stretch, turn. The stretches come out sorted biggest first, so you can drop the small ones to compress it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">U, V</td><td class=\"sa\">“U and V”</td><td>the two turning steps</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>the stretching amounts, always sorted largest first</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">rank-k</td><td class=\"sa\">“rank k”</td><td>keeping only the k biggest stretches</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A stack of transparencies ordered by importance: keep the first few, discard the rest.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-mle", "kind": "concept", "front": "State the maximum likelihood principle, and say what it derives.", "back": "<p>Choose the parameters that make the <b>data you actually observed</b> as probable as possible.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>assume Gaussian noise → you derive <b>squared error</b></li><li>assume a Bernoulli outcome → you derive <b>cross-entropy</b></li><li>take the negative log: products become sums, and nothing underflows</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">10 flips, 7 heads: L(p) = p⁷(1−p)³ peaks at p = 0.7. The loss function stops being a convention and becomes a consequence of what you assumed.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-03-maximum-likelihood.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Instead of asking “how wrong is my model”, ask “if my model were right, how likely was the data I actually saw?” Then pick whatever makes the data most likely.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">likelihood</td><td class=\"sa\">“likelihood”</td><td>how probable the observed data is, for a given guess</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−log</td><td class=\"sa\">“negative log”</td><td>makes multiplying into adding, and stops tiny numbers vanishing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">MLE</td><td class=\"sa\">“M-L-E”</td><td>maximum likelihood estimate — the guess at the peak</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A detective asking which suspect makes the evidence least surprising, rather than who looks guilty.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-jacobian", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "What is a Jacobian, and how does it relate to the gradient you already use?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>J<sub>ij</sub></var> = ∂<var>f<sub>i</sub></var>/∂<var>x<sub>j</sub></var></div><ul class='cbul'><li>rows are <b>outputs</b>, columns are <b>inputs</b></li><li>ℝⁿ → ℝᵐ gives an m × n grid</li><li>the <b>gradient is a Jacobian with one row</b>, because a cost has one output</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The chain rule becomes matrix multiplication. Backprop evaluates that product right to left, which keeps a row vector at every step instead of building huge matrices.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-04-jacobian.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>With one input and one output a derivative is one number. With several of each you need one number per pair — arranged in a grid, with outputs down the side and inputs across the top.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">J</td><td class=\"sa\">“the Jacobian”</td><td>the grid of all the partial derivatives</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂fᵢ/∂xⱼ</td><td class=\"sa\">“partial f-i by partial x-j”</td><td>how much output i moves when input j is nudged</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">(m, n)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>m outputs, n inputs — the shape tells you the function's signature</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A mixing desk: three sliders, two speakers, six answers, laid out as a table.</p></div>"}, {"id": "f0-softmax-grad", "kind": "formula", "front": "What is ∂L/∂z for softmax with cross-entropy — and why is it so simple?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">∂<var>L</var>/∂<var>z</var> = <b>p − y</b></div><p>Softmax's derivative carries a factor of p<sub>y</sub>; the log in cross-entropy contributes 1/p<sub>y</sub>. They cancel exactly.</p><div class=\"chint\">z = [2, 1, 0.5] with true class 1 → p = [0.6285, 0.2312, 0.1402], so the gradient is [−0.3715, 0.2312, 0.1402]. Same cancellation as sigmoid + log loss in C1 W3.</div>", "lesson": "f0/w3-05-softmax-gradient.html", "extra": "", "course": "F0", "week": "f0w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Maths Behind the Curtain", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Softmax has an awkward derivative and the log has another. Put them together and both messes cancel exactly, leaving “what you predicted minus what was true”. One subtraction.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">p</td><td class=\"sa\">“p”</td><td>the predicted probabilities, from softmax</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">y</td><td class=\"sa\">“y”</td><td>the true answer, as a 1 in the right slot and 0s elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p − y</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the whole gradient. Negative for the true class, positive for the rest</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂</td><td class=\"sa\">partial / curly dee</td><td>rate of change of, with the other variables held still</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Two awkward gear ratios that happen to be reciprocals — in series they turn cleanly 1:1.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-model", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>linear regression model</b> — write it out.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>f</var><sub><var>w</var>,<var>b</var></sub>(<var>x</var>) = <var>wx</var> + <var>b</var></div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>w</b> — the slope (weight): how much y rises per unit of x</li><li><b>b</b> — the intercept (bias): the prediction when x = 0</li><li>both are <b>learned</b>; x is your data</li></ul>", "lesson": "c1/w1-04-linear-regression-model.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A straight line drawn through your dots. To describe a line you only ever need two numbers: how steep it is, and how high it starts. That is the whole model.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">f(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“f of x”</td><td>the machine: put a number in, get a guess out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">w</td><td class=\"sa\">“double-you”</td><td>steepness. Bigger w = the line climbs faster</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x</td><td class=\"sa\">“ex”</td><td>what you measured — the house size</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">b</td><td class=\"sa\">“bee”</td><td>where the line starts when x is zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">=</td><td class=\"sa\">“is”</td><td>just says the left side and right side are the same</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like working out a taxi fare: £2 to get in (that is b), plus £3 per mile (that is w).</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-notation", "kind": "concept", "front": "What do these three superscripts mean?<br><div class=\"ceq\"><var>x</var><sup>(2)</sup> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <var>a</var><sup>[2]</sup></div>", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>x<sup>(2)</sup></b> — round brackets: training <b>example</b> number 2</li><li><b>x<sup>2</sup></b> — plain: x <b>squared</b></li><li><b>a<sup>[2]</sup></b> — square brackets: <b>layer</b> 2 (Course 2)</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The bracket style carries the meaning. The course is consistent about it.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-04-linear-regression-model.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Little numbers floating next to a letter can mean three totally different things, and the <em>brackets</em> tell you which. Getting this wrong makes formulas look impossible.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">x⁽²⁾</td><td class=\"sa\">“x, example two”</td><td>the SECOND house in your list</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x²</td><td class=\"sa\">“x squared”</td><td>x times itself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a[²]</td><td class=\"sa\">“a, layer two”</td><td>something belonging to layer 2 of a network</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">( )</td><td class=\"sa\">round brackets</td><td>→ which example</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">[ ]</td><td class=\"sa\">square brackets</td><td>→ which layer</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Same as a shirt label: “size 10” and “10 in the pack” are both 10 and mean nothing alike.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-cost", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>squared error cost function</b> — write it out.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>J</var>(<var>w</var>,<var>b</var>) = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>2<var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>i</var>=1</sub><sup><var>m</var></sup> ( <var>f</var>(<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup>) − <var>y</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> )<sup>2</sup></div><ul class='cbul'><li>square → makes errors positive, and punishes big misses disproportionately</li><li>1/m → the average, so J does not grow just because you collected more data</li><li>the 2 → pure convenience; it cancels when you differentiate</li></ul>", "lesson": "c1/w1-05-cost-function-formula.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One score for how badly your line fits. For every dot, measure how far off you were, square that number, add them all up, then divide to get an average. Low score = good line.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">J</td><td class=\"sa\">“jay”</td><td>the badness score. Small is good</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum of”</td><td>add up the following for every dot</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">i = 1 to m</td><td class=\"sa\">“for each example”</td><td>go through dot 1, dot 2, … dot m</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m</td><td class=\"sa\">“em”</td><td>how many dots (training examples) you have</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">( f − y )</td><td class=\"sa\">“guess minus truth”</td><td>how far off you were on that dot</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">( … )²</td><td class=\"sa\">“squared”</td><td>multiply it by itself, so misses can't cancel out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">1/2m</td><td class=\"sa\">“divided by two-m”</td><td>take the average; the 2 is just for tidiness later</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a golf score: every stroke you waste adds up, and lower is better.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-cost-shape", "kind": "concept", "front": "What <b>shape</b> is J(w) for linear regression, and why does the shape matter?", "back": "<p>A <b>parabola</b> — and with two parameters, a <b>bowl</b> (convex).</p><ul class='cbul'><li>it is a sum of squared linear terms, so it is a quadratic</li><li>a convex surface has <b>exactly one</b> minimum</li><li>so gradient descent always finds the global optimum, from any starting point</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">You lose this guarantee entirely once you build a neural network.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-06-cost-function-intuition.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>If you draw the badness score for every possible line, you get a smooth bowl shape — like the inside of a salad bowl. A bowl has exactly one lowest point, so you can never get lost.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">convex</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-vex”</td><td>bowl-shaped: one bottom, no side dips</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">parabola</td><td class=\"sa\">“pa-RAB-o-la”</td><td>the U-shape you get from squaring things</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">minimum</td><td class=\"sa\">“minimum”</td><td>the lowest point — the best line</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Drop a marble anywhere in a salad bowl and it always rolls to the same spot. That is what “convex” buys you.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-contour", "kind": "concept", "front": "On a <b>contour plot</b> of J(w, b), what does a single ring represent?", "back": "<p>A set of (w, b) pairs that all produce <b>different lines that fit equally badly</b> — same J, different model.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>rings close together → steep, J changes fast</li><li>rings far apart → flat</li><li>the bullseye → the best possible w and b</li><li><b>elongated</b> rings → an awkward valley, cured by feature scaling</li></ul>", "lesson": "c1/w1-07-visualizing-the-cost-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The bowl seen from directly above, like a map of a hill. Each ring joins up all the points at the same height — so every line on one ring is equally bad.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">contour</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-tour”</td><td>a ring joining equal heights, like on a walking map</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">w axis</td><td class=\"sa\">“the w direction”</td><td>how steep the line is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">b axis</td><td class=\"sa\">“the b direction”</td><td>how high the line starts</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bullseye</td><td class=\"sa\">“the centre”</td><td>the best possible line</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Rings squeezed close together = a steep cliff. Rings spread wide = flat ground.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-gd-update", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>gradient descent update rule</b> — and the one implementation detail that is easy to get wrong.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>w</var> := <var>w</var> − <var>α</var> ∂<var>J</var>/∂<var>w</var> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <var>b</var> := <var>b</var> − <var>α</var> ∂<var>J</var>/∂<var>b</var></div><p><b>Simultaneous update.</b> Compute <em>both</em> derivatives from the old values first, <em>then</em> assign both.</p><div class=\"chint\">The sequential version still often reduces J — which is exactly why the bug is hard to spot.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-09-implementing-gradient-descent.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Take one small step downhill, over and over. The formula says: look at which way is uphill, then move a little bit the OTHER way.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">:=</td><td class=\"sa\">“becomes”</td><td>not “equals” — it means “replace the old value with this”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">“alpha”</td><td>step size. How big a stride you take</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂J/∂w</td><td class=\"sa\">“the slope in the w direction”</td><td>which way is uphill, and how steep</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus”</td><td>the whole trick: go the OPPOSITE way to uphill</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Simultaneous update means: both feet decide where to step BEFORE either one moves. Otherwise you trip.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-gd-sign", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why does <b>subtracting</b> the derivative always move you towards the minimum?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>positive slope → uphill is to the right → subtracting moves w <b>left</b> ✓</li><li>negative slope → uphill is to the left → subtracting a negative moves w <b>right</b> ✓</li><li>at the minimum the slope is 0, so nothing moves — it stops on its own</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">And the steps shrink automatically near the bottom, because the slope shrinks. You never need to reduce α by hand.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-10-gradient-descent-intuition.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The slope has a sign — plus or minus — and that sign alone tells you which way to walk. You never need to know where the bottom is.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">positive slope</td><td class=\"sa\">“going up to the right”</td><td>so step LEFT to go down</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">negative slope</td><td class=\"sa\">“going down to the right”</td><td>so step RIGHT to go down</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">slope = 0</td><td class=\"sa\">“flat”</td><td>you are at the bottom; stop</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Standing on a hill in fog: you can't see the valley, but you can always feel which way your feet tilt.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-alpha", "kind": "concept", "front": "What are the <b>four regimes</b> of the learning rate α?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>far too small</b> — converges, glacially</li><li><b>about right</b> — falls fast, then flattens</li><li><b>too large</b> — overshoots, J oscillates</li><li><b>far too large</b> — diverges; J grows, then <code>NaN</code></li></ul><div class=\"chint\"><b>If J ever increases between iterations, α is too large.</b> The single most useful debugging rule in the specialization.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-11-learning-rate.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>α is your stride length. Too tiny and you shuffle forever. Too huge and you leap right over the valley and land higher up the other side — then leap even further next time.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">“alpha”</td><td>how far you move each step</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">diverge</td><td class=\"sa\">“di-VERGE”</td><td>fly off to infinity instead of settling down</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">NaN</td><td class=\"sa\">“nan”</td><td>“Not a Number” — the computer's way of saying the maths broke</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">oscillate</td><td class=\"sa\">“OSS-il-ate”</td><td>bounce back and forth without settling</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Stepping stones across a river: too small and you never cross, too big and you land in the water.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-alpha-debug", "kind": "trap", "front": "Your model will not learn. How do you tell whether it is <b>α</b> or a <b>bug</b>?", "back": "<p>Set α absurdly small — <code>0.0001</code>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>J now decreases → the gradient is fine, α was too large</li><li>J still does not decrease → it is <b>not</b> α. There is a bug, usually a sign error or a wrong index in the gradient</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">With a small enough step, J is mathematically <em>guaranteed</em> to fall — if the gradient is correct.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-11-learning-rate.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A trick to tell two different problems apart. Make the steps ridiculously tiny. If the score still won't come down, the problem isn't your step size — something in your code is wrong.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">0.0001</td><td class=\"sa\">“a really tiny step”</td><td>so small it CANNOT overshoot</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">gradient</td><td class=\"sa\">“GRAY-dee-ent”</td><td>the slope your code calculated</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sign error</td><td class=\"sa\">“a plus where a minus should be”</td><td>the most common bug of all</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like testing whether a car won't start because of the fuel or the battery — change one thing only.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-derivatives", "kind": "formula", "front": "The two <b>derivatives</b> for linear regression.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">∂<var>J</var>/∂<var>w</var> = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span><var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span> ( <var>f</var>(<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup>) − <var>y</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> ) <b>· <var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup></b></div><div class=\"ceq\">∂<var>J</var>/∂<var>b</var> = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span><var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span> ( <var>f</var>(<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup>) − <var>y</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> )</div><p>The only difference is the <b>· x<sup>(i)</sup></b> on the w version — because an example with a large x is more sensitive to a change in w.</p><div class=\"chint\">This is where the 2 in 1/2m goes: differentiating the square brings down a 2 that cancels it.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-12-gradient-descent-for-linear-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>These two formulas answer: “if I nudge w a tiny bit, how much does the badness change?” and the same for b. They are what tells gradient descent which way to walk.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂</td><td class=\"sa\">“partial dee”</td><td>a fancy d. It means “rate of change of”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂J/∂w</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee J by dee w”</td><td>how much the score changes when w changes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">· x<sup>(i)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“times x”</td><td>big houses push harder on w than small ones do</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">(no x on b)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>b shifts every guess equally, so every dot gets an equal vote</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Think of w and b as two taps. These formulas say how much the water level moves per turn of each tap.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-batch", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does <b>“batch”</b> gradient descent mean, and what are the alternatives?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>batch</b> — every update uses all m examples (what Course 1 uses)</li><li><b>stochastic</b> — one example per update; noisy, many more steps per second</li><li><b>mini-batch</b> — a subset (32–512); the practical compromise, used by all deep learning</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The name “batch” is why the compromise is called <em>mini</em>-batch.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-13-running-gradient-descent.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>How many examples you look at before taking one step. Look at all of them, one of them, or a handful.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">batch</td><td class=\"sa\">“batch”</td><td>use ALL your examples for every single step</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">stochastic</td><td class=\"sa\">“sto-KAS-tik”</td><td>just a scary word for “random” — use one example at a time</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">mini-batch</td><td class=\"sa\">“mini-batch”</td><td>a small handful, usually 32 to 512. What everyone actually uses</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Batch = read the whole book before deciding. Mini-batch = read a chapter. Stochastic = read one sentence.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-three-parts", "kind": "concept", "front": "What are the <b>three parts</b> that every algorithm in this specialization is built from?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>a <b>model</b> — what f(x) is allowed to look like</li><li>a <b>cost function</b> — one number saying how wrong it is</li><li>an <b>optimiser</b> — gradient descent, which makes that number smaller</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Course 2 makes the model a neural network. Course 3 changes the cost. The structure never changes.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-13-running-gradient-descent.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Every single algorithm in all three courses is built from the same three pieces. Once you spot them, nothing later is really new.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">model</td><td class=\"sa\">“the guesser”</td><td>the shape of the guess it is allowed to make</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cost function</td><td class=\"sa\">“the scorer”</td><td>one number saying how wrong the guesses are</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">optimiser</td><td class=\"sa\">“the fixer”</td><td>the thing that makes that number smaller</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like darts: how you throw (model), how far off you landed (cost), and adjusting your aim (optimiser).</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w1-drill-cost", "kind": "drill", "front": "x = [1, 2], y = [300, 500], w = 100, b = 100. Compute J(w, b).", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">f(1) = 200, f(2) = 300 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; errors −100, −200</div><div class=\"ceq\">J = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>2(2)</span></span> ( 100² + 200² ) = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> (50,000) = <b>12,500</b></div><div class=\"chint\">The perfect fit (w=200, b=100) gives J=0 exactly — this is what “almost right” costs.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w1-05-cost-function-formula.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Introduction to Machine Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Plug two guesses (w=100, b=100) into the line, see how far off each prediction was, square those misses so they cannot cancel out, and average them. That single number is J.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">f(1), f(2)</td><td class=\"sa\">“f of one”, “f of two”</td><td>what the line predicts for each house</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">J</td><td class=\"sa\">“jay”</td><td>the badness score this guess earns</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Compare it to the PERFECT guess (w=200, b=100), which scores exactly 0 — 12,500 tells you this guess is a long way from perfect, not that it is hopeless.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-multi-model", "kind": "formula", "front": "The model with <b>n features</b>, in both forms.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>w</var><sub>1</sub><var>x</var><sub>1</sub> + <var>w</var><sub>2</sub><var>x</var><sub>2</sub> + … + <var>w<sub>n</sub>x<sub>n</sub></var> + <var>b</var></div><div class=\"ceq\"><span class=\"lbl\">vectorised</span><var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <b><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span> · <span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></b> + <var>b</var></div><div class=\"chint\">n = number of features. m = number of examples. Keep them straight.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-01-multiple-features.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Before, one thing decided the price. Now several do — size, bedrooms, floors, age. Each gets its own dial saying how much it matters.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">w₁, w₂, w₃</td><td class=\"sa\">“w one, w two…”</td><td>one dial per thing you measured</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x₁, x₂, x₃</td><td class=\"sa\">“x one, x two…”</td><td>the actual measurements</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\"><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span></td><td class=\"sa\">“w vector”</td><td>the little arrow means “this is a whole list of numbers, not one”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">·</td><td class=\"sa\">“dot”</td><td>multiply each pair and add them all up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">n</td><td class=\"sa\">“en”</td><td>how many features (columns) you have</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m</td><td class=\"sa\">“em”</td><td>how many examples (rows) you have</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A shopping bill: quantity of each item × its price, all added together.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-subscripts", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is <b>x<sub>2</sub><sup>(3)</sup></b>?", "back": "<p>Feature <b>2</b> of training example <b>3</b> — one single number.</p><ul class='cbul'><li><b>subscript</b> = which feature (which column)</li><li><b>superscript in round brackets</b> = which example (which row)</li></ul>", "lesson": "c1/w2-01-multiple-features.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two little numbers pin down one single value in a table: which column, and which row.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">subscript ₂</td><td class=\"sa\">“sub two”</td><td>which FEATURE — which column</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">superscript ⁽³⁾</td><td class=\"sa\">“example three”</td><td>which EXAMPLE — which row</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x₂⁽³⁾</td><td class=\"sa\">“x two, example three”</td><td>the bedrooms of the third house</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Exactly like a spreadsheet cell: column B, row 3.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-dot-vs-star", "kind": "trap", "front": "In NumPy, what is the difference between <code>w * x</code> and <code>np.dot(w, x)</code>?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>w * x → </b><code>[10, 40, 90]</code><br>elementwise — returns an <b>array</b></div><div><b>np.dot(w, x) → </b><code>140</code><br>multiplies <b>and sums</b> — returns a <b>number</b></div></div><div class=\"chint\">A neuron needs the number. Also: a Python <em>list</em> times 2 repeats it; a NumPy <em>array</em> times 2 doubles it. No error either way.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-02-vectorization.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two ways to multiply lists that look almost the same and do very different things. One gives you a list back; the other gives you a single number.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">*</td><td class=\"sa\">“star” / “times”</td><td>multiply each pair, keep them separate → a LIST</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.dot</td><td class=\"sa\">“numpy dot”</td><td>multiply each pair AND add them up → ONE number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">list</td><td class=\"sa\">“a Python list”</td><td>[1,2,3]. Times 2 makes it longer!</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">array</td><td class=\"sa\">“a NumPy array”</td><td>np.array([1,2,3]). Times 2 doubles each number</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Star = the price of each item. Dot = the total bill.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-why-fast", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why is <code>np.dot</code> faster than a for loop? (not “because NumPy is clever maths”)", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>the loop pays <b>Python interpreter overhead per element</b></li><li>np.dot hands a contiguous block to <b>compiled</b> library code (BLAS)</li><li>which uses <b>SIMD</b> — one instruction multiplying several pairs at once</li><li>and often multiple cores</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Same maths, same answer, 10–100× the speed. This is also why GPUs matter.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-03-why-vectorization-is-fast.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Your computer can multiply many pairs of numbers at the exact same instant. A slow loop hands it one pair at a time, so nearly all that power sits idle.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">loop</td><td class=\"sa\">“a for loop”</td><td>do it one at a time, in order</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">vectorised</td><td class=\"sa\">“VEK-tor-ised”</td><td>hand over the whole list at once</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">SIMD</td><td class=\"sa\">“sim-dee”</td><td>hardware that multiplies many pairs in one go</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">BLAS</td><td class=\"sa\">“blass”</td><td>an old, extremely fast maths library NumPy secretly uses</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">interpreter overhead</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the time Python wastes just deciding what to do next</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">One cashier serving a queue, versus twenty tills open at once.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-scaling-why", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why does <b>feature scaling</b> speed up gradient descent?", "back": "<p>Wildly different feature ranges make the cost bowl a <b>long thin canyon</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>the step is perpendicular to the contour, which in a canyon points <b>across</b> the valley, not along it → zig-zagging</li><li>and you must keep α small to stop it bouncing out, making the crawl slower still</li><li>scaled → near-circular contours → a direct path, and a much larger α is safe</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Both effects pull the same way, which is why the speedup is often an order of magnitude.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-05-feature-scaling.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>If one column counts to 2000 and another only to 5, the bowl gets squashed into a long thin canyon. Walking down a canyon by always heading straight downhill means bouncing off the walls.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">scaling</td><td class=\"sa\">“scaling”</td><td>shrinking all columns to roughly the same size</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">canyon</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a bowl that is much steeper one way than the other</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">zig-zag</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>bouncing side to side instead of walking forward</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Rescaling turns the canyon back into a round bowl, and then straight downhill really does point at the bottom.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-scaling-how", "kind": "formula", "front": "The three <b>feature scaling</b> methods.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>divide by max:</b> x := x / max &nbsp;→ 0…1</li><li><b>mean normalisation:</b> x := (x − μ) / (max − min) &nbsp;→ ≈ −0.5…0.5</li><li><b>z-score:</b> x := (x − μ) / σ &nbsp;→ mean 0, sd 1 &nbsp;<b>(the usual choice)</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Aim for roughly −1…1. Andrew's rules of thumb: −3…3 fine, −0.3…0.3 fine. 0…0.001 or −100…100 needs rescaling.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-05-feature-scaling.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Three recipes for shrinking every column to about the same size. The third is what nearly everybody uses.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew”</td><td>the mean — the plain average of that column</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>the standard deviation — how spread out that column is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x − μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus the average”</td><td>slides the numbers so they sit around zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">÷ σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“divided by sigma”</td><td>squeezes them so the spread is about 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">z-score</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee score”</td><td>the name for doing both of those together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like converting prices from yen and pounds into one shared currency before comparing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-scaling-trap", "kind": "trap", "front": "What are the two classic <b>feature scaling</b> mistakes?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>Computing μ and σ on the whole dataset</b> before splitting — leaks test information into training</li><li><b>Forgetting to scale at prediction time</b> — the model was trained on standardised inputs; feeding it a raw 2000 gives nonsense</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Fit the scaler on <b>train only</b>, then apply those same numbers everywhere. <code>sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline</code> exists to stop you forgetting.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-05-feature-scaling.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two mistakes that give you a model that looks fine and is quietly broken.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">leak</td><td class=\"sa\">“leakage”</td><td>letting the model peek at the test data by accident</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">fit the scaler</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>work out μ and σ. Do this on TRAINING data only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">apply the scaler</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>use those SAME numbers on everything else, forever</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like marking your own exam using next year's answer sheet — the score means nothing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-convergence", "kind": "concept", "front": "You plot J against iterations. What do the four shapes mean?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>falls, then flattens</b> → healthy and converged; stop</li><li><b>falls, still falling</b> → healthy; run longer</li><li><b>oscillates</b> → α too large; reduce it</li><li><b>increases steadily</b> → α far too large, or a bug</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Three lines of matplotlib, and the cheapest diagnostic in machine learning.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-06-checking-convergence.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Draw a graph of “how wrong am I” against “how many steps have I taken”. The shape of that line tells you everything.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">converged</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-VERGED”</td><td>it stopped improving — it has finished</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">plateau</td><td class=\"sa\">“pla-TOE”</td><td>gone flat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">oscillating</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>wobbling up and down, so your steps are too big</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">iteration</td><td class=\"sa\">“it-er-AY-shun”</td><td>one single step</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like watching a kettle: still rising, or has it settled?</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-alpha-ladder", "kind": "concept", "front": "How do you <b>choose α</b> in practice?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>try a ladder roughly ×3 apart: 0.001, 0.003, 0.01, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3, 1</li><li>plot J against iterations for each</li><li>keep the <b>largest</b> α that still decreases smoothly</li><li>optionally back off one notch for safety</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">×3 rather than +0.1 because α acts multiplicatively — equal ratios matter, not equal differences.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-07-choosing-the-learning-rate.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>There is no formula for the right step size. You try a few, look at the graphs, and keep the biggest one that still behaves.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">×3 apart</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>0.001, 0.003, 0.01… because step size acts by multiplying, not adding</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">smooth decrease</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the graph goes down cleanly with no wobbles</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">hyperparameter</td><td class=\"sa\">“hyper-parameter”</td><td>a setting YOU pick, not one the computer learns</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Trying shoe sizes: you jump 6, 7, 8 — not 7.01, 7.02, 7.03.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-feateng", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why can a linear model not learn that <b>area = frontage × depth</b> matters?", "back": "<p>Because its formula is a <b>weighted sum</b>: w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + b. There is no choice of w₁ and w₂ that produces a <em>product</em>. It is outside what the model can represent.</p><p>So you compute x₃ = x₁ × x₂ yourself and hand it over. That is <b>feature engineering</b>, and it is where domain knowledge enters an ML system.</p><div class=\"chint\">Products, ratios, differences, logs. Neural networks reduce the need for this — they do not remove it.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-08-feature-engineering.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The model can only ADD things up. It cannot multiply two of your columns together. So if what really matters is width × depth, you have to work that out yourself and hand it over.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">feature</td><td class=\"sa\">“FEE-cher”</td><td>one column of your data — one thing you measured</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">feature engineering</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>inventing a useful new column yourself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">interaction</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a new column made by multiplying two others together</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The model is a calculator stuck on the + key. If you need ×, you press it before handing over the number.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-polyreg", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why is <b>polynomial regression</b> still called <em>linear</em> regression?", "back": "<p>Because “linear” refers to being linear in the <b>parameters w</b>, not in x.</p><p>f = w₁x + w₂x² + w₃x³ + b is a weighted sum of features. That the features happen to be powers of x changes nothing about the algorithm.</p><div class=\"chint\">But it makes feature scaling <b>mandatory</b>: if x is 1–1000 then x³ is 1–10⁹.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-09-polynomial-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A straight line can't follow a curve. So you secretly hand the model x-squared as an extra column — and adding up straight things that include a squared thing gives you a curve.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">polynomial</td><td class=\"sa\">“poly-NOH-mee-al”</td><td>a formula with powers in it: x², x³</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">linear in the parameters</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>“linear” describes the w's, NOT the x's</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x³</td><td class=\"sa\">“x cubed”</td><td>x × x × x</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">You didn't change the machine. You changed what you fed it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w2-drill-zscore", "kind": "drill", "front": "A feature has &mu; = 218.67, &sigma; = 39.96. A house has x = 200. What is its z-score?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>z</var> = (200 − 218.67) / 39.96 = −18.67 / 39.96 = <b>−0.47</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Negative means below average — this house is a bit smaller than typical, by about half a standard deviation.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w2-05-feature-scaling.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Regression with Multiple Variables", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Take a house's size, subtract the average size, then divide by how spread-out sizes usually are. The answer says how many ‘typical wobbles’ this house is from average.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">&mu;</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew”</td><td>the average value of this feature</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">&sigma;</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>the typical spread — how much sizes usually vary</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">z</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee”</td><td>the rescaled value: how many spreads above or below average</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a school grade curve: a z-score of −0.47 means ‘a little below the class average’, regardless of what units the original number was in.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-why-not-linreg", "kind": "concept", "front": "Two reasons <b>linear regression fails</b> at classification.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>Unbounded output.</b> It will predict −0.4 or 1.8, which cannot be read as a class or a probability.</li><li><b>Outliers move the boundary.</b> Squared error punishes the line for being far from a point — even one that is unambiguously in its class. So a clear-cut example drags the decision boundary.</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">The second is the deeper one. In classification, an obvious example should barely influence the boundary; squared error makes it influence it <em>most</em>.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-01-motivations.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Using a straight line to answer yes/no questions breaks in two ways: it gives answers like −0.4, and one extreme-but-obvious example drags the whole line sideways.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">classification</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>sorting things into groups (yes/no) rather than guessing a number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">outlier</td><td class=\"sa\">“OUT-lie-er”</td><td>a point sitting far away from all the others</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">decision boundary</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the dividing line between “yes” and “no”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">unbounded</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>can be any number at all, including silly ones</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like letting one very tall person shift where you draw the line between “tall” and “short”.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-sigmoid", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>sigmoid</b>, and the logistic regression model.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>g</var>(<var>z</var>) = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>1 + <var>e</var><sup>−<var>z</var></sup></span></span></div><div class=\"ceq\"><var>z</var> = <b><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span>·<span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></b> + <var>b</var> &nbsp;→&nbsp; <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>g</var>(<var>z</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li>always strictly between 0 and 1 — never reaches either</li><li><b>g(0) = 0.5</b>, because e⁰ = 1</li><li>read the output as <b>P(y = 1 | x; w, b)</b></li></ul>", "lesson": "c1/w3-02-logistic-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A squasher. Feed it any number at all — minus a million, plus a million — and it hands back something between 0 and 1, which you can read as a chance.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">g(z)</td><td class=\"sa\">“g of z”</td><td>the squasher function</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">z</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee”</td><td>the plain old straight-line answer, before squashing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e</td><td class=\"sa\">“ee”</td><td>a fixed number, about 2.718. It just happens to make the maths tidy</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e^−z</td><td class=\"sa\">“e to the minus z”</td><td>gets tiny when z is big, huge when z is very negative</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">1/(1+…)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the shape that forces the answer between 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">P(y=1|x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“probability y is 1, given x”</td><td>the chance of a yes</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a volume knob that physically cannot go below 0 or above 10, however hard you turn it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-sigmoid-values", "kind": "number", "front": "Sigmoid values worth knowing: g(−5), g(−2), g(0), g(2), g(5)?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>g(−5) ≈ <b>0.007</b></li><li>g(−2) ≈ <b>0.12</b></li><li>g(0) = <b>0.50</b></li><li>g(2) ≈ <b>0.88</b></li><li>g(5) ≈ <b>0.993</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Past about ±5 the sigmoid has saturated — extra distance changes almost nothing. That is exactly why outliers stop dragging the boundary.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-02-logistic-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Worth memorising a few points on the S-curve so you can sanity-check numbers in your head.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">g(0) = 0.5</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>dead centre: completely undecided</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g(2) ≈ 0.88</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>fairly confident yes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g(5) ≈ 0.993</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>almost certain yes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">saturated</td><td class=\"sa\">“SAT-you-ray-ted”</td><td>so far along the curve it has flattened out and stopped responding</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Past about ±5 the curve is basically flat — pushing further changes nothing. That is exactly why extreme examples stop dragging the line.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-boundary", "kind": "concept", "front": "Where is the <b>decision boundary</b>, and what determines its shape?", "back": "<p>Wherever <b>z = 0</b> — because g(0) = 0.5 exactly.</p><div class=\"ceq\"><var>f</var> ≥ 0.5 &nbsp;↔&nbsp; <var>z</var> ≥ 0 &nbsp;↔&nbsp; <b><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span>·<span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></b> + <var>b</var> ≥ 0</div><p>The shape is a property of the <b>features you supply</b>, not of logistic regression. x₁, x₂ → a straight line. x₁², x₂² → a circle.</p><div class=\"chint\">The model is always linear in z. A curved boundary does not make it a non-linear model.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-03-decision-boundary.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The line where the model stops saying “no” and starts saying “yes”. It sits wherever the unsquashed answer is exactly zero, because that is where the squasher gives 0.5.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">z = 0</td><td class=\"sa\">“z is zero”</td><td>the exact tipping point</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">↔</td><td class=\"sa\">“if and only if”</td><td>these two statements always agree with each other</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">boundary</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the dividing line on your chart</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">threshold</td><td class=\"sa\">“THRESH-old”</td><td>the cutoff you chose — usually 0.5, but you may move it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≥</td><td class=\"sa\">greater than or equal to</td><td>at least this much. “≤” is at most</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The features you hand over decide the SHAPE of that line: a straight line, or a circle, or something wigglier.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-why-not-sq-error", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why can't logistic regression use <b>squared error</b>?", "back": "<p>Because f now has a <b>sigmoid inside it</b>. Squaring the error of a squashed function produces a <b>non-convex</b> surface — many local minima instead of one bowl.</p><div class=\"chint\">There is a second reason too: the gradient would pick up a g′(z) factor that is near zero exactly where the model is confidently wrong — learning stalls where it is needed most.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-04-cost-function-for-logistic-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The old scoring method now makes a lumpy landscape full of little dips instead of one clean bowl. Walk downhill and you get stuck in the nearest dip, thinking you're done.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">non-convex</td><td class=\"sa\">“non-CON-vex”</td><td>lumpy: many dips, not one bowl</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">local minimum</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a dip that is not the deepest one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">squared error</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the Course 1 scoring method: (guess − truth)²</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A golf course full of small hollows, versus one big funnel.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-logloss", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>logistic loss</b> — both the two-case and the one-line form.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>L</var> = −log(<var>f</var>) &nbsp;if <var>y</var> = 1 &nbsp;·&nbsp; −log(1 − <var>f</var>) &nbsp;if <var>y</var> = 0</div><div class=\"ceq\"><span class=\"lbl\">the combined version</span><var>L</var>(<var>f</var>, <var>y</var>) = <b>−<var>y</var> log(<var>f</var>) − (1 − <var>y</var>) log(1 − <var>f</var>)</b></div><p>Since y is only ever 0 or 1, one term is always multiplied by zero and disappears.</p><div class=\"chint\">This is <b>binary cross-entropy</b>. You will use it for every binary classifier in Course 2 and Course 3.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-06-simplified-cost-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A scoring rule with an opinion: being confidently wrong should hurt enormously; being confidently right should cost nearly nothing.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">L</td><td class=\"sa\">“ell”</td><td>the loss — badness for ONE example</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log</td><td class=\"sa\">“log”</td><td>a function that turns very small numbers into very big penalties</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−log(f)</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus log f”</td><td>used when the true answer is yes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−log(1−f)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>used when the true answer is no</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">y</td><td class=\"sa\">“why”</td><td>the true answer: 1 for yes, 0 for no</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">f</td><td class=\"sa\">“eff”</td><td>what the model guessed, between 0 and 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Because y is only ever 0 or 1, one of the two halves is multiplied by zero and vanishes.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-logloss-shape", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does the logistic loss do to a <b>confidently wrong</b> prediction?", "back": "<p>The penalty goes to <b>infinity</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>y = 1, f = 0.99 → loss <b>0.01</b> — confident and right, essentially free</li><li>y = 1, f = 0.5 → loss <b>0.69</b> — hedging, moderate cost</li><li>y = 1, f = 0.01 → loss <b>4.61</b> — confidently wrong, very expensive</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">−log(0.5) = 0.693 is the coin-flip reference point. A model averaging below it is beating guesswork.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-05-logistic-loss.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The penalty doesn't rise gently — it accelerates. Going from “fairly sure” to “very sure” about something false costs far more than the first mistake did.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">0.69</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the score for a pure coin flip (guessing 50/50)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">→ ∞</td><td class=\"sa\">“goes to infinity”</td><td>grows without limit as you get more confidently wrong</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">confident</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a guess close to 0 or close to 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a bet: shrugging costs you little, betting your house on a lie costs you the house.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-why-log", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why a <b>logarithm</b> in the loss? Two reasons.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>it makes the overall cost <b>convex</b>, so gradient descent gets its guarantee back</li><li>it is the <b>negative log-likelihood</b> — the w and b that make the observed data most probable</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">It is not an arbitrary trick; it falls out of statistics. And the sigmoid + log loss are a <em>matched pair</em> — the tidiness disappears if you mix and match.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-04-cost-function-for-logistic-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two good reasons the logarithm shows up here, and neither is “someone made it up”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">convex</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>makes the landscape a clean bowl again</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">likelihood</td><td class=\"sa\">“LIKE-lee-hood”</td><td>how probable your actual data is, under this model</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">negative log-likelihood</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>picking the settings that make what you SAW most believable</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">It isn't a trick — it falls straight out of statistics.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-gd-logistic", "kind": "formula", "front": "The gradient descent update for <b>logistic</b> regression.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>w<sub>j</sub></var> := <var>w<sub>j</sub></var> − <var>α</var> <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span><var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span> ( <var>f</var>(<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup>) − <var>y</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> ) <var>x<sub>j</sub></var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup></div><p><b>Character-for-character identical</b> to linear regression. The only difference is what f means: <code>g(w·x + b)</code> instead of <code>w·x + b</code>.</p><div class=\"chint\">Not a coincidence — g′(z) = g(z)(1−g(z)) cancels exactly against the 1/f from the logarithm.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-07-gradient-descent-logistic.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The step you take is written in exactly the same letters as before. Only the meaning of one symbol changed.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">f</td><td class=\"sa\">“eff”</td><td>before: the straight line. Now: the SQUASHED straight line</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">everything else</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>genuinely unchanged, letter for letter</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g′(z)</td><td class=\"sa\">“g prime of z”</td><td>the slope of the squasher — it cancels out neatly</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Same recipe, one swapped ingredient.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-overfit", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>Underfitting</b> vs <b>overfitting</b> — the names, the symptoms, the fixes.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>Underfitting</b><ul class='cbul'><li>also called <b>high bias</b></li><li>poor on training data</li><li>poor on new data</li><li>the model is too simple</li></ul></div><div><b>Overfitting</b><ul class='cbul'><li>also called <b>high variance</b></li><li><b>excellent</b> on training data</li><li>poor on new data</li><li>the model is too flexible</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Both look like “the model is bad”, and they need <b>opposite</b> fixes. That is why naming them matters.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-08-the-problem-of-overfitting.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two opposite ways to be bad at this, and they need opposite cures — so naming them correctly matters.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">underfit / high bias</td><td class=\"sa\">“BY-ass”</td><td>too simple. Wrong even on stuff it has already seen</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">overfit / high variance</td><td class=\"sa\">“VAIR-ee-ance”</td><td>memorised the answers. Great on old stuff, useless on new</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">generalise</td><td class=\"sa\">“JEN-er-al-ise”</td><td>do well on things it has never seen — the only thing that counts</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Underfit = the student who learned one fact. Overfit = the student who memorised last year's exam paper.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-address-overfit", "kind": "concept", "front": "Three ways to <b>address overfitting</b>, and why the third is usually first.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>more data</b> — the best fix, and often impossible</li><li><b>fewer features</b> — you may throw away one that mattered</li><li><b>regularisation</b> — keep every feature, shrink the weights</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Regularisation beats feature selection because it does not force a binary choice: a feature that matters a little keeps a small weight.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-09-addressing-overfitting.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Three ways to stop a model memorising. The third keeps all your information, which is why people reach for it first.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">more data</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>with enough examples there is no room left to memorise</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">fewer features</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>fewer dials to fiddle with</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">regularisation</td><td class=\"sa\">“REG-you-lar-eye-zay-shun”</td><td>keep every dial, but don't let any turn too far</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Option 2 throws a tool out of the box. Option 3 just stops you swinging it too hard.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-regcost", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>regularised cost function</b>.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>J</var> = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>2<var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span>( <var>f</var> − <var>y</var> )<sup>2</sup> + <span class='fr'><span><var>λ</var></span><span>2<var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>j</var>=1</sub><sup><var>n</var></sup> <var>w<sub>j</sub></var><sup>2</sup></div><ul class='cbul'><li>first term: <b>fit the data</b>. second term: <b>keep the weights small</b></li><li>λ = 0 → no penalty, overfits. λ enormous → all w ≈ 0, f ≈ b, underfits</li><li>the sum starts at <b>j = 1</b> — <b>b is not regularised</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Shrinking b only slides the curve up and down; it does nothing about wiggliness.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-10-cost-function-with-regularization.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You now tell the model two things at once: “match the data” AND “keep your numbers small”. It has to balance them, so it only uses a big number when a big number really earns its place.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ</td><td class=\"sa\">“lambda”</td><td>how loudly you say the second thing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ w²</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum of w squared”</td><td>how big the dials are overall</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ = 0</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>second rule switched off → memorises</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ huge</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>second rule shouts → everything flattens to a straight line</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">j = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">“starting at one”</td><td>deliberately skips b — b is left alone</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a budget: you can spend, but every pound has to be justified.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-weight-decay", "kind": "formula", "front": "Rearrange the regularised update rule. What is the resulting name?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>w<sub>j</sub></var> := <b>(1 − <var>αλ</var>/<var>m</var>)</b> <var>w<sub>j</sub></var> − <var>α</var> <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span><var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span>( <var>f</var> − <var>y</var> )<var>x<sub>j</sub></var></div><p><b>Weight decay.</b> Every iteration multiplies w by a number just below 1 <em>before</em> taking the ordinary step.</p><div class=\"chint\">This is the <code>weight_decay</code> argument in every modern optimiser — AdamW, <code>kernel_regularizer=l2(0.01)</code>, all of it.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-11-regularized-gradient-descent.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Rearranged, the update says: shrink every dial by a hair FIRST, then take your normal step. Do that thousands of times and dials only stay big if the data keeps pushing them back up.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">(1 − αλ/m)</td><td class=\"sa\">“one minus alpha lambda over m”</td><td>a number just under 1, like 0.9998</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">decay</td><td class=\"sa\">“de-KAY”</td><td>fade away slowly</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">weight decay</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the name for this, used in every modern ML library</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a slow puncture: constant tiny leak, so you only stay inflated if you keep pumping.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c1w3-drill-sigmoid", "kind": "drill", "front": "w = 1, b = 0, x = 1.5. Compute z, then g(z).", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>z</var> = 1(1.5) + 0 = 1.5</div><div class=\"ceq\"><var>g</var>(1.5) = 1 / (1 + <var>e</var><sup>−1.5</sup>) = 1 / (1 + 0.223) = <b>0.818</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Above 0.5, so this example is classified y&#770; = 1 — and fairly confidently.</div>", "lesson": "c1/w3-02-logistic-regression.html", "extra": "", "course": "C1", "week": "c1w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Classification", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Weigh the input, add the bias, and you get a raw score z. Squash that raw score through the S-shaped sigmoid curve, and out comes a number between 0 and 1 you can read as a probability.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">z</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee”</td><td>the raw, unsquashed score — can be any number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g(z)</td><td class=\"sa\">“g of z”</td><td>the sigmoid function — squashes z into 0…1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">z=1.5 is a fair way into ‘probably yes’ territory, and the squashed answer, 0.818, confirms it: about an 82% chance of being class 1.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-neuron", "kind": "formula", "front": "What does <b>one artificial neuron</b> compute?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>z</var> = <b><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span>·<span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></b> + <var>b</var> &nbsp;→&nbsp; <var>a</var> = <var>g</var>(<var>z</var>)</div><p>Identical to logistic regression. <b>One neuron <em>is</em> one logistic regression unit.</b></p><ul class='cbul'><li>w — how much this neuron trusts each input (learned)</li><li>b — its default mood (learned)</li><li>a — the activation it passes on</li></ul>", "lesson": "c2/w1-01-neurons-and-the-brain.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A neuron is a kid who listens to a few friends, trusts each one a different amount, adds it all up, and then shouts an answer between 0 and 1. That is genuinely all it does.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\"><span class=\"ov vec\">w</span></td><td class=\"sa\">“w vector”</td><td>the trust levels — one per friend</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\"><span class=\"ov vec\">x</span></td><td class=\"sa\">“x vector”</td><td>what the friends said — the inputs</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">·</td><td class=\"sa\">“dot”</td><td>multiply each pair and add them up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">b</td><td class=\"sa\">“bee”</td><td>the kid's own stubborn starting mood</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">z</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee”</td><td>the raw total, before squashing. Any number at all</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g</td><td class=\"sa\">“g”</td><td>the squasher</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a</td><td class=\"sa\">“a” (activation)</td><td>what it finally shouts out and passes on</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">This is exactly logistic regression from Course 1. One neuron IS one logistic regression.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-hidden-layer", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does a <b>hidden layer</b> actually buy you over plain logistic regression?", "back": "<p><b>Learned features.</b> In Course 1 you invented x₁x₂ or x² by hand. A hidden layer invents its own intermediate features and learns which are worth keeping.</p><div class=\"chint\">The names we give hidden units (“affordability”, “awareness”) are a story we tell afterwards. Nothing in training assigns them meaning.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-02-demand-prediction.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>In Course 1 you invented useful new columns by hand. A hidden layer invents its own — and works out which of its inventions are worth keeping.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">hidden layer</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a middle row of neurons you never see the output of directly</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">learned features</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>new columns the network made up for itself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">hidden unit</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one neuron in that middle row</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The names people give hidden units (“affordability”, “awareness”) are a story told afterwards. Nothing in training assigns them meaning.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-layer", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is a <b>layer</b>, and what does <code>units=3</code> control?", "back": "<p>Several neurons that all read the <b>same input vector</b> and each output <b>one</b> number.</p><p><code>units</code> sets the <b>length of that layer's output vector</b>. Nothing else — not the number of inputs, not the number of examples.</p><div class=\"chint\">Neurons within a layer never talk to each other. That independence is exactly what lets them be computed as one matrix multiply.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-04-neural-network-layer.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A layer is a panel of judges. They all watch the same performance, each holds up one score card, and the row of score cards is what gets passed on.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">layer</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a group of neurons that all read the same input</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">units</td><td class=\"sa\">“units” / “neurons”</td><td>the same thing. How many judges are on the panel</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">units=3</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>three judges, so three numbers come out</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The judges never talk to each other — which is exactly why a computer can work them all out at the same instant.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-params", "kind": "formula", "front": "How many <b>parameters</b> in a layer with n inputs and p units?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">weights = <var>n</var> × <var>p</var> &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; biases = <var>p</var></div><p>e.g. 400 inputs → 25 units: 400×25 = <b>10,000</b> weights + <b>25</b> biases.</p><div class=\"chint\"><code>model.summary()</code> prints exactly these totals. Comparing them against a hand count is the fastest way to catch a wiring mistake.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-04-neural-network-layer.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Counting the knobs the network has to learn. Every input connects to every neuron, so you multiply — then add one extra knob per neuron for its mood.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">parameter</td><td class=\"sa\">“pa-RAM-eter”</td><td>a number the network learns for itself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">n</td><td class=\"sa\">“en”</td><td>how many numbers come IN</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p</td><td class=\"sa\">“pee”</td><td>how many neurons are in this layer</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">n × p</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one wire from each input to each neuron</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">+ p</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one bias per neuron</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like plugging every one of 400 cables into each of 25 sockets: 10,000 connections.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-master-eq", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>master equation</b> of forward propagation.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>a</var><sub><var>j</var></sub><sup>[<var>l</var>]</sup> = <var>g</var>( <b>w</b><sub><var>j</var></sub><sup>[<var>l</var>]</sup> · <b>a</b><sup>[<var>l</var>−1]</sup> + <var>b</var><sub><var>j</var></sub><sup>[<var>l</var>]</sup> )</div><p>“The activation of unit <b>j</b> in layer <b>l</b> is g of: the weights of unit j in layer l, dotted with the <b>whole</b> output of the previous layer, plus its bias.”</p><div class=\"chint\">a<sup>[0]</sup> = x, so the formula works for layer 1 with no special case.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-05-more-complex-networks.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One line that describes every single calculation in every network in this course. It looks scary and it is only saying: “this neuron listens to the whole row before it, then shouts.”</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">a</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>what a neuron shouts out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">subscript j</td><td class=\"sa\">“unit j”</td><td>WHICH neuron in the row</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">[l]</td><td class=\"sa\">“layer l”</td><td>WHICH row it is in</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">[l−1]</td><td class=\"sa\">“the row before”</td><td>the previous layer — it listens to ALL of that row</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">w, b</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>that particular neuron's trust levels and mood</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the squasher</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Say it out loud: “what neuron j in row l shouts is the squash of — its own trust levels, dotted with everything the previous row said, plus its own mood.”</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-brackets", "kind": "trap", "front": "In a neural network, what does a <b>square-bracket superscript</b> mean, and what does it never mean?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>a<sup>[2]</sup></b> — layer 2. <b>Never</b> a power.</li><li><b>x<sup>(2)</sup></b> — training example 2</li><li><b>x<sup>2</sup></b> — x squared</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Reading <sup>[2]</sup> as “squared” is the single most common notation error in Course 2.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-05-more-complex-networks.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The same tiny raised number means three different things depending on the brackets around it. Mixing these up is the number one way formulas become unreadable.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">a[²]</td><td class=\"sa\">“a, layer two”</td><td>square brackets → which LAYER</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x⁽²⁾</td><td class=\"sa\">“x, example two”</td><td>round brackets → which EXAMPLE</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x²</td><td class=\"sa\">“x squared”</td><td>no brackets → a genuine power</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Reading a[²] as “a squared” is the single most common mistake in Course 2.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-weight-vec-len", "kind": "concept", "front": "A network is 4 → 5 → 3 → 1. How long is the weight vector <b>w<sub>2</sub><sup>[3]</sup></b>?", "back": "<p><b>3.</b> Layer 3 reads layer 2, which has 3 units.</p><div class=\"chint\">Weight-vector length always equals the <b>width of the previous layer</b>. And unit 2 of layer 3 reads <em>every</em> unit of layer 2, not just unit 2.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-05-more-complex-networks.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>How many trust levels does one neuron need? Exactly as many as there are things talking to it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">w₂[³]</td><td class=\"sa\">“w two, layer three”</td><td>the trust levels of neuron 2 in row 3</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">previous layer width</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how many neurons are in the row before</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">4 → 5 → 3 → 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a network with rows of those sizes</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Careful: neuron 2 of row 3 listens to EVERY neuron in row 2 — not just neuron 2.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-dense-cols", "kind": "concept", "front": "In <code>W</code> for a dense layer, what do the <b>rows</b> and <b>columns</b> mean?", "back": "<p><b>Columns are neurons. Rows are input features.</b></p><p><code>W[:, j]</code> is neuron j's personal weight vector. W of shape (2, 3) = 2 inputs, 3 neurons.</p><div class=\"chint\">This orientation is what makes the vectorised version work with no transposes.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-10-forward-prop-single-layer.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>W is a table of trust levels. Reading it the right way round is what makes everything else work.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">W</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital double-you”</td><td>the whole layer's trust levels as one table</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">columns</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one column per NEURON</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">rows</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one row per INPUT</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">W[:, j]</td><td class=\"sa\">“all rows, column j”</td><td>the colon means “everything”. Grabs neuron j's own column</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">shape (2,3)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>2 inputs, 3 neurons</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Careful: W[0] grabs a ROW, not a column. Different thing entirely, and NumPy won't warn you.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-shapes", "kind": "trap", "front": "What is the difference between <code>(2,)</code> and <code>(1, 2)</code> in NumPy?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>np.array([200, 17]) → (2,)</b>a <b>1-D array</b> — no rows or columns at all. Old Course 1 style; Keras will not accept it.</div><div><b>np.array([[200, 17]]) → (1,2)</b>a <b>matrix</b>: 1 row × 2 columns. One training example with two features.</div></div><div class=\"chint\">Convention for the whole specialization: <b>rows = examples, columns = features</b>.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-08-data-in-tensorflow.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two things that look almost identical to a human and completely different to the computer. The extra pair of brackets is doing real work.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">(2,)</td><td class=\"sa\">“shape two comma”</td><td>a plain list of 2 numbers. No rows, no columns</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">(1, 2)</td><td class=\"sa\">“shape one by two”</td><td>a proper table: 1 row, 2 columns</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the trailing comma</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>Python's way of writing a one-item tuple. It is not a typo</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">rows / columns</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>rows = examples, columns = features. Always</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A bag of eggs versus an egg box. Same eggs; only the box says which is a row.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-dense-code", "kind": "code", "front": "Write <code>dense()</code> — the looped version.", "back": "<pre><code>def dense(a_in, W, b):\n    units = W.shape[1]        # columns = neurons\n    a_out = np.zeros(units)\n    for j in range(units):\n        w = W[:, j]           # column j\n        z = np.dot(w, a_in) + b[j]\n        a_out[j] = g(z)\n    return a_out</code></pre><div class=\"chint\"><code>W.shape[1]</code>, not <code>[0]</code> — [0] is the number of <em>inputs</em>.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-11-general-forward-prop.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The slow, honest version. Walk along the columns of the trust table, one neuron at a time, and fill in its answer.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">W.shape[1]</td><td class=\"sa\">“shape, index one”</td><td>the number of COLUMNS = number of neurons</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">W.shape[0]</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the number of ROWS = number of inputs. NOT what you want here</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.zeros(units)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>an empty box with a slot for each answer</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">for j in range(units)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>do this once per neuron</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.dot(w, a_in)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>multiply the pairs and add them up</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Using shape[0] instead of shape[1] gives you an answer of the wrong length — a very common bug.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-dense-vec", "kind": "code", "front": "Write <code>dense()</code> — the <b>vectorised</b> version.", "back": "<pre><code>def dense(A_in, W, B):\n    Z = np.matmul(A_in, W) + B   # (m,n)@(n,units) -> (m,units)\n    return g(Z)</code></pre><p>No loop, no slicing, no index arithmetic. <code>B</code> is (1, units) and broadcasting adds it to every row.</p>", "lesson": "c2/w1-16-matmul-code.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The same thing with the loop deleted. One instruction now does every neuron for every example at the same instant.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">A_in</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital A in”</td><td>ALL your examples stacked up, one per row</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">np.matmul</td><td class=\"sa\">“mat-mul”</td><td>matrix multiply — a whole grid of dot products at once</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">@</td><td class=\"sa\">“at”</td><td>shorthand for np.matmul</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">B</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital B”</td><td>the biases, one row</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">broadcasting</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>NumPy quietly copying that one row down every row for you</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Six lines like this, stacked, is a complete working neural network.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-matmul-rule", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>matrix multiplication shape rule</b>.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">(<var>m</var> × <b><var>n</var></b>) × (<b><var>n</var></b> × <var>p</var>) = (<var>m</var> × <var>p</var>)</div><p>Write the two shapes side by side. If the <b>inner</b> numbers match it is legal, and the answer is the <b>outer</b> two.</p><div class=\"chint\">The inner dimension gets summed away — it never appears in the result. And A@W ≠ W@A; usually one of them is not even legal.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-15-matmul-rules.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A test you can do in your head to check whether two tables can be multiplied, and what size the answer will be.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">(m × n)</td><td class=\"sa\">“m by n”</td><td>a table with m rows and n columns</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">inner numbers</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the two in the middle. They MUST match</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">outer numbers</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the two on the ends. They become your answer's size</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the inner number</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>gets added away and vanishes from the result</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≠</td><td class=\"sa\">not equal to</td><td>these two are different — the shape rule uses it a lot</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Write the two shapes side by side: (2×3)(3×4). Middles match → legal. Answer is (2×4).</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-dotprod", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why does a neuron need a <b>dot product</b> rather than elementwise multiplication?", "back": "<p>Because a neuron must produce a <b>single number</b> z. Elementwise multiplication leaves you with a list.</p><div class=\"chint\">Geometrically a·w = |a||w|cos θ — it measures how much the input <em>resembles the pattern stored in the weights</em>.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-14-matrix-multiplication.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A neuron has to end up with ONE number. Multiplying lists item by item leaves you with a list, which is no use.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">dot product</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>multiply the pairs AND add them up → one number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">elementwise</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>multiply the pairs and stop → still a list</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cos θ</td><td class=\"sa\">“cos theta”</td><td>how much two arrows point the same way</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A shopping list × a price list. Elementwise gives you each item's cost; the dot product gives you the total bill.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-vectorization", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why did neural networks become practical in the 2010s?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>data</b> — the internet supplied millions of labelled examples</li><li><b>compute</b> — GPUs made the matrix multiplies ~100× cheaper</li><li><b>scale behaviour</b> — classical algorithms plateau with more data; large networks keep improving</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The maths is from 1958 and 1986. Nothing about it changed.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-13-vectorization.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The maths behind neural networks is from 1958 and 1986. Three other things changed, and that is why they suddenly started working.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">data</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the internet supplied millions of labelled examples</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">compute</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>graphics cards made the maths about 100× cheaper</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">GPU</td><td class=\"sa\">“gee-pee-you”</td><td>a graphics card: thousands of tiny calculators working at once</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">scale behaviour</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>old methods stop improving with more data; big networks keep going</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Nobody invented better maths. They got more examples and faster chips.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w1-drill-forwardprop", "kind": "drill", "front": "One neuron: w = [2, −1], b = 0.5, x = [1, 3]. Compute z, then a (sigmoid).", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>z</var> = 2(1) + (−1)(3) + 0.5 = 2 − 3 + 0.5 = −0.5</div><div class=\"ceq\"><var>a</var> = <var>g</var>(−0.5) = 1 / (1 + <var>e</var><sup>0.5</sup>) = <b>0.378</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Below 0.5 — this neuron does not fire. Same two-step recipe at every unit in every layer.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w1-04-neural-network-layer.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Neural Networks", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A neuron weighs each input by how much it trusts it, adds a starting bias, and squashes the total into 0…1 with the sigmoid curve. Two inputs, two weights, one bias, one squash.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">w</td><td class=\"sa\">“double-you”</td><td>how much this neuron trusts each input</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">b</td><td class=\"sa\">“bee”</td><td>the neuron's default mood before seeing any input</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">z</td><td class=\"sa\">“zee”</td><td>the raw weighted total, before squashing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a</td><td class=\"sa\">“ay”</td><td>the squashed output — what the neuron actually passes on</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">z came out negative, so after squashing a landed below 0.5 — this neuron stays quiet.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-three-steps", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>three steps</b> of training in TensorFlow, and their Course 1 twins.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>define the model</b> — <code>Sequential([...])</code> ← what f(x) is</li><li><b>say what wrong means</b> — <code>model.compile(loss=...)</code> ← the cost function</li><li><b>minimise it</b> — <code>model.fit(X, Y, epochs=...)</code> ← gradient descent</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Only <code>fit</code> changes the weights. <code>compile</code> just records settings.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-01-tensorflow-training.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Training anything, in three moves — and they are the same three from Course 1, wearing TensorFlow's clothes.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Sequential</td><td class=\"sa\">“se-QUEN-shal”</td><td>stack these layers, feed each into the next</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">compile</td><td class=\"sa\">“com-PILE”</td><td>write down the rules. Changes nothing yet</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">loss=</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>which scoring rule to use</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">fit</td><td class=\"sa\">“fit”</td><td>actually train. The ONLY step that changes the numbers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">epochs</td><td class=\"sa\">“EP-ocks”</td><td>how many times to go through the whole dataset</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like teaching a dog: name the trick, define what “wrong” means, then practise.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-bce", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Binary cross-entropy</b> — and the difference between loss and cost.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>L</var>(<var>f</var>, <var>y</var>) = −<var>y</var> log(<var>f</var>) − (1 − <var>y</var>) log(1 − <var>f</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>loss</b> L — the error on <b>one</b> example</li><li><b>cost</b> J — the <b>average</b> loss over all m examples: J = (1/m)ΣL</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The distinction is asked about in interviews and relied on throughout Course 2.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-02-training-details.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The scoring rule for yes/no answers, and a word distinction people get tested on.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">L</td><td class=\"sa\">“ell” (loss)</td><td>how wrong you were on ONE example</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">J</td><td class=\"sa\">“jay” (cost)</td><td>the AVERAGE loss over all your examples</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cross-entropy</td><td class=\"sa\">“cross-EN-tro-pee”</td><td>the official name for this scoring rule</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>turns a tiny probability into a huge penalty</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">y</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the true answer: 1 or 0</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Loss = one exam question. Cost = your overall mark.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-relu", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>ReLU</b>, and why it replaced sigmoid in hidden layers.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>g</var>(<var>z</var>) = max(0, <var>z</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>slope is exactly 1</b> on the positive side → gradients survive deep stacks</li><li>one <code>max</code>, no <code>exp</code> → meaningfully faster</li><li>sigmoid's slope peaks at 0.25; stack ten layers and 0.25¹⁰ ≈ 10⁻⁶ — the early layers receive nothing</li></ul>", "lesson": "c2/w2-03-sigmoid-alternatives.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The simplest useful squasher there is: if the number is negative, say zero; otherwise pass it straight through. Two words of logic, and it beat the elegant S-curve.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">ReLU</td><td class=\"sa\">“RAY-loo”</td><td>Rectified Linear Unit. A blunt but very effective squasher</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">max(0, z)</td><td class=\"sa\">“max of zero and z”</td><td>whichever is bigger</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">slope</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how steep the squasher is. Learning needs slope</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">saturate</td><td class=\"sa\">“SAT-you-rate”</td><td>go flat, so it stops responding to anything</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">vanishing gradient</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the learning signal fading to nothing through deep stacks</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Sigmoid's slope maxes out at 0.25. Multiply that through ten layers and you get 0.0000001 — the early layers hear nothing at all.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-dying-relu", "kind": "trap", "front": "What is a <b>dying ReLU</b>, and why is it permanent?", "back": "<p>A unit whose z is negative for <b>every</b> training example. Its gradient is 0 always, so its weights never update, so its z never changes.</p><div class=\"chint\">A lower learning rate, or Leaky ReLU, avoids it.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-03-sigmoid-alternatives.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>ReLU's one weakness. A neuron that outputs zero for every single example gets no learning signal, so it can never fix itself. It's stuck forever.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">dying ReLU</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a neuron permanently stuck at zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">gradient = 0</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>no learning signal, so the dials never move</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Leaky ReLU</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a version with a tiny slope on the negative side, so it can recover</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A light switch jammed off — and the only thing that could unjam it is the light being on.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-activation-choice", "kind": "concept", "front": "Which <b>output activation</b> for each task? And for hidden layers?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>binary classification → <b>sigmoid</b></li><li>regression, can be negative → <b>linear</b></li><li>regression, never negative → <b>ReLU</b></li><li>multiclass, pick one → <b>softmax</b></li><li><b>every hidden layer → ReLU</b>, almost always</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">“Linear activation” means <em>no</em> activation: g(z) = z.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-04-choosing-activations.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The last layer is the one that talks to you, so it has to speak the right language. The middle layers talk only to each other, so they don't need to be polite.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">activation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the squasher applied at the end of a layer</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sigmoid</td><td class=\"sa\">“SIG-moid”</td><td>answers between 0 and 1 → a chance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">linear</td><td class=\"sa\">“LIN-ee-ar”</td><td>no squashing at all. Any number, positive or negative</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">softmax</td><td class=\"sa\">“soft max”</td><td>several chances that add up to 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ReLU</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>zero or positive. Also the default for every hidden layer</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">“Linear activation” and “no activation” are the same sentence.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-why-nonlinear", "kind": "concept", "front": "Prove that a network with <b>linear activations everywhere</b> is pointless.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>a</var><sup>[2]</sup> = <var>W</var><sup>[2]</sup>(<var>W</var><sup>[1]</sup><var>x</var> + <var>b</var><sup>[1]</sup>) + <var>b</var><sup>[2]</sup> = <b>(<var>W</var><sup>[2]</sup><var>W</var><sup>[1]</sup>)</b><var>x</var> + <b>(…)</b> = <var>W′x</var> + <var>b′</var></div><p>Two matrices multiplied together are just another matrix. A 100-layer linear network is <b>algebraically identical to one layer</b> — exactly zero extra expressive power.</p><div class=\"chint\">Each ReLU unit adds exactly one “kink”. Enough kinks and you can trace any curve.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-05-why-activations.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Tape two straight sticks together in a line and you get… a longer straight stick. Do it a hundred times and it's still straight. Something has to be allowed to bend.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">W[¹], W[²]</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the trust tables of two layers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">collapse</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>several layers turning out to be the same as one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">W′</td><td class=\"sa\">“W prime”</td><td>the single table the two multiply out into</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">non-linear</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>not a straight line. What lets the network bend</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A hundred linear layers has exactly as much power as one. Not less accuracy — literally none extra.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-softmax", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Softmax</b> — the formula and what the two moves do.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>a<sub>j</sub></var> = <span class='fr'><span><var>e</var><sup><var>z<sub>j</sub></var></sup></span><span><span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>k</var></sub> <var>e</var><sup><var>z<sub>k</sub></var></sup></span></span> = <var>P</var>(<var>y</var> = <var>j</var> | <var>x</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>exp</b> → makes everything positive</li><li><b>÷ by the total</b> → makes them sum to exactly 1</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The <b>only</b> activation where each output depends on all the others. That coupling is what forces the sum to 1.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-07-softmax.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Ten judges shout scores; some are negative, some huge. Two moves turn them into “what share of the vote did each get”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">softmax</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a gentle way of picking the biggest, letting the others keep a share</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e^z</td><td class=\"sa\">“e to the z”</td><td>makes every score positive, and stretches the gaps</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum of”</td><td>add all of them up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">÷ Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>divide by the total, so the shares add to exactly 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a_j</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>class j's share — its probability</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The only squasher where each output depends on all the others. Push one up and the rest must come down — they share a fixed budget of 100%.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-softmax-shift", "kind": "concept", "front": "z = [10, 1, 1] and z = [110, 101, 101]. Do these give the same softmax output?", "back": "<p><b>Yes — identical.</b> Softmax depends only on the <b>differences</b> between the z's. Adding a constant to every z changes nothing.</p><div class=\"chint\">This fact is the basis of the numerical fix: compute <code>exp(z − max(z))</code>, so the largest exponent is exp(0) = 1 and overflow is impossible.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-07-softmax.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Softmax only cares about the GAPS between scores, not the scores themselves. Add the same amount to every score and nothing changes at all.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">differences</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the gaps between the z's. This is all softmax sees</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">overflow</td><td class=\"sa\">“OH-ver-flow”</td><td>a number too big for the computer to hold → infinity</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">exp(z − max z)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>subtract the biggest first, so nothing ever explodes</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a race: adding 10 seconds to everyone's time changes nobody's position.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-from-logits", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does <code>from_logits=True</code> do, and what is the catch?", "back": "<p>It tells Keras the output layer is <code>linear</code> and the loss should apply the sigmoid/softmax itself — using an algebraically rearranged formula that never builds the intermediate probability, so nothing rounds to 0 or 1.</p><div class=\"ceq\"><span class=\"lbl\">same maths, safer</span>−log( 1/(1+<var>e</var><sup>−z</sup>) ) = log(1 + <var>e</var><sup>−<var>z</var></sup>)</div><p><b>The catch:</b> <code>predict</code> now returns raw logits, not probabilities. Apply <code>tf.nn.softmax()</code> yourself.</p><div class=\"chint\">At z = 20, sigmoid rounds to exactly 1.0 in float32, so −log(1) = 0 — the model is told it made no error and learning stops.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-09-improved-softmax.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Computers store numbers with limited precision. Squash first and then take a log, and the answer can round away to nothing. Rearranging the sum avoids ever building the dangerous middle number.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">logits</td><td class=\"sa\">“LOW-jits”</td><td>the raw scores BEFORE any squashing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">from_logits=True</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>“I'll hand you raw scores, you do the squashing safely”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">float32</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the computer's number format: about 7 useful digits</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">rounds to 1.0</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>0.999999998 becomes exactly 1, and then log(1) = 0 — the error vanishes</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The catch: predict() now hands back raw scores, not chances. Run tf.nn.softmax on them yourself.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-multiclass-vs-label", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>Multi-class</b> vs <b>multi-label</b>.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>Multi-class</b><ul class='cbul'><li>exactly <b>one</b> answer is correct</li><li>y is an integer, e.g. 7</li><li><code>Dense(N, 'softmax')</code></li><li>outputs sum to <b>1</b></li><li>outputs are coupled</li></ul></div><div><b>Multi-label</b><ul class='cbul'><li><b>several</b> can be true at once</li><li>y is a vector, e.g. [1, 0, 1]</li><li><code>Dense(N, 'sigmoid')</code></li><li>outputs sum to anything</li><li>outputs are independent</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Using softmax for multi-label structurally prevents the model from saying “both” — it can never be right about a photo containing a car and a bus.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-10-multi-label.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two questions that look the same and are not: “which ONE is it?” versus “which of these are present?”</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">multi-class</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>exactly one answer is right. Cat OR dog OR horse</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">multi-label</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>several can be right at once. Car AND bus AND person</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">mutually exclusive</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew-chew-ally ex-CLOO-siv”</td><td>only one can be true</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">softmax</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>forces the answers to compete for one shared 100%</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sigmoid ×N</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>gives each question its own separate 100%</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Use softmax for multi-label and the model literally cannot say “both”, however clear the photo.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-adam", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does <b>Adam</b> do that plain gradient descent does not?", "back": "<p>It keeps a <b>separate learning rate for every parameter</b> and adapts each one:</p><ul class='cbul'><li>gradient consistently the same direction → <b>increase</b> the step</li><li>gradient keeps flip-flopping → <b>decrease</b> the step</li></ul><div class=\"ceq\"><var>w</var> ← <var>w</var> − <span class='fr'><span><var>α m̂</var></span><span>√<var>v̂</var> + <var>ε</var></span></span></div><div class=\"chint\">m = running mean of the gradient (momentum). v = running mean of the <em>squared</em> gradient (bumpiness). ADAptive Moment estimation — not a person's name.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-11-advanced-optimization.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Plain gradient descent uses one stride length for every dial. Adam gives each dial its own, and adjusts it as it goes: bigger strides where progress is steady, smaller where it keeps flip-flopping.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Adam</td><td class=\"sa\">“Adam”</td><td>ADAptive Moment estimation. Not a person's name</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m</td><td class=\"sa\">“momentum”</td><td>a running average of recent slopes. Steady direction → bigger steps</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">v</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a running average of SQUARED slopes: how bumpy this dial has been</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√v on the bottom</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>bumpier dial → smaller steps</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">β₁, β₂</td><td class=\"sa\">“beta one, beta two”</td><td>how much history to keep. 0.9 and 0.999. Rarely worth changing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ε</td><td class=\"sa\">“epsilon”</td><td>a tiny number, only there to avoid dividing by zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Walking down a long narrow valley: long strides along the floor, tiny careful ones across it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-conv", "kind": "concept", "front": "What makes a <b>convolutional</b> layer different from a dense layer?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>each unit sees only a <b>small window</b>, not every input</li><li>all units <b>share the same weights</b> (one kernel, slid along)</li></ul><p>Result: far fewer parameters, faster training, less overfitting — and a detector learned in one place works everywhere.</p><div class=\"chint\">The restriction <em>is</em> the benefit: it encodes the true fact that nearby pixels are related and position should not change what a thing is.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-12-additional-layer-types.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A dense neuron reads the entire picture. A convolutional one peeks through a small window — and every window uses the SAME set of trust levels.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">convolutional</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-vo-LOO-shun-al”</td><td>sliding a small detector across the input</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">kernel / filter</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the little detector. Just a few numbers, reused everywhere</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">window</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the small patch one neuron can see</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">weight sharing</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>all the neurons use one shared detector. The key idea</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">receptive field</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how much of the original input one neuron ends up depending on</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Learn “what an edge looks like” once, and you can spot edges anywhere in the picture for free.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-backprop-cost", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why is <b>backpropagation</b> the reason deep learning is possible at all?", "back": "<p>It computes <b>all N derivatives in about two forward passes</b>, regardless of N.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>nudging each parameter separately → N forward passes</li><li>forward-mode autodiff → still N sweeps</li><li><b>backprop → 1 forward + 1 backward</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">For a million parameters that is 2 passes instead of a million. Reverse-mode autodiff is efficient exactly when you have many inputs and one output — the shape of a loss function.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-15-larger-network-example.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The surprising fact that makes training possible at all: you can find out how ALL million dials affect the final answer in about two passes, not a million.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">backpropagation</td><td class=\"sa\">“back-prop-a-GAY-shun”</td><td>walking backwards through the network to get all the slopes at once</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">forward pass</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>numbers flowing left to right, making a prediction</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">backward pass</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>slopes flowing right to left</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">N</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how many parameters the network has</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Testing each dial one at a time would need a million experiments. Backprop needs two.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-chain-rule", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>chain rule</b>, as backprop uses it.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><span class='fr'><span>∂<var>J</var></span><span>∂<var>w</var></span></span> = <span class='fr'><span>∂<var>J</var></span><span>∂<var>d</var></span></span> · <span class='fr'><span>∂<var>d</var></span><span>∂<var>a</var></span></span> · <span class='fr'><span>∂<var>a</var></span><span>∂<var>c</var></span></span> · <span class='fr'><span>∂<var>c</var></span><span>∂<var>w</var></span></span></div><p>Multiply the <b>local slopes</b> along the path. Each node only needs to know its own little multiplier.</p><div class=\"chint\">Start at ∂J/∂J = 1 and work right to left. The forward values must be kept — which is why training uses far more memory than inference.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-14-computation-graph.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A chain of gears. Turn the first one and you want to know how far the last one moves. You don't need to understand the whole machine — just each pair, multiplied together.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">chain rule</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>multiply the slopes along the path</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂J/∂d</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee J by dee d”</td><td>how much the score changes when d changes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">local slope</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one node's own little multiplier</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂J/∂J = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>where the backward walk starts</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A→B is ×2, B→C is ×3, C→D is ×0.5. So A→D is 2×3×0.5 = 3. That's the whole idea.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-mse-vs-bce", "kind": "trap", "front": "Why not use <b>mean squared error</b> for classification?", "back": "<p>It runs, and it trains badly. The gradient is <b>tiny exactly where the model is confidently wrong</b> — which is where you most need a big correction.</p><div class=\"chint\">Same reason as Course 1 Week 3: the sigmoid and the log loss are a matched pair whose derivatives cancel cleanly.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-02-training-details.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Using the Course 1 scoring rule here doesn't crash — it just trains badly, and for a sneaky reason: the learning signal goes quiet exactly where you need it loudest.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">MSE</td><td class=\"sa\">“em-ess-ee”</td><td>Mean Squared Error — the Course 1 scoring rule</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">gradient</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the learning signal</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">confidently wrong</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>very sure, and very mistaken. Where the biggest fix is needed</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The sigmoid and the log loss are a matched pair. Split them up and the tidiness disappears.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w2-drill-softmax", "kind": "drill", "front": "z = [1, 2, 3, 4]. Compute the softmax output for the largest score, a&#8324;.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>e</var><sup>1</sup>+<var>e</var><sup>2</sup>+<var>e</var><sup>3</sup>+<var>e</var><sup>4</sup> = 2.72+7.39+20.09+54.60 = 84.79</div><div class=\"ceq\"><var>a</var><sub>4</sub> = <var>e</var><sup>4</sup> / 84.79 = 54.60 / 84.79 = <b>0.644</b></div><div class=\"chint\">z=4 is only 4× z=1, but its probability comes out 20× larger — exponentiating exaggerates the gap. The other three probabilities are 0.032, 0.087, 0.237.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w2-07-softmax.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Neural Network Training", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Turn four raw scores into four probabilities that add up to 1. Bigger raw scores get disproportionately bigger shares — exponentiating stretches the gaps apart before sharing out the total.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">e</td><td class=\"sa\">“e”</td><td>Euler's number, about 2.718 — the base every score gets raised to</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a₄</td><td class=\"sa\">“a four”</td><td>the share of probability given to the 4th, largest score</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Score 4 was only 4 times bigger than score 1, but ends up with about 20 times more of the probability — softmax rewards the leader more than it looks like it should.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-diagnostic", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>central diagnostic</b>: which number tells you about bias, and which about variance?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>J<sub>train</sub> high</b> → <b>high bias</b> (underfitting)</li><li><b>J<sub>cv</sub> ≫ J<sub>train</sub></b> → <b>high variance</b> (overfitting)</li><li>both can be true at once</li></ul><div class=\"chint\"><b>J<sub>train</sub> tells you about bias. The gap tells you about variance.</b> One sentence, and it is most of the diagnostic value of the whole week.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-04-bias-and-variance.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two numbers, and each one points at a different disease. Reading them correctly is most of what this week is worth.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">J_train</td><td class=\"sa\">“J train”</td><td>how wrong it is on the examples it has already studied</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">J_cv</td><td class=\"sa\">“J see-vee”</td><td>how wrong it is on examples it has never seen</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bias</td><td class=\"sa\">“BY-ass”</td><td>too simple. Wrong even on the studied examples</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">variance</td><td class=\"sa\">“VAIR-ee-ance”</td><td>memorised. Fine on studied ones, bad on new ones</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the gap</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>J_cv minus J_train</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">J_train tells you about bias. The GAP tells you about variance. That one sentence is the whole diagnostic.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-three-sets", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why <b>three</b> data splits and not two?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>train (60%)</b> — fits w and b</li><li><b>cross-validation (20%)</b> — chooses the model: degree, λ, architecture, features</li><li><b>test (20%)</b> — read <b>once</b>, at the very end</li></ul><p>Any number you use to <b>make a decision</b> becomes optimistically biased. Selecting on the test set converts it into a second cv set.</p><div class=\"chint\">Report J<sub>test</sub>, never J<sub>cv</sub>, as your final number.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-03-model-selection.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Split your data three ways, not two. The reason is subtle: the moment you use a number to make a CHOICE, that number stops being an honest measurement.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">training set</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>used to set the dials</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cross-validation set</td><td class=\"sa\">“cross-val-i-DAY-shun”</td><td>used to CHOOSE between models. Also called the dev set</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">test set</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>opened once, at the very end, and never again</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">biased</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>flattering, because you picked whatever happened to suit it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ</td><td class=\"sa\">lambda</td><td>regularisation strength — how hard weights are pushed towards zero</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Study from a textbook, practise on mock papers, sit the real exam once. Marking yourself on the mocks and calling it your grade would be cheating.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-fix-table", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "Six things to try. Which fix <b>high variance</b> and which fix <b>high bias</b>?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>High variance</b><ul class='cbul'><li>get more training examples</li><li>try a smaller set of features</li><li>try increasing λ</li></ul></div><div><b>High bias</b><ul class='cbul'><li>try getting additional features</li><li>try adding polynomial features</li><li>try decreasing λ</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Every variance fix makes the model <b>less</b> flexible; every bias fix makes it <b>more</b> flexible. You can regenerate the table from that sentence alone.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-08-what-to-try-revisited.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Six things people try when a model is bad. Three help one disease and three help the other — and trying the wrong three wastes weeks.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">high variance fixes</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>all make the model LESS flexible</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">high bias fixes</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>all make the model MORE flexible</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ</td><td class=\"sa\">“lambda”</td><td>the stiffness dial. Up = stiffer, down = floppier</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">polynomial features</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>adding x², x³ so the model can bend</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">You never have to memorise which is which. Ask: does this make the model bendier or stiffer?</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-more-data", "kind": "trap", "front": "When does <b>collecting more data</b> not help?", "back": "<p>When you have <b>high bias</b>. More data is on the variance list <em>only</em>.</p><div class=\"chint\">It is also the most expensive item on the list. Teams routinely spend a quarter collecting data for a model that was never going to benefit — which is what the diagnostic prevents.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-08-what-to-try-revisited.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The most expensive thing on the list, and it only cures one of the two diseases. Teams routinely spend months collecting data for a model that was never going to benefit.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">high bias</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>cannot even fit what it already has. More of the same changes nothing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">high variance</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>memorising. More data leaves no room to memorise</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Buying more textbooks doesn't help a student who has stopped being able to learn from books.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-baseline", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why is “J<sub>train</sub> = 10.8%” meaningless on its own, and what are the <b>two gaps</b>?", "back": "<p>High compared to what? If humans score 10.6% on the same audio, 10.8% is nearly perfect.</p><ul class='cbul'><li><b>baseline → J<sub>train</sub></b> = <b>avoidable bias</b></li><li><b>J<sub>train</sub> → J<sub>cv</sub></b> = <b>variance</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Chasing error below the noise floor of your own labels is a way to spend a year overfitting.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-06-baseline-performance.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>“10.8% error” means nothing on its own. High compared to what? If people get 10.6% on the same task, you are nearly perfect.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">baseline</td><td class=\"sa\">“BASE-line”</td><td>what is realistically achievable — often human performance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">avoidable bias</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the gap between the baseline and your training score. Worth chasing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">variance</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the gap between your training score and your unseen score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Bayes error</td><td class=\"sa\">“BAYZ error”</td><td>the best ANY model could ever do. Some examples are just ambiguous</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Getting 89% on a test is brilliant or terrible depending entirely on what everyone else got.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-learning-curves", "kind": "concept", "front": "How do you read a <b>learning curve</b> to decide whether more data will help?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>High bias</b><ul class='cbul'><li>both curves <b>flatten early</b></li><li>well above the baseline</li><li>small gap</li><li>→ more data will <b>not</b> help</li></ul></div><div><b>High variance</b><ul class='cbul'><li>J<sub>train</sub> below the baseline</li><li><b>large gap</b></li><li>J<sub>cv</sub> still falling at the right edge</li><li>→ more data <b>will</b> help</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">J<sub>train</sub> <b>rises</b> with m — fitting 1000 points is harder than fitting 3. That is expected, not a bug.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-07-learning-curves.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Draw how wrong you are against how many examples you have. The SHAPE of those two lines tells you whether buying more data is worth it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">learning curve</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>error plotted against dataset size</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">plateau</td><td class=\"sa\">“pla-TOE”</td><td>gone flat and stopped improving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the gap</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>distance between the two curves</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">J_train rising</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>normal! Fitting 1000 points is harder than fitting 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">If both lines have already met and flattened, more data changes nothing. That is a very cheap way to say no to an expensive project.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-nn-recipe", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>neural network recipe</b> — two questions, two fixes.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>Does it do well on the <b>training</b> set? (vs the baseline)</li><li>No → <b>bigger network</b>. Go back to 1.</li><li>Yes → does it do well on the <b>cv</b> set?</li><li>No → <b>more data</b> (or more regularisation). Go back to 1.</li><li>Yes → done.</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">A larger network with proper regularisation is almost never worse than a smaller one. So “too big” is a compute problem, not an accuracy problem.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-09-bias-variance-neural-networks.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A flowchart with two questions in it. Ask them in order and it tells you what to do next, every time.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">does well on training?</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>if no, it is too small. Make it bigger</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">does well on cv?</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>if no, it is memorising. Get more data or more stiffness</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">regularisation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the stiffness setting that stops a big network memorising</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A bigger network with proper stiffness is almost never worse than a small one — so “too big” is a bill, not a mistake.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-error-analysis", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "How do you actually do <b>error analysis</b>?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>take the <b>misclassified cross-validation</b> examples</li><li>sample ~100 if there are more</li><li>read them, inventing overlapping categories as you go</li><li><b>count</b> each category</li><li>work on the biggest one that is also tractable</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">The classic outcome: the team has argued for two weeks about a category that turns out to be 3 of 100. Meanwhile a 21-of-100 category had nobody on it.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-11-error-analysis.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The highest-value hour in most projects, and it involves no maths at all. Read the mistakes. Sort them into piles. Count the piles.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">error analysis</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>reading your model's mistakes by hand</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">misclassified</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the ones it got wrong</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cross-validation set</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>use these, NOT the test set</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">~100 examples</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>enough to tell 20% from 3%. You are estimating, not auditing</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The usual outcome: the thing everyone has been arguing about turns out to be 3 mistakes out of 100, while a 21-out-of-100 pile had nobody working on it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-augmentation", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is the one rule for <b>data augmentation</b>?", "back": "<p>The distortion must be <b>representative of what actually happens in real data</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>speech → add café noise, car noise, a bad phone line ✓</li><li>clean scanned text → random per-pixel noise ✗ — it will never meet that</li><li>handwritten digits → mirror flipping ✗ — a mirrored 2 is not a 2</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Augment the <b>training set only</b>. Never the cv or test set.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-12-adding-data.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Turning one example into ten by bending it — but only in ways that could genuinely happen in real life.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">augmentation</td><td class=\"sa\">“aug-men-TAY-shun”</td><td>making new examples by distorting the ones you have</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">representative</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the distortion must be something the real world actually does</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">training set only</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>never augment the data you score yourself on</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Adding café noise to speech makes sense — you will meet noisy cafés. Adding random static to clean scans does not — you never will.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-transfer", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "<b>Transfer learning</b> — the two options and when to use each.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>1 · Freeze</b>train <b>only the new output layer</b><br>very small dataset (tens–hundreds)</div><div><b>2 · Fine-tune</b>train <b>all</b> parameters, starting from theirs<br>larger dataset (thousands+)</div></div><p>Replace only the <b>last</b> layer — it is the only genuinely task-specific part.</p><div class=\"chint\">When you unfreeze, drop the learning rate by 10–100×. A normal α destroys the pre-trained weights in the first few steps. Also: the <b>input type must match</b>.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-13-transfer-learning.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Borrow a network somebody else spent a fortune training. Keep everything except the last layer, bolt your own last layer on, and train just that on your 50 examples.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">transfer learning</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>reusing someone else's trained network for your own task</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">pre-training</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>their expensive step, on a million general images</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">fine-tuning</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>your cheap step, on your small specific set</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">freeze</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>mark layers as “don't change these”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">output layer</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the only part that genuinely depends on YOUR classes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">alpha</td><td>learning rate — the step size for gradient descent</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Early layers learn what edges and textures are — and edges are edges whether the photo is a cat or a chest X-ray.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-precision-recall", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Precision</b> and <b>recall</b> — and the trick for never mixing them up.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">precision = <span class='fr'><span>TP</span><span>TP + FP</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; recall = <span class='fr'><span>TP</span><span>TP + FN</span></span></div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>precision</b> — of those we <b>flagged</b>, how many were real?</li><li><b>recall</b> — of those that were <b>real</b>, how many did we catch?</li></ul><div class=\"chint\"><b>Precision's denominator is what you predicted. Recall's is what was true.</b> Hold onto that one difference.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-16-skewed-datasets.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two different questions about the same mistakes, and everyone mixes them up. The trick is to remember what is on the BOTTOM of each fraction.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">TP</td><td class=\"sa\">“true positive”</td><td>you said yes and you were right</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">FP</td><td class=\"sa\">“false positive”</td><td>you said yes and you were wrong — a false alarm</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">FN</td><td class=\"sa\">“false negative”</td><td>you said no and you were wrong — you missed one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">precision</td><td class=\"sa\">“pre-SI-zhun”</td><td>of everything you FLAGGED, how much was real?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">recall</td><td class=\"sa\">“re-CALL”</td><td>of everything that WAS real, how much did you catch?</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Precision's bottom is what you predicted. Recall's bottom is what was true. Hold onto that one difference and you'll never mix them up.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-accuracy-trap", "kind": "trap", "front": "Why is <b>accuracy</b> useless on a 0.5%-positive dataset?", "back": "<p><code>print(\"healthy\")</code> scores <b>99.5% accuracy</b> and catches nobody.</p><p>On skewed data, accuracy is essentially a measurement of how rare the positive class is.</p><div class=\"chint\">Report precision, recall and F1. Never accuracy alone.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-16-skewed-datasets.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>When almost everything is “no”, a model that always says “no” scores brilliantly and is completely useless.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">accuracy</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the fraction you got right overall</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">skewed</td><td class=\"sa\">“skyood”</td><td>one class is much rarer than the other</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">99.5% accurate</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>exactly what you get by never flagging anything, if 0.5% are positive</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">On rare-event problems, accuracy is basically measuring how rare the event is.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-f1", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>F1</b> — the formula, and why the <em>harmonic</em> mean specifically.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">F<sub>1</sub> = 2 · <span class='fr'><span>P · R</span><span>P + R</span></span></div><p>Because it sits close to the <b>smaller</b> of the two. P = 1.0 with R = 0.01 gives F1 ≈ 0.02, not 0.5.</p><div class=\"chint\">It refuses to be impressed by a model that is excellent at one half and useless at the other.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-16-skewed-datasets.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One number combining precision and recall — but NOT a plain average, because a plain average lets a useless model look decent.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">F1</td><td class=\"sa\">“eff one”</td><td>a single combined score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">harmonic mean</td><td class=\"sa\">“har-MON-ic”</td><td>an average that leans hard towards the SMALLER number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">P, R</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>precision and recall</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Precision 1.0 and recall 0.01: a plain average says 0.505, which flatters it. F1 says 0.02, which is the truth.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-threshold", "kind": "concept", "front": "Which way do you move the <b>threshold</b>, and who decides?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>raise it</b> (0.9) → precision ↑, recall ↓ — when a false alarm is expensive</li><li><b>lower it</b> (0.15) → recall ↑, precision ↓ — when a miss is much worse</li></ul><p>The model gives a probability. The threshold turns it into an <b>action</b>, and the right one depends on what an action <b>costs</b>.</p><div class=\"chint\">That is a product decision, not a modelling one — and F1 is a poor way to make it, because it weights the two errors equally.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-17-precision-recall-tradeoff.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The model hands you a chance. YOU decide what chance is high enough to act on — and that depends on what acting wrongly costs.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">threshold</td><td class=\"sa\">“THRESH-old”</td><td>the cutoff for saying yes. 0.5 is only a default</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">raise it</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>only shout when very sure → fewer false alarms, more misses</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">lower it</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>shout at anything suspicious → fewer misses, more false alarms</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A missed cancer versus one extra scan is not the same trade as blocking a real customer's card. That is a business decision, not a maths one.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-fairness", "kind": "concept", "front": "Your model is 92.4% accurate overall. What is the next thing to measure?", "back": "<p>Performance <b>per subgroup</b> — accuracy, false positives and false negatives, broken down.</p><p>Aggregate accuracy hides subgroup failure <b>by construction</b>: a group that is 6% of your data can be served terribly with no visible effect on the headline.</p><div class=\"chint\">Removing the sensitive attribute does <b>not</b> make a model fair — postcode, name and purchase history are proxies. You just lose the ability to measure the disparity.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w3-15-fairness-bias-ethics.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One overall score can hide a group being served terribly, purely because that group is small.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">subgroup</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a slice of your users: by age, region, skin tone, language</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">aggregate</td><td class=\"sa\">“AG-ri-git”</td><td>the single overall number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">disparity</td><td class=\"sa\">“dis-PARR-ity”</td><td>one group doing much worse than another</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">proxy</td><td class=\"sa\">“PROX-ee”</td><td>a feature that secretly stands in for another — postcode for income</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">If a group is 6% of your data you can fail them completely and the headline number barely twitches.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w3-leakage", "kind": "trap", "front": "Three ways a <b>train/test split</b> can silently lie to you.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>splitting after sorting</b> — the halves are different populations. Shuffle first (unless it is a time series, where you must split by time)</li><li><b>duplicate entities</b> — the same house or patient in both sets</li><li><b>scaling before splitting</b> — μ and σ computed over everything leaks test info</li></ul>", "lesson": "c2/w3-02-evaluating-a-model.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Advice for Applying ML", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Three ways your test score can be a lie, all of which leave you feeling great until you deploy.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">leakage</td><td class=\"sa\">“LEEK-ij”</td><td>test information sneaking into training</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">shuffle</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>mix the rows before splitting, so both halves look alike</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">time series</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>data in time order. Here you must NOT shuffle — split by date</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">de-duplicate</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>make sure the same house/patient isn't in both halves</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">sigma</td><td>standard deviation — how spread out something is</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like revising from a paper that turns out to contain the exam questions — the mark tells you nothing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-tree-decisions", "kind": "concept", "front": "What are the <b>two decisions</b> that define the tree-learning algorithm?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>which feature to split on?</b> → the one that makes the two groups purest (maximum information gain)</li><li><b>when to stop?</b> → node is 100% one class · max depth reached · gain too small · too few examples</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Every stopping rule exists for one reason: keeping the tree <b>small</b>. Depth is the tree's equivalent of polynomial degree.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-02-learning-process.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Building a tree needs only two answers, over and over: what question to ask next, and when to stop asking.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">split</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>asking a question and dividing the examples into two groups</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">pure</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a group that is all one class</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">max depth</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how many questions deep you allow the tree to go</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">leaf</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a node that stops asking and just gives an answer</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Every stopping rule exists for one reason: keeping the tree small. Depth is the tree's version of polynomial degree.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-entropy", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Entropy</b> — the formula and the two endpoints.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>H</var>(<var>p</var>) = −<var>p</var> log<sub>2</sub>(<var>p</var>) − (1 − <var>p</var>) log<sub>2</sub>(1 − <var>p</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>H = 0</b> — completely pure (p = 0 or p = 1)</li><li><b>H = 1</b> — maximum mess (p = 0.5), exactly one bit</li><li><b>symmetric</b>: H(0.8) = H(0.2) = 0.72</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">“If I reach into this bag, how surprised will I be?” Base 2 gives bits, so a 50/50 bag is one coin flip of uncertainty.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-03-measuring-purity.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A number for “how mixed up is this pile?”. All one thing → 0. Half and half → 1. And it doesn't care WHICH thing dominates.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">entropy</td><td class=\"sa\">“EN-tro-pee”</td><td>how messy or surprising a group is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">H(p)</td><td class=\"sa\">“H of p”</td><td>the messiness score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the fraction of the group that is the positive class</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log₂</td><td class=\"sa\">“log base two”</td><td>base 2 means the answer comes out in “bits” — coin flips of surprise</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">H = 0</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>perfectly pure. No surprise at all</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">H = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>exactly 50/50. Maximum surprise</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">H(0.8) and H(0.2) are both 0.72 — it only measures how lopsided the mix is, not which way.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-infogain", "kind": "formula", "front": "<b>Information gain</b> — and the part everyone forgets.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">gain = <var>H</var>(<var>p</var><sub>root</sub>) − ( <b><var>w</var><sup>left</sup></b><var>H</var>(<var>p</var><sup>left</sup>) + <b><var>w</var><sup>right</sup></b><var>H</var>(<var>p</var><sup>right</sup>) )</div><p>The <b>weights</b> are the fraction of examples going each way.</p><div class=\"chint\">Without them, splitting off a single example into its own perfectly pure branch looks fantastic every time. This is the most common bug in the assignment.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-04-information-gain.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>How much mess did this question clear up? Messiness before, minus messiness after — but weighted by how many examples went each way.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">information gain</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how much messiness the question removed</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">H(root)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the mess before you asked</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">w_left, w_right</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the FRACTION of examples going each way. The bit people forget</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">weighted</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a tidy pile of 1 is worth less than a tidy pile of 9</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Without the weights, splitting off one lonely example into its own perfect pile looks like a brilliant move every time.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-id-column", "kind": "trap", "front": "A customer-ID feature would score a <b>perfect</b> information gain. Why is that a problem?", "back": "<p>Every leaf holds one example and is perfectly pure — maximum gain. But the rule “if ID = 4471 then cat” tells you nothing about a new customer. Pure memorisation.</p><div class=\"chint\">C4.5 fixes this with <em>gain ratio</em>. The practical fix: never feed an ID column to a tree.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-04-information-gain.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A column with a unique value per row scores a perfect result and teaches the model absolutely nothing.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">cardinality</td><td class=\"sa\">“car-di-NAL-ity”</td><td>how many different values a column has</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">perfect purity</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>every leaf holds one example, so every leaf is “pure”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">memorisation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>learning the answers rather than the pattern</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">“If the customer ID is 4471, it's a cat.” Perfectly true, and useless for customer 4472.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-continuous", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "How does a tree split on a <b>continuous</b> feature like weight?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>sort the examples by that feature</li><li>consider each midpoint between consecutive distinct values (<b>m − 1</b> candidates)</li><li>compute the information gain for each</li><li>keep the best threshold, and let that be the feature's score</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">This is also why trees need <b>no feature scaling</b> — they only ever <em>compare</em> values, never add them.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-07-continuous-features.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Weight isn't “pointy or floppy” — it's a number. So the question becomes “is it under some cutoff?”, and the algorithm just tries every sensible cutoff.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">continuous</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-TIN-you-us”</td><td>a number that can take any value, not a category</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">threshold</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the cutoff point for the question</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">midpoint</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>halfway between two neighbouring values. The only cutoffs worth trying</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">m − 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>with m examples there are only that many useful cutoffs</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">This is also why trees never need feature scaling: they only ever COMPARE numbers, never add them.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-regtree", "kind": "concept", "front": "What single substitution turns a classification tree into a <b>regression</b> tree?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>Classification</b>entropy → split by <b>information gain</b><br>leaf predicts the <b>majority class</b></div><div><b>Regression</b><b>variance</b> → split by <b>variance reduction</b><br>leaf predicts the <b>mean</b></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Everything else — recursion, stopping rules, one-hot, thresholds — is identical. Note a regression tree predicts a <b>step function</b> and cannot extrapolate.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-08-regression-trees.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Same tree, different question: predict a number instead of a category. One word gets swapped and everything else stays.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">regression tree</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a tree that predicts a number</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">variance</td><td class=\"sa\">“VAIR-ee-ance”</td><td>how spread out a group of numbers is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">variance reduction</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the number version of information gain</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">mean</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the plain average. What each leaf predicts</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">step function</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>flat within each leaf, jumping at the boundaries</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Entropy asks “are these all the same LABEL?”. Variance asks “are these all the same NUMBER?”. Same job, different data type.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-onehot", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why <b>one-hot encode</b> instead of mapping {red, green, blue} to {0, 1, 2}?", "back": "<p>Because ordinal encoding tells the model blue is <b>bigger</b> than red, and twice green. For unordered categories that is a lie.</p><div class=\"chint\">For genuinely <em>ordered</em> categories (S &lt; M &lt; L) ordinal encoding is correct and better — one-hot throws the order away.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-06-one-hot-encoding.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You cannot number unordered categories, because numbering implies an order that isn't there. So give each category its own yes/no column instead.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">one-hot</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>exactly ONE column is 1 (“hot”), the rest are 0</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">categorical</td><td class=\"sa\">“cat-e-GOR-ic-al”</td><td>a column of labels, not numbers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ordinal</td><td class=\"sa\">“OR-din-al”</td><td>categories that DO have an order — small, medium, large</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">dummy variables</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the statisticians' name for the same trick</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Coding red=0, green=1, blue=2 tells the model blue is bigger than red and twice green. It is not.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-why-ensemble", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why is a single decision tree <b>high variance</b>?", "back": "<p>The root split is a single argmax. If two features score 0.281 and 0.278, a tiny change in the data flips which wins — and <b>everything below the root</b> is then built on a different foundation.</p><div class=\"chint\">Averaging only helps if the models make <b>different</b> mistakes. Three identical trees vote identically and gain you nothing.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-09-using-multiple-trees.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One tree is fragile. The very first question is picked by a razor-thin margin, and if it flips, every question underneath it changes too.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">high variance</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>swap one example and you get a very different model</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">root split</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the first question. Everything below depends on it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">argmax</td><td class=\"sa\">“arg max”</td><td>“whichever scores highest” — even by 0.003</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ensemble</td><td class=\"sa\">“on-SOM-bull”</td><td>several models voting together</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Averaging only helps if the models make DIFFERENT mistakes. Three identical trees vote identically.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-bootstrap", "kind": "number", "front": "In a <b>bootstrap sample</b> of size m drawn with replacement, what fraction of the distinct originals appears?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">1 − (1 − 1/<var>m</var>)<sup><var>m</var></sup> → 1 − 1/<var>e</var> ≈ <b>0.632</b></div><p>About <b>63%</b> appear; the other ~37% are “<b>out-of-bag</b>” and make a free validation set for that tree.</p><div class=\"chint\"><code>replace=True</code> is the whole trick. Without it you get a permutation of the same data and every tree is identical.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-10-sampling-with-replacement.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Put ten marbles in a bag. Draw one, write it down, PUT IT BACK, repeat ten times. You get ten marbles — but not the same ten. Some appear twice; some never come out.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">bootstrap</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>sampling with replacement to fake a fresh dataset</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">with replacement</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>put it back each time, so it can be drawn again</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">63%</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>roughly how many of the originals show up in one bag</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">out-of-bag</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the ~37% left out. A free test set for that tree</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bagging</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>Bootstrap AGGregating: do this many times and combine</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Without putting it back, you'd just draw all ten every time and every tree would be identical.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-random-forest", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>random forest</b> algorithm — and the one thing that makes it more than bagging.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>for b = 1…B: draw a bootstrap sample</li><li>train a tree on it — but at <b>every node</b>, choose the split from a random subset of <b>k ≈ √n</b> features</li><li>predict by majority vote (or average, for regression)</li></ol><p>The feature subsampling is the addition. Without it a dominant feature wins the root in nearly every bag and the trees correlate.</p><div class=\"chint\">More trees never hurts accuracy — only compute. Past ~100 the gains flatten.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-11-random-forest.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Many trees, each grown on its own bag of data AND allowed to consider only a random handful of questions at each step. That second restriction is what makes it work.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">random forest</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>many trees, voting</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">B</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how many trees. 100 is a fine default</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">k ≈ √n</td><td class=\"sa\">“square root of n”</td><td>how many features each split may choose from</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">feature bagging</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>randomising the COLUMNS as well as the rows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">majority vote</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>whichever answer most trees gave</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Without the feature restriction, one strong column wins the first question in nearly every tree and they all end up alike.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-boosting", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>Random forest</b> vs <b>boosting</b>.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>Random forest</b><ul class='cbul'><li>trees built <b>independently</b>, in parallel</li><li>sampling is <b>uniformly random</b></li><li>deep, fully grown trees</li><li>rarely overfits</li><li>almost no tuning</li></ul></div><div><b>Boosting / XGBoost</b><ul class='cbul'><li>trees built <b>sequentially</b>, each fixing the last</li><li>sampling <b>focuses on current errors</b></li><li><b>shallow</b> trees (depth 3–6)</li><li><b>can</b> overfit — needs early stopping</li><li>learning rate matters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Boosting is one student re-doing only the questions they got wrong. It usually wins.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-12-xgboost.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Random forest: a hundred students each revising a random chunk. Boosting: one student who marks their own test and revises only the questions they got wrong.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">boosting</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>trees built one after another, each fixing the last one's mistakes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sequential</td><td class=\"sa\">“se-QUEN-shal”</td><td>in order, one at a time. Cannot be done in parallel</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">XGBoost</td><td class=\"sa\">“ex-gee-boost”</td><td>eXtreme Gradient Boosting — a very fast implementation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">early stopping</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>stop adding trees when the validation score stops improving</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">It's a far more efficient use of effort, and it's why boosted trees usually win on spreadsheets.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-boost-shallow", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why are boosted trees kept <b>shallow</b> (depth 3–6)?", "back": "<p>Each tree only needs to make a <b>small correction</b>. The ensemble supplies the power. Deep trees would overfit their residuals, and the sequential process amplifies that.</p><div class=\"chint\">Also why boosting cannot parallelise across trees: tree b needs the errors of trees 1…b−1. XGBoost parallelises <em>within</em> each tree instead.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-12-xgboost.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Each tree in a boosted model only has to nudge things slightly. The power comes from having many of them, not from any one being clever.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">shallow</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>only 3 to 6 questions deep</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">residual</td><td class=\"sa\">“re-ZID-you-al”</td><td>what's left over — the error still remaining</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">weak learner</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a deliberately simple model. The building block of boosting</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Deep trees would overfit their leftovers, and the sequential process would amplify that mistake with every round.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-tree-vs-nn", "kind": "concept", "front": "Tabular data vs images — which model, and what is the neural network's decisive advantage?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>tabular / spreadsheet</b> → <b>trees (XGBoost) first</b>. Fast, no scaling needed, readable, still the thing to beat</li><li><b>images, audio, text</b> → <b>neural networks</b>, no contest</li></ul><p>The decisive NN advantage: <b>composability</b>. Everything is differentiable, so you can chain several networks and train the whole stack with one gradient. You cannot do that with trees.</p>", "lesson": "c2/w4-13-trees-vs-neural-networks.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Different tools for different shapes of data — and one property that only neural networks have.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">tabular</td><td class=\"sa\">“TAB-you-lar”</td><td>spreadsheet data: rows and columns of numbers and categories</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">composability</td><td class=\"sa\">“com-POSE-ability”</td><td>being able to bolt models together and train them as one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">differentiable</td><td class=\"sa\">“diff-er-EN-shable”</td><td>you can compute slopes through it, so gradients flow</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Tree splits are yes/no decisions with no useful slope, so you cannot chain trees the way you chain networks. That single property is why deep learning scaled.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c2w4-drill-entropy", "kind": "drill", "front": "A node has 10 examples, 8 of one class. Compute its entropy H.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>p</var> = 8/10 = 0.8</div><div class=\"ceq\"><var>H</var> = −0.8 log&#8322;(0.8) − 0.2 log&#8322;(0.2) = 0.258 + 0.464 = <b>0.722</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Compare to H(0.5) = 1.0 (maximally mixed) and H(1.0) = 0 (pure). 0.722 is fairly pure.</div>", "lesson": "c2/w4-03-measuring-purity.html", "extra": "", "course": "C2", "week": "c2w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "Decision Trees", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Ask: how mixed is this group? All-one-class is perfectly tidy (entropy 0). A dead-even 50/50 mix is the messiest it can be (entropy 1). 8 cats out of 10 sits closer to tidy than messy.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">p</td><td class=\"sa\">“pee”</td><td>the fraction belonging to one class — here, 0.8 are cats</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">H</td><td class=\"sa\">“aitch”</td><td>the entropy — the messiness score, from 0 to 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log₂</td><td class=\"sa\">“log base two”</td><td>a log using 2 instead of 10 — keeps the answer between 0 and 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Think of it as a measure of surprise: guessing the class of a random animal from this group is fairly easy (low surprise), because most of them are cats.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-kmeans-steps", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>K-means</b> algorithm — two steps, and what each holds fixed.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>assign</b>: c<sup>(i)</sup> := argmin<sub>k</sub> ‖x<sup>(i)</sup> − μ<sub>k</sub>‖² &nbsp;(centroids fixed)</li><li><b>move</b>: μ<sub>k</sub> := the mean of the points assigned to k &nbsp;(assignments fixed)</li></ol><p>Repeat until nothing changes.</p><div class=\"chint\">Neither step can ever <em>increase</em> J. That is the whole proof of convergence — it is coordinate descent on one cost.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-03-kmeans-algorithm.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Drop a few flags on the playground. Everyone runs to their nearest flag. Each flag then moves to the middle of its own crowd. Repeat until nobody moves.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">K</td><td class=\"sa\">“kay”</td><td>how many groups you want. YOU choose this in advance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">centroid</td><td class=\"sa\">“SEN-troid”</td><td>the middle of a group. Drawn as an ✕</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ_k</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew kay”</td><td>the position of centroid number k</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">c<sup>(i)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“c, example i”</td><td>which group example i currently belongs to</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">argmin</td><td class=\"sa\">“arg min”</td><td>“whichever gives the smallest” — the INDEX, not the value</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖ ‖²</td><td class=\"sa\">“squared distance”</td><td>how far apart, squared. Squaring avoids a square root</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Neither step can ever make things worse, which is why it always settles down.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-distortion", "kind": "formula", "front": "The K-means <b>cost</b> (distortion) — and the debugging rule it gives you.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>J</var> = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span><var>m</var></span></span> <span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>i</var></sub> ‖ <var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> − <var>μ</var><sub><var>c</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup></sub> ‖<sup>2</sup></div><p><b>J can never increase.</b> If your implementation ever shows it rising, you have a bug — usually updating centroids before finishing the assignments.</p><div class=\"chint\">Why the mean specifically? Because the mean is the <em>exact</em> minimiser of a sum of squared distances. Squared distance and “move to the mean” are two halves of one decision.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-04-kmeans-cost.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One score for how tight your groups are: the average squared distance from each point to its own flag. Smaller is tighter.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">J</td><td class=\"sa\">“jay”</td><td>the distortion — total tightness score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">distortion</td><td class=\"sa\">“dis-TOR-shun”</td><td>just the name for this score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ_c<sup>(i)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“the centroid that point i belongs to”</td><td>read it inside out: c<sup>(i)</sup> says which, μ says where</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">mean</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the plain average. Exactly the point that minimises squared distance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖</td><td class=\"sa\">the norm of x</td><td>the <b>length</b> of a vector. Double bars</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">If your code ever shows J going UP, you have a bug — usually moving flags before finishing the assignments.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-init", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "K-means can land in a <b>local optimum</b>. What is the fix?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>initialise centroids at K randomly chosen <b>training points</b> (not random coordinates)</li><li>run to convergence</li><li>repeat 50–1000 times</li><li>keep the run with the <b>lowest J</b></li></ol><div class=\"chint\">You already have the tiebreaker for free — no held-out set, no judgement call. k-means++ is the smarter seeding everyone actually uses.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-05-initializing-kmeans.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Where you drop the flags decides where you end up. So drop them somewhere else and try again, fifty times, then keep whichever attempt scored best.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">local optimum</td><td class=\"sa\">“OP-ti-mum”</td><td>a settled answer that isn't the best possible one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">initialisation</td><td class=\"sa\">“in-ish-al-eye-ZAY-shun”</td><td>where you start the flags</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">training points</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>start ON real data points, not at random empty coordinates</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">k-means++</td><td class=\"sa\">“k means plus plus”</td><td>a smarter way of seeding that spreads the flags out</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">You already have the tiebreaker for free — just keep the run with the lowest J.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-choose-k", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why can you not choose <b>K</b> by minimising J, and what do you do instead?", "back": "<p>J <b>always</b> falls as K rises. At K = m every point is its own cluster and J = 0.</p><ul class='cbul'><li><b>elbow method</b> — plot J vs K, look for the bend. Often ambiguous; Andrew says he rarely uses it</li><li><b>downstream purpose</b> — evaluate K by how well the clusters serve the actual use</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">T-shirt sizes: K = 3 (S/M/L) is cheaper to manufacture; K = 5 fits better. That is a business decision the data cannot make.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-06-choosing-k.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You cannot pick the number of groups by minimising the score, because more groups ALWAYS scores better. With one group per point the score is zero.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">elbow method</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>plot the score against K and look for a bend. Often there isn't one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">downstream purpose</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>judge K by what you'll actually DO with the groups</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">K = m</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one group per example. Score zero, information zero</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">T-shirts: 3 sizes is cheaper to make, 5 fits better. The data cannot decide that for you.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-gaussian", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>Gaussian</b>, and how you fit it to data.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = <span class='fr'><span>1</span><span>√(2π) <var>σ</var></span></span> <var>e</var><sup>−(<var>x</var>−<var>μ</var>)<sup>2</sup> / 2<var>σ</var><sup>2</sup></sup></div><div class=\"ceq\"><span class=\"lbl\">fitting</span><var>μ</var> = (1/<var>m</var>)<span class='sum'>Σ</span><var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <var>σ</var><sup>2</sup> = (1/<var>m</var>)<span class='sum'>Σ</span>(<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> − <var>μ</var>)<sup>2</sup></div><div class=\"chint\">p(x) is a <b>density</b>, not a probability — it can exceed 1 when σ is small. Only the <em>area</em> is a probability, and it totals 1.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-08-gaussian-distribution.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The bell curve. Measure a thousand people's heights and you get a hill: lots in the middle, few at the edges. Two numbers describe it completely.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew”</td><td>the mean — where the top of the hill sits</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>standard deviation — how WIDE the hill is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ²</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma squared”</td><td>the variance. Sigma is its square root</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“p of x”</td><td>how likely that value is. A density, not a probability</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e^−(…)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>makes the curve drop off fast as you move away from the middle</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">1/(√2π σ)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>just scales it so the total area is exactly 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Small σ = a tall narrow spike, so anything off-centre looks instantly suspicious.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-sigma-ranges", "kind": "number", "front": "What fraction of a Gaussian lies within μ ± 1σ, ± 2σ, ± 3σ?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>μ ± 1σ → <b>68%</b></li><li>μ ± 2σ → <b>95%</b></li><li>μ ± 3σ → <b>99.7%</b></li><li>beyond ± 4σ → 0.006%, about 1 in 15,000</li></ul>", "lesson": "c3/w1-08-gaussian-distribution.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Rules of thumb for how much of a bell curve sits within so many steps of the middle. Worth memorising — they come up constantly.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">1σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“one sigma”</td><td>one standard deviation from the middle → 68% of everything</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">2σ</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>95%. Still counts as normal</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">3σ</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>99.7%. Now it's getting unusual</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">4σ</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>about 1 in 15,000. Worth a look</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A “3-sigma event” means something in the outer 0.3% — that's where the phrase comes from.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-anomaly", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>anomaly detection</b> model, and why the multiplication is the point.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = <span class='sum'>Π</span><sub><var>j</var>=1</sub><sup><var>n</var></sup> <var>p</var>(<var>x<sub>j</sub></var>; <var>μ<sub>j</sub></var>, <var>σ<sub>j</sub></var><sup>2</sup>) &nbsp;→&nbsp; anomaly if <var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) &lt; <var>ε</var></div><p>Being mildly unusual in one way is common. Being mildly unusual in <b>five ways at once</b> multiplies into something very rare.</p><div class=\"chint\">It assumes the features are independent. They usually are not, and it works well anyway. With many features, use <b>log p = Σ log p<sub>j</sub></b> to avoid underflow.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-09-anomaly-detection-algorithm.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Check each measurement separately, then MULTIPLY the results together. Being slightly odd in one way is common; being slightly odd in five ways at once is very rare.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Π</td><td class=\"sa\">“product of”</td><td>like Σ but multiplying instead of adding. Capital pi</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the overall “how normal is this?” score</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ε</td><td class=\"sa\">“epsilon”</td><td>the cutoff. Below this and you flag it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">independent</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the assumption that the features don't affect each other</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">underflow</td><td class=\"sa\">“UNDER-flow”</td><td>so many small numbers multiplied that the answer rounds to zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">sigma</td><td>standard deviation — how spread out something is</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 is tiny — even though no single 0.3 looked alarming.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-anomaly-split", "kind": "concept", "front": "How do you split the data for anomaly detection when you have only ~20 known anomalies?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>train</b>: 6000 normal, <b>0 anomalies</b> — fits μ and σ</li><li><b>cross-validation</b>: 2000 normal + 10 anomalies — chooses ε and the features</li><li><b>test</b>: 2000 normal + 10 anomalies — one honest measurement</li></ul><p>Training stays unsupervised; evaluation borrows just enough supervision to tune ε.</p><div class=\"chint\">Put an anomaly in the training set and the model learns it is normal. Report precision, recall, F1 — never accuracy.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-10-developing-anomaly-detection.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You have thousands of normal examples and maybe twenty broken ones. Not enough to learn from — but plenty to TEST with.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">training set</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>normal examples only. This is where μ and σ come from</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cross-validation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>normal plus a few anomalies. Used to pick ε</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">test set</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>normal plus a few anomalies. Used once, at the end</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">skewed</td><td class=\"sa\">“skyood”</td><td>one class is far rarer than the other</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Put an anomaly in the training set and the model learns it's normal — which defeats the point.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-anomaly-vs-sup", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>Anomaly detection</b> vs <b>supervised learning</b> — the one deciding question.", "back": "<p><b>Do you expect future positive examples to look like the ones you already have?</b></p><div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>No → anomaly detection</b><ul class='cbul'><li>very few positives (0–20)</li><li>many different anomaly types</li><li>future ones may be unlike past ones</li><li>learns what <b>normal</b> looks like</li><li>fraud, manufacturing faults, intrusions</li></ul></div><div><b>Yes → supervised</b><ul class='cbul'><li>many positives (100s+)</li><li>enough examples of each type</li><li>future looks like past</li><li>learns what each <b>class</b> looks like</li><li>spam, weather, known defects</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Fraud is on the anomaly side because fraudsters actively change tactics to evade whatever you deployed last month.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-11-anomaly-vs-supervised.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two approaches that look interchangeable, decided by a single question: will tomorrow's problems look like yesterday's?</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">anomaly detection</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>learn what NORMAL looks like, flag anything else</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">supervised learning</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>learn what each specific class looks like</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">novel failure mode</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a way of breaking nobody has ever seen before</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Spam looks like past spam → supervised. Aircraft engines fail in brand-new ways → anomaly detection. Fraudsters actively change tactics, which is why fraud sits on the anomaly side.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-features-anomaly", "kind": "concept", "front": "Two ideas for choosing features in anomaly detection.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>make each feature roughly Gaussian</b> — plot a histogram, apply log(x + c), √x or x<sup>0.3</sup>, plot again</li><li><b>invent features from your errors</b> — look at the anomaly that slipped through and ask what would have caught it</li></ol><p>The classic: a server with normal CPU <b>and</b> normal network traffic, but an unusual <b>ratio</b>. Add x₃ = CPU / network.</p><div class=\"chint\">Feature choice matters far more here than in supervised learning, because there is no y to tell the algorithm which features to ignore.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-12-choosing-features.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Feature choice matters far more here than in supervised learning, because there's no answer key telling the algorithm which columns to ignore.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Gaussian</td><td class=\"sa\">“GOW-see-an”</td><td>bell-shaped. What the algorithm assumes each column looks like</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">skewed</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>lopsided — a long tail off to one side</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">log(x + c)</td><td class=\"sa\">“log of x plus c”</td><td>a transform that pulls a long tail back into a hill shape</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ratio</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one column divided by another. Often far more revealing than either alone</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√</td><td class=\"sa\">square root</td><td>the number that, times itself, gives this one. √9 = 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The classic: a server with normal CPU and normal network traffic, but a wildly unusual RATIO between them. Neither column alone would ever catch it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w1-drill-gaussian", "kind": "drill", "front": "&mu; = 0, &sigma; = 1, x = 0. Compute p(x) — the standard normal density at its own mean.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>p</var>(0) = 1 / &radic;(2&pi;) &middot; <var>e</var><sup>0</sup> = 1 / 2.507 = <b>0.399</b></div><div class=\"chint\">This is the PEAK of the standard bell curve — the highest density it ever reaches, right at the mean. Move away from &mu; and p(x) only ever gets smaller.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w1-08-gaussian-distribution.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Unsupervised Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The bell curve's height at its own centre — the single most likely value it will ever hand back. Move away from the centre in either direction and the height only ever drops.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">&mu;</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew”</td><td>the centre of the bell curve, its average</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">&sigma;</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>how wide the bell curve is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">p(x)</td><td class=\"sa\">“p of x”</td><td>how likely a value near x is — the bell's height there</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√</td><td class=\"sa\">square root</td><td>the number that, times itself, gives this one. √9 = 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Standing exactly at the middle of a hill is the highest point you can be — walk any direction and you go downhill from there. 0.399 is that hilltop height.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-notation", "kind": "concept", "front": "In a ratings matrix, what is the difference between <b>r(i,j) = 0</b> and <b>y(i,j) = 0</b>?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>r(i,j) = 0</b> — the user never rated it. <b>No information.</b></li><li><b>y(i,j) = 0</b> — they rated it zero stars. <b>Strong negative information.</b></li></ul><p>Every cost function this week sums <b>only where r(i,j) = 1</b>.</p><div class=\"chint\">Treating a question mark as a 0 teaches the model that everything unwatched is bad — and unwatched is 99.99% of the matrix.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-01-making-recommendations.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A blank square in a ratings table means “we don't know”, not “they hated it”. Confusing those two wrecks the model.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">r(i,j)</td><td class=\"sa\">“r of i j”</td><td>1 if this person rated this film at all, 0 if they never did</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">y(i,j)</td><td class=\"sa\">“y of i j”</td><td>the actual rating they gave. Only exists where r = 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">i</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>which film (row)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">j</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>which person (column)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sparse</td><td class=\"sa\">“sparce”</td><td>mostly empty. A real ratings table is 99.99% blank</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Treat blanks as zeros and you teach the model that every unwatched film is terrible — and unwatched is nearly everything.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-collab", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>collaborative filtering</b> cost — and the one thing that makes it collaborative.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>J</var>(<var>w</var>,<var>b</var>,<b><var>x</var></b>) = ½ <span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub>(<var>i</var>,<var>j</var>):<var>r</var>=1</sub> (<var>w</var><sup>(<var>j</var>)</sup>·<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> + <var>b</var><sup>(<var>j</var>)</sup> − <var>y</var><sup>(<var>i,j</var>)</sup>)<sup>2</sup> + <span class='fr'><span><var>λ</var></span><span>2</span></span><span class='sum'>Σ</span><var>w</var><sup>2</sup> + <span class='fr'><span><var>λ</var></span><span>2</span></span><span class='sum'>Σ</span><b><var>x</var></b><sup>2</sup></div><p><b>x is now a parameter too.</b> Gradient descent descends in w, b <em>and</em> x simultaneously.</p><div class=\"chint\">The users teach the algorithm what the movies are like, and the movies teach it what the users are like. Neither was labelled.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-03-collaborative-filtering.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two chicken-and-egg problems solved at once. If I knew what the films were like, I could work out what you like. If I knew what you like, I could work out what the films are like. So guess both, then improve both together.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">w<sup>(j)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“w for person j”</td><td>that person's taste dials</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">x<sup>(i)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“x for film i”</td><td>that film's hidden qualities. NOW ALSO LEARNED</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">b<sup>(j)</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>that person's general generosity with stars</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">r=1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>only sum over squares that actually have a rating in them</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">λ</td><td class=\"sa\">“lambda”</td><td>keeps all the numbers from getting silly</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">collaborative</td><td class=\"sa\">“col-LAB-or-ative”</td><td>everyone's ratings help everyone else's predictions</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Nobody labelled any film as romantic or action. The algorithm works it out from who liked what.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-collab-init", "kind": "trap", "front": "Why can't you initialise w and x to <b>zero</b> in collaborative filtering?", "back": "<p>All the gradients would be symmetric and nothing would ever differentiate. Initialise to <b>small random values</b> — exactly as with neural networks.</p><div class=\"chint\">Also: without λ there are infinitely many equivalent solutions (scale w up and x down). Regularisation is what breaks the tie.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-03-collaborative-filtering.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Start everything at zero and nothing can ever tell itself apart — every dial gets the identical nudge, forever.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">initialise</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the values you start with, before any learning</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">symmetric</td><td class=\"sa\">“sim-ET-ric”</td><td>identical, so they all move together and never differentiate</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">small random values</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the fix. Tiny differences let them specialise</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Also: without λ there are infinitely many equally good answers (scale w up, scale x down). Regularisation is what picks one.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-meannorm", "kind": "concept", "front": "A new user has rated nothing. What does the model predict, and what is the fix?", "back": "<p>Regularisation drives their w to 0, so every prediction is exactly <b>0.0</b> — we recommend nothing, or the worst films.</p><p><b>Mean normalisation:</b> subtract each movie's mean rating before training, add it back when predicting.</p><div class=\"ceq\">prediction = <var>w</var><sup>(<var>j</var>)</sup>·<var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> + <var>b</var><sup>(<var>j</var>)</sup> + <b><var>μ<sub>i</sub></var></b></div><div class=\"chint\">Normalise by <b>row</b> (per movie) — the goal is helping new <em>users</em>. It does not fix the new-<em>movie</em> problem.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-05-mean-normalization.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A brand-new user has rated nothing, so the maths keeps their dials at zero, so every prediction is zero stars. Fix it by making “zero” mean “average” instead of “terrible”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μᵢ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew i”</td><td>the average rating of film i, across everyone who rated it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">mean normalisation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>subtract that average before training, add it back when predicting</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cold start</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the problem of knowing nothing about a new user or item</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">by row</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>per FILM. This helps new users. By column would help new films</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Now a new user is shown the generally well-liked films, which is exactly the right default.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-R-mask", "kind": "code", "front": "In the vectorised collaborative filtering cost, what does <code>* R</code> do?", "back": "<pre><code>j = (tf.matmul(X, tf.transpose(W)) + b - Y) * R\nJ = 0.5 * tf.reduce_sum(j ** 2)</code></pre><p>R is the 0/1 matrix. Multiplying by it <b>zeroes every unrated cell before squaring</b>, so question marks contribute nothing to the cost or the gradient.</p><div class=\"chint\">One character does the job of the “sum only where r(i,j) = 1” notation.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-06-tensorflow-collaborative.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A one-character trick that does the job of “only count the squares that have a rating in them”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">R</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital R”</td><td>a table of 1s and 0s: 1 where a rating exists</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">* R</td><td class=\"sa\">“times R”</td><td>multiply elementwise — zeroes out every blank square</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">elementwise</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>pair up matching positions and multiply each pair</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">before squaring</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>zero it first, so blanks contribute nothing to the score OR the slope</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Anything multiplied by zero is zero, so the blanks quietly disappear from the sum.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-related", "kind": "formula", "front": "How do you find <b>related items</b> from learned features?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">‖ <var>x</var><sup>(<var>k</var>)</sup> − <var>x</var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> ‖<sup>2</sup> = <span class='sum'>Σ</span><sub><var>l</var></sub> ( <var>x<sub>l</sub></var><sup>(<var>k</var>)</sup> − <var>x<sub>l</sub></var><sup>(<var>i</var>)</sup> )<sup>2</sup></div><p>Smallest distance wins. You <b>never need to know what the features mean</b> — the relative geometry is what carries the information.</p><div class=\"chint\">At scale, scanning every item is too slow: production uses approximate nearest-neighbour indexes (FAISS, ScaNN, HNSW). Same technique as vector databases for LLM retrieval.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-07-finding-related-items.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The algorithm gave each film a little list of numbers. You have no idea what they mean — and it doesn't matter. Films with SIMILAR lists turn out to be similar films.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖ ‖²</td><td class=\"sa\">“squared distance”</td><td>how far apart two lists of numbers are</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">nearest neighbour</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the closest few</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">l</td><td class=\"sa\">“ell”</td><td>which position in the list you're comparing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">FAISS / HNSW</td><td class=\"sa\">“face” / “aitch-en-ess-double-you”</td><td>libraries that find nearest neighbours among millions in milliseconds</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">capital sigma</td><td><b>add all of these up</b>. A for loop</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">This is the same technique that powers “vector search” for AI chatbots.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-cf-vs-cbf", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "<b>Collaborative</b> vs <b>content-based</b> filtering.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>Collaborative</b><ul class='cbul'><li>uses <b>ratings from similar users</b></li><li>needs lots of ratings per item</li><li>✗ cold start: new item has no ratings</li><li>✗ cold start: new user is unknown</li><li>✓ finds links nobody described</li></ul></div><div><b>Content-based</b><ul class='cbul'><li>uses <b>features of the user and item</b></li><li>needs good descriptions</li><li>✓ new item has features from day one</li><li>✓ new user has age, location, sign-up survey</li><li>✗ limited to what the features encode</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Real systems use both: content-based to cover cold start, collaborative to catch the patterns nobody thought to describe.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-08-collaborative-vs-content.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two philosophies. One learns from who else liked it; the other from what the thing actually IS. Each fails exactly where the other works.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">collaborative</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>“people like you also liked…”. Knows nothing about the film itself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">content-based</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>“you're 27 and like sci-fi, and this is sci-fi”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cold start</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a brand-new user or item, with no history at all</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">features</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>known facts: age, genre, year, cast</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Real systems run both: content-based covers the newcomers, collaborative catches the patterns nobody thought to write down.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-two-tower", "kind": "concept", "front": "The <b>two-tower</b> architecture — and the one constraint in the whole design.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>v<sub>u</sub></var> = UserNN(<var>x<sub>u</sub></var>) &nbsp;·&nbsp; <var>v<sub>m</sub></var> = ItemNN(<var>x<sub>m</sub></var>) &nbsp;→&nbsp; prediction = <b><var>v<sub>u</sub></var> · <var>v<sub>m</sub></var></b></div><p>Different inputs, different depths, different widths. The <b>only</b> constraint is that both output vectors have the <b>same length</b>.</p><div class=\"chint\">Why the dot product matters: v<sub>m</sub> depends only on the item, so you can precompute every one overnight. Serving becomes one user-tower pass plus a matrix multiply.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-09-deep-content-based.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Build two separate machines. One turns everything about a person into 32 numbers; the other turns everything about a film into 32 numbers. Now they're comparable, so multiply and add.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">tower</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one of the two networks</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">v_u</td><td class=\"sa\">“v sub u”</td><td>the user's 32-number summary</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">v_m</td><td class=\"sa\">“v sub m”</td><td>the film's 32-number summary</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">embedding</td><td class=\"sa\">“em-BED-ing”</td><td>the posh word for “a summary as a list of numbers”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">dot product</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>multiply the pairs and add. Big number = good match</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The two towers can have totally different shapes and inputs. Only their OUTPUT lengths must match.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-retrieval", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "How do you recommend from a catalogue of <b>10 million</b> items?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>retrieval</b> — cheap rules and nearest-neighbour lookups cut it to ~100 candidates. Optimised for <b>recall</b>: do not miss anything good</li><li><b>ranking</b> — run the full two-tower network on just those 100. Optimised for <b>precision</b>: get the order right</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Anything retrieval drops can never be recommended, however good the ranker is. Choose the candidate count by an offline experiment: raise it until the recommendations stop improving.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-10-large-catalogues.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You're choosing a present from a shop with ten million things. You don't examine all ten million — you grab a trolley of plausible ones quickly, then look at those properly.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">retrieval</td><td class=\"sa\">“ri-TREE-val”</td><td>the fast, rough shortlist step</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ranking</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the slow, accurate scoring step</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">candidates</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the shortlist. Usually around 100</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">recall</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>did I keep all the good ones? Retrieval's job</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">precision</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>did I get the ORDER right? Ranking's job</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Anything retrieval throws away can never be recommended, however clever the ranker is.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-ethics", "kind": "concept", "front": "What makes a recommender different from every other ML system, ethically?", "back": "<p>It <b>changes the data it will later be trained on</b>. It shapes the very preferences it claims to be measuring.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>optimise engagement → amplifies outrage, because outrage measurably works</li><li>optimise ad revenue → the <b>more exploitative business can bid more</b>, so it gets shown more</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The maths is neutral. What you point it at is not. Ask what the system is <em>really</em> optimising.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-11-ethics-recommenders.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Most models predict a world they don't affect. A recommender changes what people watch — and then trains on what they watched.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">feedback loop</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the output changes the input, round and round</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">engagement</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>clicks and watch time. Easy to measure, easy to game</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">amplify</td><td class=\"sa\">“AM-pli-fy”</td><td>make more of something appear</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">proxy metric</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>an easy number you measure instead of the thing you actually want</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Tell a system “maximise watch time” and it will find that outrage works — without any malice. It's simply what the instruction meant, taken literally.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-l2norm", "kind": "code", "front": "Why does the two-tower model call <code>tf.linalg.l2_normalize</code> before the dot product?", "back": "<p>It scales each vector to length 1, turning the dot product into a <b>cosine similarity</b> — direction only, bounded in [−1, 1].</p><p>Without it the network can lower the loss by <b>inflating magnitudes</b> rather than learning better directions, and training becomes unstable.</p><div class=\"chint\">Also: this needs the <b>functional API</b>, not Sequential — two inputs meeting in the middle.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-12-tensorflow-content-based.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Scale both summaries to the same length before comparing, so you're comparing DIRECTION rather than size.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">l2_normalize</td><td class=\"sa\">“ell two normalise”</td><td>divide a list by its own length, so its length becomes 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cosine similarity</td><td class=\"sa\">“CO-sine”</td><td>how much two arrows point the same way. Between −1 and 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">magnitude</td><td class=\"sa\">“MAG-ni-tude”</td><td>how long the arrow is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">functional API</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the Keras style you need when a model has two separate inputs</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Without it the network can cheat: instead of learning better directions, it just makes the arrows longer.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-pca", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>PCA</b> algorithm, and what it is optimising.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>mean-normalise</b> every feature (and usually scale them)</li><li>compute the <b>covariance matrix</b></li><li>its <b>eigenvectors</b> are the principal components; sort by eigenvalue</li><li>project: z = x · u</li></ol><p>It finds the axis along which the projections are <b>most spread out</b> — equivalently, the one that loses the least when you squash onto it.</p><div class=\"chint\"><b>Not</b> linear regression: that minimises <em>vertical</em> distance to a label y. PCA minimises <em>perpendicular</em> distance and has no label at all.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-14-pca-algorithm.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Shine a torch at a crowd and look at the shadows on the wall. From one angle everyone overlaps; from another they're nicely spread out. PCA finds the angle that spreads them out most.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">PCA</td><td class=\"sa\">“pee-see-ay”</td><td>Principal Component Analysis</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">principal component</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one of the new axes it finds</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">covariance matrix</td><td class=\"sa\">“co-VAIR-ee-ance”</td><td>a table of how the columns move together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">eigenvector</td><td class=\"sa\">“EYE-gen-vector”</td><td>a direction the data naturally stretches along</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">eigenvalue</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how MUCH it stretches in that direction. Sort by this</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">project</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>drop each point onto the new axis and record how far along it lands</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Spread = information kept. Not the same as linear regression: that measures vertical distance to an answer; PCA measures perpendicular distance and has no answer at all.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w2-pca-use", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is PCA actually <b>for</b>, today?", "back": "<p><b>Visualisation.</b> Squash 50 features to 2 so a human can plot them and look.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>compression — storage is cheap now</li><li>speeding up supervised learning — modern hardware and regularisation handle extra features well, and PCA discards directions <b>without ever seeing y</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">For 2-D plotting specifically, t-SNE and UMAP usually produce more legible structure. PCA stays the choice when you need a fast, linear, invertible transform.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w2-15-pca-in-code.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Recommender Systems", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Honest answer about what this is actually good for these days.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">visualisation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>squash 50 columns to 2 so a human can draw it and look</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">compression</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>storage is cheap now. This mattered more in 1998</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">t-SNE / UMAP</td><td class=\"sa\">“tee-snee” / “YOU-map”</td><td>newer methods that usually make prettier 2-D pictures</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">PCA never sees your answers, so it can happily throw away the one small direction that actually predicts what you care about.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-rl-vs-sup", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "How does reinforcement learning differ from supervised learning?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>you get a <b>reward signal</b>, not the right answer</li><li>feedback is <b>a number, often much later</b></li><li>you learn a <b>policy</b> (state → action), not a mapping x → y</li><li><b>the agent generates its own training data by acting</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">That last row changes everything: a bad early policy produces bad data, which makes learning harder. It is the core difficulty of the field.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-01-what-is-rl.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>How you train a dog. You never show it a thousand photos labelled “correct sitting posture”. You say sit, and when something roughly right happens, it gets a treat.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">reinforcement learning</td><td class=\"sa\">“ree-in-FORCE-ment”</td><td>learning from rewards instead of answers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">reward</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a number the world hands back. Positive good, negative bad</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">policy</td><td class=\"sa\">“POL-i-see”</td><td>the rule being learned: what to do in each situation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">agent</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the thing making decisions. Your program</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">environment</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>everything else — the world it acts in</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The hard part is the delay: the treat comes after the sit, but WHICH of the last twenty things earned it?</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-return", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>discounted return</b>, and what γ encodes.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">Return = <var>R</var><sub>1</sub> + <var>γR</var><sub>2</sub> + <var>γ</var><sup>2</sup><var>R</var><sub>3</sub> + <var>γ</var><sup>3</sup><var>R</var><sub>4</sub> + …</div><ul class='cbul'><li>γ near 1 → a <b>patient</b> agent, happy to wait for a bigger payoff</li><li>γ near 0 → an <b>impatient</b> one that grabs whatever is closest</li><li>R₁ is multiplied by γ⁰ = 1 — the exponent counts <b>steps taken</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">γ is a choice about how much you care about the future, not a fact about the world. Real problems use 0.9–0.999; this course uses 0.5 to keep the arithmetic readable.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-03-the-return.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>£100 today or £100 next year? Today. £100 today or £110 next year? Now it depends how patient you are. γ is the dial for exactly that.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">return</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the total reward, counting the future as worth slightly less</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ</td><td class=\"sa\">“gamma”</td><td>the discount. A number between 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ²</td><td class=\"sa\">“gamma squared”</td><td>two steps away, so discounted twice</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">R₁, R₂</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the reward at step 1, step 2, and so on</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ⁰ = 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the first reward isn't discounted at all</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">γ near 1 = patient, happy to wait. γ near 0 = grabs whatever's closest. It's a choice you make, not a fact about the world.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-mdp", "kind": "concept", "front": "What are the five pieces of an <b>MDP</b>, and what does “Markov” assume?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>S</b> states · <b>A</b> actions · <b>R(s)</b> rewards · <b>γ</b> discount · <b>π(s)</b> policy</li></ul><p><b>Markov:</b> the future depends only on <b>where you are now</b>, not on how you got here.</p><div class=\"chint\">If that is false for your problem, put the missing history <b>into the state</b> — which is exactly why Atari agents stack four consecutive frames.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-05-review-of-key-concepts.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The formal name for this whole setup, and the one assumption hiding inside it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">MDP</td><td class=\"sa\">“em-dee-pee”</td><td>Markov Decision Process. Just a name for the S/A/R/γ/π setup</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">S</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>all the situations you could be in</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">A</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>all the things you could do</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">R(s)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>what each situation is worth</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">π(s)</td><td class=\"sa\">“pi of s”</td><td>your plan: given this situation, do that. Nothing to do with 3.14</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Markov</td><td class=\"sa\">“MAR-koff”</td><td>the future depends only on where you are NOW, not how you got here</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">If that assumption is false, put the missing history INTO the state — which is exactly why game-playing AIs stack four consecutive video frames.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-q", "kind": "concept", "front": "Define <b>Q(s, a)</b> precisely — including the odd bit.", "back": "<p>The return if you start in s, take action a <b>once</b>, and then <b>behave optimally forever after</b>.</p><div class=\"ceq\"><var>π</var>*(<var>s</var>) = argmax<sub><var>a</var></sub> <var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>, <var>a</var>) &nbsp;·&nbsp; <var>V</var>(<var>s</var>) = max<sub><var>a</var></sub> <var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>, <var>a</var>)</div><div class=\"chint\">The first action can be a silly one; everything after is assumed optimal. That odd shape is what makes choosing trivial: compare two numbers, take the bigger.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-06-state-action-value-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Q answers one very specific question: “if I'm here, and I do this ONE thing, and then play perfectly forever after — how much do I get in total?”</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Q(s,a)</td><td class=\"sa\">“Q of s a”</td><td>the value of doing action a in situation s</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">s</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the situation you're in</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the action you're considering</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">π*</td><td class=\"sa\">“pi star”</td><td>the BEST possible plan. The star means optimal</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">argmax_a</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>whichever ACTION gives the biggest Q</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">V(s)</td><td class=\"sa\">“V of s”</td><td>the value of being in situation s at all — the best Q available there</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The odd shape (one free action, then perfect play) is what makes choosing trivial: work out two numbers, take the bigger.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-bellman", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>Bellman equation</b> — and what each half means.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>, <var>a</var>) = <b><var>R</var>(<var>s</var>)</b> + <b><var>γ</var></b> max<sub><var>a</var>′</sub> <var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>′, <var>a</var>′)</div><ul class='cbul'><li><b>R(s)</b> — what you get <b>right now</b></li><li><b>γ max Q(s′, a′)</b> — the best you can do from wherever you land, discounted</li></ul><p>At a terminal state there is no “next”, so Q(s, a) = R(s). That is the base case.</p><div class=\"chint\">Every long journey splits into “this step” plus “the rest”. Every RL algorithm ever written is built on this.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-08-bellman-equation.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Every long journey splits into two bits: what you get right now, and how good tomorrow will be. One line, and every reinforcement learning algorithm ever written is built on it.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">R(s)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>what you collect immediately. The “now” half</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>discount the future half slightly</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">s′</td><td class=\"sa\">“s prime”</td><td>the situation you land in next. Prime always means “next”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">a′</td><td class=\"sa\">“a prime”</td><td>the action you'd take from there</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">max_a′</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>assume you'd pick the best one available</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Q appears on both sides — it defines itself. That sounds circular, but there's exactly one set of numbers that works, and you can find it by repeatedly applying the equation.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-rover-values", "kind": "number", "front": "Mars rover, rewards 100 and 40, γ = 0.5. What are V(1)…V(6) and the optimal policy?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">V = [ <b>100, 50, 25, 12.5, 20, 40</b> ]</div><p>Optimal policy for states 2–5: <b>← ← ← →</b></p><ul class='cbul'><li>Q(4,←) = 0 + 0.5 × V(3) = 0.5 × 25 = <b>12.5</b></li><li>Q(4,→) = 0 + 0.5 × V(5) = 0.5 × 20 = <b>10</b> → so state 4 goes left</li><li>Q(5,→) = 0 + 0.5 × 40 = <b>20</b> beats Q(5,←) = 6.25 → state 5 goes right</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The split at states 4/5 is the interesting part — and moving γ moves the dividing line.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-06-state-action-value-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The little six-square example, worked all the way through. Worth being able to reproduce on paper — everything else in the week is this, scaled up.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">V(s)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how much each square is worth</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">terminal</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>reaching square 1 or 6 ends the trip</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">← ← ← →</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the best plan: squares 2, 3, 4 go left; square 5 goes right</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">0.5 × 25</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one step away from something worth 25, discounted by γ = 0.5</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Square 5 goes right because 40 is one step away, while 100 is four steps away — and with γ = 0.5, four steps costs you a lot.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-stochastic", "kind": "formula", "front": "What changes in the Bellman equation for a <b>random</b> environment?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>, <var>a</var>) = <var>R</var>(<var>s</var>) + <var>γ</var> <b>E</b>[ max<sub><var>a</var>′</sub> <var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>′, <var>a</var>′) ]</div><p>One <b>E</b> — an expectation, averaging over every possible next state weighted by its probability. Nothing else changes.</p><div class=\"chint\">Every value falls as the misstep probability rises: a world you cannot fully control is genuinely worth less to be in.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-09-stochastic-environments.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>What if you say “go left” and the ground is icy, so 10% of the time you slide right instead? You stop asking “what will I get” and start asking “what will I get ON AVERAGE”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">stochastic</td><td class=\"sa\">“sto-KAS-tik”</td><td>random. Just a scary word for “sometimes it goes wrong”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">E[…]</td><td class=\"sa\">“the expected value of”</td><td>the average over everything that could happen, weighted by how likely each is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">expected return</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>not what happens once — what happens on average over many tries</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ</td><td class=\"sa\">gamma</td><td>discount factor — how much a future reward is worth now</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Every value drops when the world is unreliable. That's correct: a world you can't control is genuinely worth less to be in.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-continuous", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why can't you store Q in a <b>table</b> for a continuous state space?", "back": "<p>Infinitely many states. Discretised, a 6-D state at 100 buckets per dimension is 100⁶ = a trillion cells — the <b>curse of dimensionality</b>.</p><p>So you <b>compute</b> Q from a vector of numbers instead. That function is a neural network.</p><div class=\"chint\">A network also <b>generalises</b>: it learns that nearby states have similar Q. A table has no notion of “nearby”.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-10-continuous-state-spaces.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Six squares you can write on a piece of paper. A truck's position, speed and angle — you could never list them all, because there are infinitely many.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">continuous</td><td class=\"sa\">“con-TIN-you-us”</td><td>made of real numbers, not a short list of options</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ℝ<sup>n</sup></td><td class=\"sa\">“R to the n”</td><td>a list of n real numbers. Just means “n measurements”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">curse of dimensionality</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>chop each of 6 measurements into 100 buckets and you need a trillion boxes</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">function approximation</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>COMPUTE the value instead of looking it up in a table</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">generalise</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>guess sensibly about situations you've never actually been in</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A table has no idea that 12.34 metres and 12.35 metres are nearly the same thing. A network does.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-dqn", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The <b>deep Q-learning</b> (DQN) algorithm.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>initialise the network with <b>random</b> weights</li><li>act in the environment; store each <b>(s, a, R(s), s′)</b> tuple</li><li>keep the 10,000 most recent — the <b>replay buffer</b></li><li>build a training set: <b>x = (s, a)</b>, <b>y = R(s) + γ max<sub>a′</sub> Q(s′, a′)</b></li><li>train Q<sub>new</sub> so Q<sub>new</sub>(x) ≈ y — ordinary supervised learning</li><li>set Q = Q<sub>new</sub>, and repeat</li></ol><div class=\"chint\">Why it converges rather than spiralling: <b>R(s) is real</b>. Every target contains one genuine measured reward, and that truth propagates outwards.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-12-learning-the-state-value-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The trick that makes it work: you have no correct answers, so you INVENT them — using the network you're currently training.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">DQN</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee-cue-en”</td><td>Deep Q-Network</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">(s, a, R, s′)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one memory: where I was, what I did, what I got, where I ended up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">replay buffer</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the last 10,000 memories, kept and reused</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">target y</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the made-up “right answer” you train towards</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bootstrapping</td><td class=\"sa\">“BOOT-strapping”</td><td>improving a guess using another of your own guesses</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ</td><td class=\"sa\">gamma</td><td>discount factor — how much a future reward is worth now</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">It doesn't spiral into nonsense because R is REAL. Every made-up target contains one genuine measured reward, and that truth spreads outwards.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-replay", "kind": "concept", "front": "What two problems does the <b>replay buffer</b> solve?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>correlation</b> — consecutive frames are nearly identical, which violates the i.i.d. assumption and makes the network oscillate. Random sampling breaks it</li><li><b>data efficiency</b> — each expensive experience is reused many times</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Removing it was tested in the original DQN paper and performance collapses. It is load-bearing, not an optimisation.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-12-learning-the-state-value-function.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Why keeping a pile of old memories matters more than it looks.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">correlated</td><td class=\"sa\">“COR-e-lay-ted”</td><td>very similar to each other. Consecutive video frames nearly are</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">i.i.d.</td><td class=\"sa\">“eye-eye-dee”</td><td>independent and identically distributed — the “examples should be unrelated” assumption all supervised learning rests on</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">random sampling</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>pulling memories out in a jumbled order, which breaks the correlation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">data efficiency</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>reusing each hard-won experience many times</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">It was tested without the buffer in the original paper and performance collapsed. It's holding the roof up, not decorating it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-arch", "kind": "concept", "front": "What is the <b>improved DQN architecture</b>, and why does it matter?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>✗ naive</b>input: 8 state + 4 one-hot action = 12<br>output: <b>1</b> number<br>→ <b>4</b> forward passes per decision</div><div><b>✓ improved</b>input: <b>8 state only</b><br>output: <b>4</b> numbers, one per action<br>→ <b>1</b> forward pass</div></div><p>max<sub>a′</sub> Q(s′, a′) becomes a max over four numbers you already have.</p><div class=\"chint\">Output activation must be <b>linear</b> — Q values are unbounded reals, not probabilities.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-13-improved-architecture.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A design change that costs nothing and saves three quarters of the work. Move the actions from the input side to the output side.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">one-hot action</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>encoding “which action” as four columns of 0s and one 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">forward pass</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>running the network once to get an answer</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">4 outputs</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one Q value per action, all in one go</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">linear output</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>no squashing. Q values can be −200 or +140, so squashing would ruin them</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Picking an action happens millions of times during training, so a 4× saving on that is enormous.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-epsilon", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>ε-greedy</b> policy, and why explore at all?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">with prob 1 − <var>ε</var>: take argmax<sub><var>a</var></sub> <var>Q</var>(<var>s</var>,<var>a</var>) &nbsp;·&nbsp; with prob <var>ε</var>: pick at <b>random</b></div><p>A randomly-initialised Q might permanently believe “firing the main engine is bad”. If you never try it, you never find out otherwise.</p><div class=\"chint\">Start at ε = 1.0 (all random) and decay towards 0.01. Never to exactly 0 — there may still be states you have not visited.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-14-epsilon-greedy.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>You always order the same dish because you know it's good. But there are forty others and one might be better — and you'll never find out.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">ε</td><td class=\"sa\">“epsilon”</td><td>how often you deliberately ignore your own advice. Usually small</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">greedy</td><td class=\"sa\">“GREE-dee”</td><td>taking the best option you currently know about</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">exploration</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>trying something random, just to see</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">exploitation</td><td class=\"sa\">“ex-ploy-TAY-shun”</td><td>using what you already know</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">decay</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>start at ε = 1 (all random), shrink towards 0.01 as your knowledge improves</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A randomly-started network might believe “firing the engine is bad”. If it never tries, it never learns otherwise.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-soft-update", "kind": "formula", "front": "The <b>soft update</b>, and why DQN needs it when supervised learning does not.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>W</var> := <var>τ</var> <var>W</var><sub>new</sub> + (1 − <var>τ</var>) <var>W</var><sub>old</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; with <var>τ</var> ≈ 0.01</div><p>Because the <b>targets are computed from the network being trained</b>. If Q lurches, every target lurches, and you are chasing something that keeps jumping.</p><div class=\"chint\">In supervised learning the targets y are fixed data. Here they are not — which is the whole reason this refinement exists.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-15-minibatch-soft-updates.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Don't throw the old network away and swap in the new one. Blend them — keep 99% of the old and mix in 1% of the new.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">τ</td><td class=\"sa\">“tau”</td><td>how much of the new network to mix in. About 0.01</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">soft update</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>blending rather than replacing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">target</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the made-up right answer you're training towards</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">W</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the network's weights</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The targets are computed FROM the network being trained. If the network jumps, every target jumps, and you're chasing something that keeps moving.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-reward-design", "kind": "trap", "front": "Why is <b>reward design</b> considered the hard part of applied RL?", "back": "<p>The agent maximises precisely what you wrote down, <b>including loopholes you did not notice</b>. This is called <b>specification gaming</b>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>a boat-racing agent rewarded for power-ups span in circles forever, never finishing — and outscored humans</li><li>robots rewarded for “distance travelled” learn to fall over in the right direction</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The behaviour you get is a consequence of the reward function, not of your intentions.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-07-state-action-value-example.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The agent maximises exactly what you wrote down — including the loopholes you didn't spot. This has a name and a long history of funny, expensive failures.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">reward function</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the rules you wrote saying what's worth points</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">specification gaming</td><td class=\"sa\">“spess-if-i-KAY-shun”</td><td>finding a loophole in the rules rather than doing what you meant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">reward shaping</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>designing rewards to encourage the behaviour you want</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">proxy</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>an easy-to-measure stand-in for what you actually care about</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A boat-racing AI rewarded for collecting power-ups learned to spin in circles forever, never finishing the race — and outscored every human.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-state-of-rl", "kind": "concept", "front": "Honest assessment: where does RL actually work, and where does it not?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>✓ works</b><ul class='cbul'><li>games with perfect simulators</li><li>some control: data-centre cooling, robotics</li><li><b>RLHF</b> — how every modern chat model is tuned</li></ul></div><div><b>⚠ hard</b><ul class='cbul'><li>sim-to-real transfer</li><li>sample efficiency — millions of trials</li><li>extreme sensitivity to reward design and hyperparameters</li><li>far fewer production applications than supervised learning</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">The hype exceeds the deployed reality — and the ideas still matter, because RLHF is quietly the biggest deployed application of RL that exists.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-16-state-of-rl.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>An honest scorecard, because the hype around this exceeds what is actually deployed.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">simulator</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>a computer model of the world. Trials are free and instant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sim-to-real</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the gap between the simulator and actual reality. Usually painful</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sample efficiency</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>how many tries you need. RL needs millions</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">RLHF</td><td class=\"sa\">“are-ell-aitch-eff”</td><td>RL from Human Feedback — how chat models are tuned. Quietly the biggest real use of RL there is</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Superhuman in video games. Fragile and expensive on real robots. Worth knowing; rarely the first tool to reach for.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c3w3-drill-return", "kind": "drill", "front": "&gamma; = 0.5. A reward of 100 arrives 3 steps from now, with 0 reward along the way. Compute the return.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">Return = <var>R</var>&#8321; + &gamma;<var>R</var>&#8322; + &gamma;&sup2;<var>R</var>&#8323; = 0 + 0 + (0.5)&sup2;(100)</div><div class=\"ceq\">= 0.25 &times; 100 = <b>25</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Each extra step away halves the value again — this is exactly why the Mars rover chooses the closer, smaller reward once the discount has eaten enough of the far one.</div>", "lesson": "c3/w3-03-the-return.html", "extra": "", "course": "C3", "week": "c3w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "Reinforcement Learning", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Add up every future reward, but shrink each one the further away it is — a reward three steps from now is worth less than the same reward arriving right now.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">&gamma;</td><td class=\"sa\">“gamma”</td><td>the discount — how much patience the agent has, between 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">R₁, R₂, R₃</td><td class=\"sa\">“R one, R two, R three”</td><td>the reward received at each future step</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like preferring £100 today over £100 in three years — the further away a reward is, the less it counts right now.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-order", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why can a bag-of-words model never distinguish “the dog bit the man” from “the man bit the dog”?", "back": "<p>Because it receives the same input for both: identical words, identical counts. Order is not <em>lost detail</em> — it was never encoded.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>everything in C1–C3 treated an example as an unordered set of features</li><li>a sequence is not a set: <var>x</var> = (x&lt;1&gt;, …, x&lt;T&gt;)</li><li>T varies per example, unlike n — which forces padding and masking</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Three superscript styles now: (i) = which example, [l] = which layer, &lt;t&gt; = which position.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-01-why-order-matters.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Shuffle the words of a sentence and the meaning changes completely — but a model that only counts how often each word appears sees no difference at all. Order has to be put back in deliberately.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">x⟨t⟩</td><td class=\"sa\">“x at time t”</td><td>the t-th word of this sentence</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">T</td><td class=\"sa\">“capital T”</td><td>how many words this particular sentence has — different for every one</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">⟨ ⟩</td><td class=\"sa\">angle brackets</td><td>→ which POSITION (round = which example, square = which layer)</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a recipe: same ingredients, different order, and you get bread or a burnt mess.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-tokens", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why do models use <b>subword</b> tokens rather than words or characters?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>the two extremes</b><ul class='cbul'><li>whole words: vocabulary is unbounded and any unseen word is unrepresentable</li><li>characters: sequences become very long and each piece means almost nothing</li></ul></div><div><b>the compromise</b><ul class='cbul'><li>subword (BPE): ~30k–100k vocabulary</li><li>single characters stay in the vocabulary, so <b>nothing is ever unrepresentable</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">~0.75 words per token in English. It is also why models are bad at spelling — they never see letters, only chunks.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-02-tokens.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A model can only look words up in a fixed list it was built with. Whole words make the list impossibly long; single letters make it meaningless. So it uses chunks of words — a compromise.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">token</td><td class=\"sa\">“token”</td><td>one chunk of text — usually part of a word</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">vocabulary</td><td class=\"sa\">“vocab”</td><td>the fixed list of chunks the model knows. It never grows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">BPE</td><td class=\"sa\">“byte-pair encoding”</td><td>the method for choosing the chunks — a compression algorithm</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like a printer's tray of type: single letters AND common chunks like “ing”, so anything can be set.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-onehot", "kind": "trap", "front": "One-hot encoding worked for decision trees. What are its <b>two</b> failures for a 50,000-word vocabulary?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>size</b> — 50,000 numbers per word, and 25.6 M parameters into a 512-unit layer, almost all multiplying zeros</li><li><b>meaning</b> — every pair of distinct words has dot product exactly <b>0</b>, so “cat” is as similar to “dog” as to “bulldozer”</li></ul><p>The second is the fatal one: the encoding destroys the only signal downstream dot products could use.</p><div class=\"chint\">Using the word's index as a number is worse — it invents an ordering that does not exist.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-03-one-hot-and-why-it-fails.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Giving every word its own slot with a 1 in it wastes enormous space — but worse, it makes every word exactly as different from every other. “Cat” ends up as unrelated to “dog” as to “bulldozer”.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">one-hot</td><td class=\"sa\">“one hot”</td><td>a long list of 0s with a single 1 marking which item this is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">dot product</td><td class=\"sa\">“dot product”</td><td>the site's way of measuring similarity. Here it is always 0</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like hotel room numbers: they identify each guest and say nothing about who is family.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-embedding", "kind": "formula", "front": "What <b>is</b> an embedding, mechanically?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>E</var> ∈ ℝ<sup><var>V</var> × <var>d</var></sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; <var>e</var><sub><var>w</var></sub> = <var>E</var>[<var>w</var>]</div><ul class='cbul'><li>a matrix of <b>learned parameters</b>, one row per token</li><li>“embedding a token” = looking up its row</li><li>mathematically identical to multiplying the one-hot vector by E — the 1 selects one row and the zeros delete the rest</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">You built this in C3 W2: collaborative filtering learned a feature vector per movie with no genre labels. Same construction, words instead of films.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-04-embeddings.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Give every word a short list of numbers, stored in one big table. Look up the row to “embed” a word. The numbers start random and are learned by ordinary gradient descent, like any weight.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">E</td><td class=\"sa\">“the embedding matrix”</td><td>the table: one row per word</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">V</td><td class=\"sa\">“vocab size”</td><td>how many words — typically 30,000 to 100,000</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">d</td><td class=\"sa\">“the dimension”</td><td>how many numbers describe each word — often 512</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">ℝ</td><td class=\"sa\">“the real numbers”</td><td>just means “ordinary decimal numbers go here”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">∈</td><td class=\"sa\">is an element of</td><td>“is one of”. y ∈ {0, 1} means y is 0 or 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">You built this in Course 3 for movies. Words instead of films; identical machinery.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-cosine", "kind": "number", "front": "king = [2,1,0], queen = [3,1,0]. Compute the cosine similarity.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">dot = 2(3) + 1(1) + 0(0) = <b>7</b></div><div class=\"ceq\">‖king‖ = √5 = 2.236 &nbsp;&nbsp; ‖queen‖ = √10 = 3.162</div><div class=\"ceq\">cos = 7 ÷ (2.236 × 3.162) = <b>0.990</b></div><div class=\"chint\">Against banana = [0,1,2] it is 0.200. Cosine divides out both lengths so only direction counts — magnitude tracks training frequency, which is not what you want to compare meanings on.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-04-embeddings.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>To ask how similar two words are, multiply their number-lists together pairwise and add up — then divide by both lengths so only the DIRECTION counts, not how big the numbers happen to be.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">cos</td><td class=\"sa\">“cosine”</td><td>a similarity score from −1 to 1. Near 1 = very alike</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">‖x‖</td><td class=\"sa\">“the norm of x”, or “length”</td><td>how long the arrow is: √(sum of squares)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">·</td><td class=\"sa\">“dot”</td><td>multiply matching entries, then add them all up</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Dividing by the lengths matters because long vectors just mean “this word was common in training”.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-rnn", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "What does an RNN do, in one formula and one sentence?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>h</var>&lt;t&gt; = <var>g</var>(<var>W<sub>h</sub>h</var>&lt;t−1&gt; + <var>W<sub>x</sub>x</var>&lt;t&gt; + <var>b</var>)</div><p>Read one item at a time, carrying a running summary. Update the summary from the old summary plus the new item.</p><div class=\"chint\">The weights are <b>shared across positions</b> — that is why one fixed-size model handles any sequence length.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-05-rnn-idea.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Read the sentence one word at a time, keeping a running summary in your head. Each new word updates the summary. At the end, the summary is your understanding.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">h⟨t⟩</td><td class=\"sa\">“h at time t”</td><td>the running summary after t words. Also called the hidden state</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">h⟨t−1⟩</td><td class=\"sa\">“h at t minus one”</td><td>the summary BEFORE this word — this is the loop</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">g</td><td class=\"sa\">“the activation function”</td><td>the squashing step, same as every neuron in Course 2</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Like taking a phone message with no paper: fine for short ones, hopeless for long ones.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-rnn-fails", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "The <b>two</b> reasons RNNs were replaced — and which one an LSTM fixes.", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>problem 1</b><b>Forgetting</b><ul class='cbul'><li>slopes multiply once per timestep</li><li>at 0.25/step, 20 steps back = 9.1 × 10⁻¹³</li><li>LSTM <b>does</b> mitigate this — gates let information pass unmultiplied</li></ul></div><div><b>problem 2</b><b>Serial dependency</b><ul class='cbul'><li>step t needs step t−1</li><li>cannot use parallel hardware at all</li><li>LSTM does <b>nothing</b> for this — it is architectural</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">Same vanishing-gradient arithmetic as deep sigmoid nets in C2 W2 — one factor per timestep instead of per layer, and sequences are far longer than nets are deep.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-06-why-rnns-failed.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two separate problems, both fatal. It forgets things from the start of long text. And it has to process words strictly one after another, so it cannot use fast parallel hardware.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">vanishing gradient</td><td class=\"sa\">“the signal fades”</td><td>many numbers below 1 multiplied together → almost zero</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">LSTM</td><td class=\"sa\">“L-S-T-M”</td><td>a smarter RNN. Fixes forgetting somewhat; fixes the speed problem not at all</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">serial</td><td class=\"sa\">“one after another”</td><td>step 41 cannot start until step 40 finishes</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A whispered message down a line of a hundred people: it gets garbled, AND it takes a hundred turns.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w1-attention-2014", "kind": "concept", "front": "What did attention (2014) fix, and how?", "back": "<p>The <b>bottleneck</b>: translation models compressed a whole sentence into one fixed vector before writing a single output word.</p><div class=\"ceq\"><var>c</var> = Σ<sub><var>t</var></sub> <var>α</var><sub><var>t</var></sub> <var>h</var>&lt;t&gt;</div><ul class='cbul'><li>keep <b>every</b> position's summary, not just the last</li><li>weights α come from a softmax, so they are positive and sum to 1</li><li>recomputed fresh for <b>every output word</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Softmax rather than picking the best because argmax has no gradient — the choosing itself has to be trainable.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w1-07-the-bottleneck.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w1", "weekNum": 1, "weekTitle": "Sequences, Embeddings and the Old Answers", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Instead of squeezing a whole sentence into one summary, keep the summary from every position and let the model choose which ones to look at — a different choice for every word it writes.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">c</td><td class=\"sa\">“the context vector”</td><td>what the model actually reads — a blend of every position</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">α</td><td class=\"sa\">“alpha”</td><td>how much to take from each position. They all add up to 1</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum over t”</td><td>add up across every position</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A translator with the page in front of them, glancing back — rather than one working from memory.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-formula", "kind": "formula", "front": "The attention formula — write it out.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">Attention(<var>Q</var>, <var>K</var>, <var>V</var>) = softmax(<span class='fr'><span><var>QK</var><sup>T</sup></span><span>√<var>d<sub>k</sub></var></span></span>) <var>V</var></div><p>Read it right to left: <b>score every pair, turn the scores into weights summing to 1, take a weighted average.</b></p><div class=\"chint\">Both halves are things you already knew — the dot product from F0 W1 and softmax from C2 W2. Nothing mathematically new is introduced.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-01-the-idea.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Score how relevant every word is to every other word, turn those scores into proportions that add to 1, then take a weighted average. That is the whole of attention.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">Q, K, V</td><td class=\"sa\">“queries, keys, values”</td><td>three versions of the same input — see the next card</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">QKᵀ</td><td class=\"sa\">“Q K transpose”</td><td>every query matched against every key. The T just flips it to line the shapes up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√dₖ</td><td class=\"sa\">“root d k”</td><td>shrinks the scores so they do not get huge. Card 5 explains why</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">softmax</td><td class=\"sa\">“soft max”</td><td>turns any scores into positive numbers adding to exactly 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Read it right to left and it is one sentence: score, weight, average.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-qkv", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "Query, key and value — what is each one, and where do they come from?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>Q</b> — what am I looking for?</li><li><b>K</b> — what do I advertise about myself?</li><li><b>V</b> — what do I hand over if chosen?</li></ul><div class=\"ceq\"><var>Q</var> = <var>XW<sub>Q</sub></var> &nbsp; <var>K</var> = <var>XW<sub>K</sub></var> &nbsp; <var>V</var> = <var>XW<sub>V</sub></var></div><p>Three <b>learned projections of the same input</b>. Key and value are separate on purpose: what makes a word easy to find is not what makes it useful to read.</p><div class=\"chint\">A library: your request (Q), the catalogue card (K), the book (V).</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-02-query-key-value.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Every word produces three different versions of itself: what it is looking for, what it advertises to others, and what it actually hands over if chosen. Three learned tables, one input.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">W_Q, W_K, W_V</td><td class=\"sa\">“W-Q, W-K, W-V”</td><td>three learned matrices, one per role</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">X</td><td class=\"sa\">“the input”</td><td>the sentence, already turned into numbers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">projection</td><td class=\"sa\">“projection”</td><td>just means “multiplied by a matrix to get a new version”</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A library: your request, the catalogue card, and the book itself. Three different things.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-shapes", "kind": "trap", "front": "For T positions and query size d<sub>k</sub>, what shape is QK<sup>T</sup> — and what shape does attention output?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><var>Q</var>, <var>K</var> are (T, d<sub>k</sub>)</li><li><var>QK</var><sup>T</sup> is <b>(T, T)</b> — d<sub>k</sub> is the inner dimension and vanishes</li><li>output is (T, d<sub>v</sub>) — <b>one vector per input position</b></li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The output having the same number of rows as the input is what lets attention layers <b>stack</b>. A layer that changed the length could not be repeated.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-02-query-key-value.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Shapes are how you check attention code is right. T positions in, T positions out — and the score grid is always T by T, one number for every pair.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">(T, dₖ)</td><td class=\"sa\">“T by d-k”</td><td>T rows (one per word), each dₖ numbers long</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">(T, T)</td><td class=\"sa\">“T by T”</td><td>the score grid: every word against every word</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Same number of rows in as out is what lets you stack these layers twelve deep.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-byhand", "kind": "number", "front": "Q = K = [[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]], d<sub>k</sub> = 2. What are the attention weights in row 1, and what must they sum to?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">scores: [1, 0, 1] &nbsp;→&nbsp; ÷√2: [0.7071, 0, 0.7071]</div><div class=\"ceq\">softmax: <b>[0.4011, 0.1978, 0.4011]</b> &nbsp;&nbsp; sum = <b>1.0000</b></div><p>e<sup>0.7071</sup> = 2.028, e<sup>0</sup> = 1, total 5.056. 2.028 ÷ 5.056 = 0.4011.</p><div class=\"chint\">Rows sum to 1; columns do not. Softmaxing the wrong axis runs without error and produces meaningless output — the most common bug here.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-03-attention-by-hand.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Work it through with tiny numbers once and it stops being mysterious. Score the pairs, shrink them, softmax each row, and check the row adds to 1.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">0.4011</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>e^0.7071 ÷ (sum of all three exponentials) = 2.028 ÷ 5.056</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">row sums to 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the check that tells you you did it right</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√</td><td class=\"sa\">square root</td><td>the number that, times itself, gives this one. √9 = 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">If your rows do not add to 1, you softmaxed the wrong direction — and nothing will error.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-scale", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why divide the scores by √d<sub>k</sub>?", "back": "<p>Because a dot product of d<sub>k</sub> unit-variance components has standard deviation <b>√d<sub>k</sub></b>. Bigger vectors give bigger scores for no meaningful reason.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>measured at d<sub>k</sub> = 512: spread 22.591, and √512 = 22.627</li><li>oversized scores <b>saturate</b> the softmax into a near one-hot</li><li>a saturated softmax has ~zero gradient, so the layer stops learning</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Same instinct as feature scaling in C1 W2 — keep the numbers entering a nonlinearity in the range where it still responds.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-05-why-scale.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Longer number-lists give bigger dot products just because you added up more terms. Big scores make the softmax pick one winner absolutely, and once it does that there is nothing left to learn from.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">dₖ</td><td class=\"sa\">“d k”</td><td>how long each query and key is — often 512</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">√</td><td class=\"sa\">“square root”</td><td>the amount the spread grows by, so dividing by it cancels it out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">saturate</td><td class=\"sa\">“saturate”</td><td>the softmax goes to 1.0000 and 0.0000 — no gradient left</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Same instinct as feature scaling in Course 1: keep numbers in the range where things still respond.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-multihead", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does multi-head attention buy, and what does it cost?", "back": "<p>Several attentions in parallel, each with its own W<sub>Q</sub>, W<sub>K</sub>, W<sub>V</sub>, concatenated and mixed by a final W<sub>O</sub>.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>a word relates to a sentence in several ways at once; one head computes one weighted average and so can chase only one</li><li>cost: <b>nothing</b> — 8 heads of size 64 is the same parameter count as 1 head of size 512</li><li>W<sub>O</sub> matters: without it the heads' outputs never interact</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Honest caveat: many heads can be pruned after training with little loss.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-06-multi-head.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One attention can chase one kind of relationship. Run eight side by side, each with its own tables, and glue the answers together — for the same total number of parameters.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">head</td><td class=\"sa\">“a head”</td><td>one complete attention, with its own three matrices</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">concat</td><td class=\"sa\">“concatenate”</td><td>lay the outputs side by side</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">W_O</td><td class=\"sa\">“W-O”</td><td>a final matrix that lets the heads' answers actually mix together</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Three specialists surveying one building — you want all three reports, not one averaged opinion.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-mask", "kind": "trap", "front": "Why is the causal mask −∞ rather than 0?", "back": "<p>Because masking happens <b>before</b> the softmax.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>score 0 → e<sup>0</sup> = 1 → an ordinary, unremarkable weight</li><li>score −∞ → e<sup>−∞</sup> = 0 → genuinely removed</li></ul><p>In code it is <code>-1e9</code>, because arithmetic with true infinity produces NaN in some paths.</p><div class=\"chint\">This one triangle is the whole architectural difference between GPT (masked, can generate) and BERT (unmasked, better at understanding).</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-07-masking.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A model learning to predict the next word must not be able to see it. Setting future scores to minus infinity before the softmax makes their weight exactly zero.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">−∞</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus infinity”</td><td>e to the power of it is 0, so the position vanishes completely</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">causal</td><td class=\"sa\">“causal”</td><td>can depend on the past and present, never the future</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">-1e9</td><td class=\"sa\">“minus one e nine”</td><td>what code actually uses — real infinity causes NaN errors</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">An exam where the invigilator physically covers the rest of the page.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w2-cost", "kind": "number", "front": "Context goes from 2,000 to 8,000 tokens. By what factor does attention work grow — and why did the transformer win anyway?", "back": "<p>4× the length → <b>16×</b> the pairwise comparisons. O(T²).</p><p>It won because wall-clock time is operation count <em>divided by how many you can do at once</em>. An RNN's T steps are strictly serial; attention's T² operations are independent.</p><div class=\"chint\">It traded a quantity hardware could absorb (parallel work) for one it could not (serial dependency).</div>", "lesson": "c4/w2-08-the-cost.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w2", "weekNum": 2, "weekTitle": "Attention", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Comparing every word with every other means the work grows with the SQUARE of the length. Double the text and you quadruple the work.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">O(T²)</td><td class=\"sa\">“order T squared”</td><td>work grows as length × length</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">O(T)</td><td class=\"sa\">“order T”</td><td>work grows in step with length — what an RNN costs</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Handshakes at a party: 10 people is 45, but 20 people is 190 — not double, over four times.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-posenc", "kind": "concept", "front": "Why does a transformer need positional encoding at all?", "back": "<p>Because attention is a weighted <b>average</b>, and an average is order-blind. Shuffle the inputs and the outputs shuffle identically — nothing else changes.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>a distinctive pattern is <b>added</b> to each embedding before attention runs</li><li>sinusoids at geometrically-spaced frequencies — fast dims separate neighbours, slow dims separate distant positions</li><li>every position's encoding has the same magnitude, since sin² + cos² = 1</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Context-window extension is largely a positional-encoding problem — RoPE and ALiBi are schemes for exactly this.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-01-positional-encoding.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Averaging does not care what order things came in, so attention is blind to word order. The fix is to stamp a distinctive pattern onto each word saying where it sat, before attention runs.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">PE</td><td class=\"sa\">“positional encoding”</td><td>the pattern added to each word based on its position</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">sin, cos</td><td class=\"sa\">“sine and cosine”</td><td>waves. Several at different speeds, read together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">pos</td><td class=\"sa\">“position”</td><td>which slot in the sentence — 0, 1, 2…</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Numbering the pages before you drop the manuscript.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-residual", "kind": "formula", "front": "Write the residual connection, differentiate it, and say why the result matters.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>y</var> = <var>x</var> + Sublayer(<var>x</var>)</div><div class=\"ceq\">∂<var>y</var>/∂<var>x</var> = <b>1</b> + ∂Sublayer/∂<var>x</var></div><p>The <b>1</b> is an identity path that no chain of multiplications can shrink.</p><ul class='cbul'><li>without: 12 layers at 0.7 each → 0.7¹² = <b>0.014</b> of the gradient</li><li>with: the identity route delivers 1.0 at any depth</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Third appearance of the same enemy: deep sigmoid nets (C2 W2), RNNs across time (C4 W1), and now depth. Residuals are the cleanest answer.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-02-residuals.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Instead of “output = layer(x)”, write “output = x + layer(x)”. The layer now learns what to CHANGE, and the gradient gets a route home that passes through no weights at all.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">∂y/∂x</td><td class=\"sa\">“dee y by dee x”</td><td>how much y changes when x is nudged — a derivative</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">the 1</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the shortcut. No chain of multiplications can shrink it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">residual</td><td class=\"sa\">“residual”</td><td>the leftover — what the layer adds on top of the input</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Tracked changes on a document, rather than retyping every page and risking a new typo each time.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-layernorm", "kind": "formula", "front": "Layer normalization — the formula, and what it averages over.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">LN(<var>x</var>) = <var>γ</var> · <span class='fr'><span><var>x</var> − <var>μ</var></span><span><var>σ</var></span></span> + <var>β</var></div><ul class='cbul'><li>μ and σ are computed across <b>one position's d features</b> — not across the batch</li><li>γ and β are learned, so the layer can undo the normalisation if that helps</li><li>x = [2,8,4,6] → mean 5, sd 2.236 → [−1.342, 1.342, −0.447, 0.447]</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">It is C1 W2's z-score applied inside the network. Not batch norm — which would make an example's output depend on which others share its batch.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-03-layer-norm.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Take one word's numbers, subtract their average, divide by their spread. Now they are centred and sensibly sized for the next layer — the same z-score you did in Course 1.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">μ</td><td class=\"sa\">“mew”</td><td>the average of this one word's numbers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sigma”</td><td>the spread — how far they typically sit from that average</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">γ, β</td><td class=\"sa\">“gamma and beta”</td><td>learned dials so the layer can undo this if it prefers</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Retuning the instrument between movements, not only before the concert.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-ffn", "kind": "trap", "front": "Which holds more parameters — attention or the feed-forward layer? And why must the feed-forward layer exist at all?", "back": "<p><b>Feed-forward, by two to one.</b> Attention is 4d²; feed-forward is 2 × d × 4d = 8d².</p><p>It must exist because attention is a weighted <b>average</b> — a linear operation. Stacking linear operations collapses to one, exactly the C2 W2 argument. The feed-forward layer supplies the non-linearity.</p><div class=\"chint\">It is also the only place NO information moves between positions. Attention is the only step where positions interact.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-04-feed-forward.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>After attention has mixed information between words, each word goes through a small two-layer network on its own. It exists because averaging is linear, and stacking linear things gets nothing.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">FFN</td><td class=\"sa\">“feed-forward network”</td><td>the two-layer network from Course 2, unchanged</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">d_ff</td><td class=\"sa\">“d f f”</td><td>the wide middle — usually 4 times the normal width</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">position-wise</td><td class=\"sa\">“position-wise”</td><td>applied to each word separately. No mixing happens here</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Attention is the meeting; this is everyone going away to write their own report.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-block", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "Write out one transformer block.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">x = x + attention(layer_norm(x))</div><div class=\"ceq\">x = x + feed_forward(layer_norm(x))</div><ul class='cbul'><li>two sublayers, each wrapped identically</li><li>two residual connections, two layer norms</li><li>output shape = input shape, which is what lets blocks stack</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">This is <b>pre-norm</b>, the modern arrangement. The 2017 paper used post-norm, which needs a carefully tuned warm-up to train stably at depth.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-05-the-block.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Two steps, each wrapped the same way: normalise, do the thing, add the input back. Do that with attention, then with the small network. That is one block — and a transformer is this repeated.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">sublayer</td><td class=\"sa\">“sublayer”</td><td>one of the two steps inside a block</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">pre-norm</td><td class=\"sa\">“pre-norm”</td><td>normalise BEFORE the step. The modern arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A production-line station whose output is the same shape as its input, so you can put fifty in a row.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-gptbert", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "GPT versus BERT — what single component differs?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>masked</b><b>GPT — decoder-only</b><ul class='cbul'><li>causal mask</li><li>predicts the next token</li><li><b>can generate</b></li><li>writing, chat, completion</li></ul></div><div><b>bidirectional</b><b>BERT — encoder-only</b><ul class='cbul'><li>no mask</li><li>fills in hidden tokens</li><li>cannot generate left to right</li><li>classification, search, extraction</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">The block itself is identical. Decoder-only dominates public attention because generation is visible, not because it wins everywhere.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-07-gpt-vs-bert.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>One triangle of blocked-out scores is the whole difference between the two famous families. Block the future and you can generate text; leave it open and you understand better but cannot write.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">decoder-only</td><td class=\"sa\">“decoder only”</td><td>GPT-style: masked, generates</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">encoder-only</td><td class=\"sa\">“encoder only”</td><td>BERT-style: unmasked, understands</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">bidirectional</td><td class=\"sa\">“both directions”</td><td>can see words before AND after</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Writing a letter versus proofreading one. Both skilled; neither can do the other's job.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w3-count", "kind": "number", "front": "GPT-2 small: 12 layers, d = 768, V = 50,257, context 1,024. Where do the parameters live?", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li>token embeddings: 50,257 × 768 = <b>38.6 M</b> (31.6%)</li><li>attention, all layers: 4d² × 12 = 28.3 M (22.8%)</li><li><b>feed-forward, all layers: 8d² × 12 = 56.6 M (45.5%)</b></li><li>total ≈ <b>124.4 M</b>, against a published 124 M</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">The feed-forward layers — the plainest component — are the largest share. The attention everyone talks about is under a quarter.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w3-08-counting-a-real-model.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w3", "weekNum": 3, "weekTitle": "The Transformer Block", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Every number on a model's spec sheet is something you can add up yourself. Do it once and model cards stop being marketing and start being information.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">124 M</td><td class=\"sa\">“124 million”</td><td>how many numbers this model stores and learns</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">d</td><td class=\"sa\">“the width”</td><td>how many numbers represent each word inside the model</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">V × d</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>the embedding table: one row of d numbers per vocabulary item</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">≈</td><td class=\"sa\">approximately equals</td><td>close enough — usually because the number was rounded</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">The plain feed-forward layers are the biggest part. The famous attention is under a quarter.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-objective", "kind": "formula", "front": "The pretraining objective — and why it produces more than autocomplete.", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\"><var>L</var> = −Σ<sub><var>t</var></sub> log <var>P</var>(<var>x<sub>t</sub></var> | <var>x</var><sub>&lt;<var>t</var></sub>)</div><p>Cross-entropy from C2 W2, over a 50,257-way softmax, at every position.</p><p>It produces knowledge because <b>predicting well requires it</b>: finishing “the capital of France is ___” with high probability means having stored the fact, so gradient descent stores it.</p><div class=\"chint\">Every position contributes a loss term simultaneously — a 1,000-token document is 1,000 training signals in one forward pass.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-01-next-token.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Show it text, hide the next word, ask it to guess, correct it. A few hundred billion times. That is all pretraining is — and facts get stored because storing them is the only way to guess well.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">P(x_t | x_<t)</td><td class=\"sa\">“probability of x-t given everything before”</td><td>how likely this word is, given the text so far</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">−log</td><td class=\"sa\">“negative log”</td><td>the loss from Course 1 and 2. Confidently wrong costs enormously</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">Σ</td><td class=\"sa\">“sum over t”</td><td>add up the loss at every position in the text</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">An apprentice who spent ten years finishing their master's sentences, and absorbed the trade doing it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-perplexity", "kind": "number", "front": "A model assigns 0.10 to the correct token. What are the loss and the perplexity, and what does perplexity mean?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">loss = −log(0.10) = <b>2.303</b> &nbsp;&nbsp; perplexity = e<sup>2.303</sup> = <b>10.0</b></div><p>Perplexity reads as <b>how many options the model is effectively choosing between</b>. 10 means it is about as uncertain as picking uniformly from 10 tokens.</p><div class=\"chint\">A uniform guess over 50,257 tokens has perplexity 50,257 and loss 10.825.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-01-next-token.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Perplexity turns the loss into something you can picture: roughly how many options the model is torn between. Lower is better; 1 would be perfect certainty.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">perplexity</td><td class=\"sa\">“perplexity”</td><td>e raised to the loss</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">e</td><td class=\"sa\">“e”</td><td>about 2.718 — the same constant from Foundations</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Perplexity 10 means it is about as unsure as if it were picking at random from ten words.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-temperature", "kind": "concept", "front": "What does temperature do, and does it change the model?", "back": "<div class=\"ceq\">p = softmax(logits / <var>T</var>)</div><ul class='cbul'><li>T &lt; 1 sharpens; T &gt; 1 flattens; T → 0 becomes argmax</li><li>applied to the <b>logits</b>, before the softmax — so it rescales probability <em>ratios</em></li><li><b>not a single weight changes</b> — it sits entirely outside the model</li></ul><p>Always taking the most likely token gives flat, repetitive text that loops, which is why real systems sample.</p><div class=\"chint\">Generating 1,000 tokens is 1,000 <b>sequential</b> forward passes — the parallelism that made training fast is gone.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-02-generation.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The model gives probabilities; something else has to actually pick a word. Temperature is the dial controlling how adventurous that picking is — and it changes nothing inside the model.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">T</td><td class=\"sa\">“temperature”</td><td>divides the scores before softmaxing. Low = safe, high = wild</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">logits</td><td class=\"sa\">“logits”</td><td>the raw scores, before they are turned into probabilities</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">argmax</td><td class=\"sa\">“arg max”</td><td>always taking the single highest. Sounds right, produces flat repetitive text</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A musician who only ever plays the most expected note. Every note defensible, the solo dead.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-rlhf", "kind": "algorithm", "front": "The three training stages that turn a text predictor into an assistant.", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li><b>pretraining</b> — hundreds of billions of tokens; where essentially all capability comes from</li><li><b>supervised fine-tuning</b> — tens of thousands of demonstrations; teaches the <em>format</em> of a helpful answer</li><li><b>RLHF</b> — humans rank competing answers, a reward model learns those rankings, and the policy is optimised against it</li></ol><p>Stage 1 gives capability; stages 2 and 3 give <b>behaviour</b>, and are cheap by comparison.</p><div class=\"chint\">This is C3 W3's reinforcement learning, and it is the largest deployed application of RL that exists. Caveat: RLHF optimises what people <em>rate highly</em>, which is not the same as true — hence sycophancy.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-04-rlhf.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>A freshly trained model just continues text — ask it a question and it might reply with more questions. Turning it into an assistant takes two more, much cheaper, training stages.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">SFT</td><td class=\"sa\">“supervised fine-tuning”</td><td>showing it written examples of good answers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">RLHF</td><td class=\"sa\">“R-L-H-F”</td><td>reinforcement learning from human feedback</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">reward model</td><td class=\"sa\">“reward model”</td><td>a stand-in for a human, trained on which answer people preferred</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Someone who has read every cookbook but never cooked for a guest. What they need is feedback, not more reading.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-context", "kind": "distinguish", "front": "A long context costs you two different things. What are they?", "back": "<div class=\"ctwo\"><div><b>attention</b><b>Compute — O(T²)</b><ul class='cbul'><li>every pair of positions scored</li><li>4k → 32k is 64× the work</li></ul></div><div><b>the cache</b><b>Memory — O(T)</b><ul class='cbul'><li>the KV cache</li><li>GPT-2 small: 38 MB at 1k, <b>4.7 GB</b> at 128k</li><li>usually the binding constraint on serving</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"chint\">The cache exists so generation does not recompute every previous token's keys and values each step — without it generation would be cubic in length.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-05-context-and-cost.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>The context window is the model's entire working memory — anything outside it simply does not exist. Making it bigger costs you in two different ways at once.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">context window</td><td class=\"sa\">“the window”</td><td>how many tokens it can attend to at once</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">KV cache</td><td class=\"sa\">“K-V cache”</td><td>saved keys and values so generation does not redo old work</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">O(T²) vs O(T)</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>compute grows with the square; memory grows in step with length</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A desk you can spread papers on. Bigger desk, more documents — and papers off the edge are gone.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-limits", "kind": "trap", "front": "Name three things a language model cannot reliably do — and the mechanism behind each.", "back": "<ul class='cbul'><li><b>hallucination</b> — the objective rewards <em>likely</em> continuations; nothing ever distinguished true from plausible</li><li><b>saying “I don't know”</b> — the softmax always yields a confident distribution and there is no abstain token; it has to be trained in during RLHF</li><li><b>counting letters</b> — the model receives subword chunks, never letters</li></ul><div class=\"chint\">Scale reduces several of these and removes none. Hallucination follows from the objective, not from capacity.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-06-what-it-cannot-do.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Every well-known failure of these systems follows from something you now understand. Being able to predict where one will fail is as much a mark of understanding as knowing what it can do.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">hallucination</td><td class=\"sa\">“hallucination”</td><td>confident, fluent, false. The objective rewarded likely, not true</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">calibration</td><td class=\"sa\">“calibration”</td><td>whether stated confidence matches actual accuracy</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">A brilliant improviser who was never told they are allowed to say “I don't know”.</p></div>"}, {"id": "c4w4-throughline", "kind": "concept", "front": "What do all four courses share, and what did Course 4 actually change?", "back": "<ol class='csteps'><li>a <b>model</b> — what f is allowed to be</li><li>a <b>cost</b> — one number for how wrong it is</li><li>an <b>optimiser</b> — gradient descent, usually Adam</li></ol><p>Course 4 changed <b>only the model</b>. The cost is still cross-entropy from C2 W2 and the optimiser is still Adam from C2 W2.</p><div class=\"chint\">Attention itself is the F0 W1 dot product plus the C2 W2 softmax. The genuinely new idea is letting every position read every other in one step.</div>", "lesson": "c4/w4-07-where-to-go-next.html", "extra": "", "course": "C4", "week": "c4w4", "weekNum": 4, "weekTitle": "From Transformer to Language Model", "plain": "<div class=\"cplain\"><span class=\"cplain-tag\">In plain English</span><p>Four courses, one shape. Choose what the model may look like, define one number for how wrong it is, and roll downhill. Course 4 only changed the first of those three.</p><table class=\"cdec\"><thead><tr><th>symbol</th><th>say it</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"sy\">model</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>what f is allowed to be</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">cost</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>one number saying how wrong it is</td></tr><tr><td class=\"sy\">optimiser</td><td class=\"sa\">—</td><td>gradient descent, usually Adam</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"calso\">Attention itself is the Foundations dot product plus the Course 2 softmax. The new idea is what they point at.</p></div>"}];
